export const B1 = [
  {"id":"b1-01","level":"B1","icon":"📦","title":"Moving house","grammar":"Sub-clauses: toen, terwijl, voordat, nadat, hoewel, zodat","summary":"After toen, terwijl, voordat, nadat, hoewel, zodat and the other subordinating conjunctions every verb goes to the end. Toen: once in the past; als/wanneer: now, later or repeatedly. Sub-clause first: the main clause starts with its verb.","explain":"A sub-clause (de bijzin) cannot stand on its own: it hangs on a main clause and starts with a conjunction. You know `omdat`, `dat` and `als` from unit A2 · 23. This unit adds the others you need to say when something happened, why, and in spite of what. The rule is the same for all of them: **every verb goes to the end** of the sub-clause.\n\n### The conjunctions\n\n| conjunction | meaning |\n| toen | when (once, in the past) |\n| als / wanneer | when, whenever; if |\n| terwijl | while; whereas |\n| voordat / nadat | before / after |\n| totdat / zodra | until / as soon as |\n| sinds | since (time) |\n| omdat / doordat | because |\n| zodat | so that |\n| hoewel | although |\n| tenzij | unless |\n\n> We pakken de dozen in terwijl de kinderen slapen. — We pack the boxes while the children are asleep.\n> Hoewel de huur hoog is, willen we hier blijven. — Although the rent is high, we want to stay here.\n\n### Toen or als?\n\nEnglish *when* has three Dutch words, and the choice depends on time:\n\n- `toen`: **one** moment or **one** period in the past, with the past tense: `toen we in Delft woonden`.\n- `als`: the present and the future, and something that happened **again and again** in the past (*whenever*).\n- `wanneer`: the same as `als`, a little more formal.\n\n> Toen we de woning bezichtigden, was het er koud. — When we viewed the flat, it was cold there.\n> Als de cv-ketel kapot is, bel je de huisbaas. — When the boiler is broken, you call the landlord.\n> Als het hard regende, lekte het dak. — Whenever it rained hard, the roof leaked.\n\n`Als` also means *if*; the context tells you which. Do not confuse the conjunction `toen` with the adverb `toen` (*then*): after the adverb the verb comes second, `Toen belde ik de huisbaas` (Then I called the landlord).\n\n### Words with a catch\n\n- `terwijl` means *while* (at the same time) and also *whereas*: `Ik betaal meer, terwijl mijn kamer kleiner is.`\n- `omdat` gives a reason or a cause and is always correct. `doordat` gives only a cause, something that simply happened, never a person's reason or intention.\n- `zodat` gives the result or the purpose: *so that*.\n- `sinds` with the present tense means something is still going on: `sinds we hier wonen` (since we have lived here).\n- `nadat`: in a story about the past it is mostly followed by `had` or `was` + participle (unit B1 · 9). Here we use it about now and later, with the perfect tense.\n- `totdat` may be shortened to `tot`, also in writing; `voordat` to `voor` in speech.\n\n### The verbs at the end\n\n| | the end of the sub-clause |\n| one verb | … dat ik **verhuis** |\n| perfect | … dat ik **ben verhuisd** / **verhuisd ben** |\n| modal | … dat ik **moet verhuizen** |\n| separable | … dat hij me **opbelt** |\n\n- With the perfect, both orders are correct and both are common.\n- A modal verb normally comes **before** its infinitive. You will also hear `verhuizen moet`; that is correct, but less usual.\n- A separable verb is **one word** at the end: `opbelt`, `inpakte`, `opzegt`. It splits around `te` (`op te bellen`, B1 · 17) and may split around a modal (`op moet bellen`).\n\n> Je krijgt de sleutel zodra je het contract hebt ondertekend. — You get the key as soon as you have signed the contract.\n> We bellen de makelaar voordat we de huur opzeggen. — We will call the estate agent before we give notice.\n\n### Sub-clause first\n\nThe sub-clause can come first. Then the whole sub-clause is the first element of the sentence, so the main clause starts with **its verb**, followed by the subject. A comma separates the two clauses. When the sub-clause comes second, the comma may be left out.\n\n> Toen we verhuisden, regende het de hele dag. — When we moved, it rained all day.\n> Zodra de makelaar belt, gaan we kijken. — As soon as the estate agent calls, we will go and have a look.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The verb stays in the middle: *hoewel het huis is klein → `hoewel het huis klein is`.\n- The subject first after a sub-clause: *Toen we aankwamen, de huisbaas wachtte al → `Toen we aankwamen, wachtte de huisbaas al`.\n- toen for now or later: *Toen ik tijd heb, bel ik → `Als ik tijd heb, bel ik`.\n- als for one event in the past: *Als we in Delft aankwamen, … → `Toen we in Delft aankwamen, …`.\n- A split separable verb: *dat hij me belt op → `dat hij me opbelt`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian `коли` covers both `toen` and `als`; in Dutch you choose by the time: once in the past is `toen`. And after `коли`, `хоча` or `тому що` Ukrainian keeps the normal order, while Dutch sends every verb to the end.","words":[{"nl":"huurder","art":"de","pl":"huurders","en":"tenant","pos":"n"},{"nl":"huisbaas","art":"de","pl":"huisbazen","en":"landlord","pos":"n"},{"nl":"huurcontract","art":"het","pl":"huurcontracten","en":"tenancy agreement / lease","pos":"n"},{"nl":"huurprijs","art":"de","pl":"huurprijzen","en":"rent (the amount)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"servicekosten","art":"de","pl":"","en":"service charges","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"opzegtermijn","art":"de","pl":"opzegtermijnen","en":"notice period","pos":"n"},{"nl":"makelaar","art":"de","pl":"makelaars","en":"estate agent","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezichtiging","art":"de","pl":"bezichtigingen","en":"viewing (of a house)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verhuisdoos","art":"de","pl":"verhuisdozen","en":"moving box","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lekkage","art":"de","pl":"lekkages","en":"leak","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schimmel","art":"de","pl":"schimmels","en":"mould","pos":"n"},{"nl":"cv-ketel","art":"de","pl":"cv-ketels","en":"boiler (central heating)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ondertekenen","past":"ondertekende","perf":"heeft ondertekend","en":"to sign","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opzeggen","past":"zei op","perf":"heeft opgezegd","en":"to give notice / to cancel (a contract)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"inpakken","past":"pakte in","perf":"heeft ingepakt","en":"to pack","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"gemeubileerd","en":"furnished","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Toen we de verhuisdozen inpakten, vonden we oude foto's.","en":"When we were packing the moving boxes, we found old photos.","hint":["toen","verhuisdoos","inpakken","vinden","oud","foto"],"note":"One period in the past, so toen with the past tense. The separable verb inpakten stands at the end as one word, and the main clause starts with its verb: vonden we.","alt":["We vonden oude foto's toen we de verhuisdozen inpakten."]},{"nl":"Als de cv-ketel kapot is, moet de huisbaas hem repareren.","en":"If the boiler is broken, the landlord has to repair it.","hint":["als","cv-ketel","kapot","moeten","huisbaas","repareren"],"note":"Als for a condition in the present. Is goes to the end of the sub-clause; then the main clause begins with its verb moet, and repareren closes the sentence.","alt":["Wanneer de cv-ketel kapot is, moet de huisbaas hem repareren.","Als de cv-ketel kapot is, moet de huisbaas die repareren.","Wanneer de cv-ketel kapot is, moet de huisbaas die repareren.","De huisbaas moet de cv-ketel repareren als hij kapot is.","De huisbaas moet de cv-ketel repareren als die kapot is."]},{"nl":"De buren betalen minder huur, terwijl hun woning groter is.","en":"The neighbours pay less rent, whereas their home is bigger.","hint":["buren","betalen","huur","terwijl","woning","groot"],"note":"Here terwijl does not mean 'while' in time but 'whereas': it sets two facts against each other. The verb is stands at the end of the sub-clause."},{"nl":"Hoewel de woning gemeubileerd is, nemen we onze eigen bank mee.","en":"Although the flat is furnished, we are bringing our own sofa.","hint":["hoewel","woning","gemeubileerd","meenemen","eigen","bank"],"note":"Hoewel sends is to the end. The sub-clause comes first, so the main clause starts with the verb nemen, then we; the separable verb is split in the main clause as usual.","alt":["We nemen onze eigen bank mee, hoewel de woning gemeubileerd is."]},{"nl":"Ik krijg de sleutel nadat ik het huurcontract heb ondertekend.","en":"I get the key after I have signed the tenancy agreement.","hint":["krijgen","sleutel","nadat","huurcontract","ondertekenen"],"note":"Two verbs at the end of the sub-clause: heb ondertekend or ondertekend heb, both are correct. Nadat with the perfect, because the signing comes first.","alt":["Ik krijg de sleutel nadat ik het huurcontract ondertekend heb.","Nadat ik het huurcontract heb ondertekend, krijg ik de sleutel.","Nadat ik het huurcontract ondertekend heb, krijg ik de sleutel."]},{"nl":"Er zit schimmel in de badkamer, doordat het dak al maanden lekt.","en":"There is mould in the bathroom because the roof has been leaking for months.","hint":["schimmel","badkamer","doordat","dak","maand","lekken"],"note":"Doordat gives a cause: nobody wanted the roof to leak. Omdat is also correct here. Lekt goes to the end; al + present tense says it is still going on.","alt":["Er zit schimmel in de badkamer omdat het dak al maanden lekt.","Doordat het dak al maanden lekt, zit er schimmel in de badkamer.","Omdat het dak al maanden lekt, zit er schimmel in de badkamer."]},{"nl":"We zeggen vandaag de huur op, zodat we op 1 juni kunnen verhuizen.","en":"We are giving notice today, so that we can move on 1 June.","hint":["opzeggen","vandaag","huur","zodat","kunnen","verhuizen"],"note":"Zodat gives the purpose. The modal kunnen normally stands before its infinitive at the end: kunnen verhuizen (verhuizen kunnen is possible but less usual).","alt":["We zeggen de huur vandaag op, zodat we op 1 juni kunnen verhuizen.","We zeggen vandaag de huur op, zodat we op 1 juni verhuizen kunnen.","We zeggen de huur vandaag op, zodat we op 1 juni verhuizen kunnen."]},{"nl":"Zodra de huisbaas terugbelt, stuur ik je een bericht.","en":"As soon as the landlord calls back, I will send you a message.","hint":["zodra","huisbaas","terugbellen","sturen","bericht"],"note":"The separable verb terugbellen stays in one piece at the end of the sub-clause: terugbelt. The main clause then begins with stuur, and ik follows.","alt":["Zodra de huisbaas terugbelt, stuur ik jou een bericht.","Ik stuur je een bericht zodra de huisbaas terugbelt.","Ik stuur jou een bericht zodra de huisbaas terugbelt."]},{"nl":"Sinds we hier wonen, hebben we geen problemen met de huisbaas gehad.","en":"Since we have lived here, we have not had any problems with the landlord.","hint":["sinds","hier","wonen","probleem","huisbaas","hebben"],"note":"Sinds + present tense: we still live here, so wonen, not a perfect as in English. The sub-clause is the first element, so hebben comes next and then we.","alt":["We hebben geen problemen met de huisbaas gehad sinds we hier wonen.","Sinds we hier wonen, hebben we met de huisbaas geen problemen gehad.","We hebben met de huisbaas geen problemen gehad sinds we hier wonen."]},{"nl":"Voordat de nieuwe huurder komt, moeten we de woning schoonmaken.","en":"Before the new tenant comes, we have to clean the flat.","hint":["voordat","huurder","komen","moeten","woning","schoonmaken"],"note":"Komt goes to the end of the sub-clause. The main clause starts with its verb moeten, and the infinitive schoonmaken goes to the end.","alt":["We moeten de woning schoonmaken voordat de nieuwe huurder komt."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ we in Rotterdam woonden, hadden we een kleine tuin.","options":["Als","Toen","Wanneer"],"correct":1,"en":"When we lived in Rotterdam, we had a small garden.","note":"One period in the past, so toen. Als and wanneer are for the present, the future, or something that happened again and again."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We nemen de woning, hoewel ___.","options":["de servicekosten zijn hoog","zijn de servicekosten hoog","de servicekosten hoog zijn"],"correct":2,"en":"We are taking the flat, although the service charges are high.","note":"Hoewel starts a sub-clause, so the verb zijn goes to the very end. The order of a main clause is wrong here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ de cv-ketel kapot is, bellen we de huisbaas.","options":["Toen","Hoewel","Als"],"correct":2,"en":"When the boiler is broken, we call the landlord.","note":"This is about now and every time it happens, in the present tense, so als. Toen is only for the past, and hoewel (although) makes no sense here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb de dozen al ingepakt, ___ we morgen vroeg kunnen beginnen.","options":["doordat","zodat","hoewel"],"correct":1,"en":"I have already packed the boxes, so that we can start early tomorrow.","note":"Zodat gives the result or the purpose of packing early. Doordat would make the early start the cause of the packing, which is the wrong way round."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Toen we bij de bezichtiging aankwamen, ___ al voor de deur.","options":["de makelaar stond","stond de makelaar","de makelaar staat"],"correct":1,"en":"When we arrived at the viewing, the estate agent was already standing at the door.","note":"The sub-clause is the first element, so the main clause begins with its verb: stond de makelaar. Staat is the present and does not fit a story in the past."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Als het hard ___ (regenen), lekte het dak altijd.","answers":["regende"],"en":"Whenever it rained hard, the roof always leaked.","note":"It happened again and again in the past, so als with the past tense, not toen. Regende goes to the end of the sub-clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We blijven in deze woning ___ de opzegtermijn voorbij is.","answers":["totdat","tot"],"en":"We are staying in this flat until the notice period is over.","note":"Until is totdat, often shortened to tot. It is a conjunction, so the verb is goes to the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik bel je zodra de verhuizers ___ (aankomen).","answers":["aankomen"],"en":"I will call you as soon as the movers arrive.","note":"At the end of a sub-clause a separable verb is one word: aankomen, never komen … aan."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Sinds we in deze woning ___ (wonen), hebben we last van schimmel.","answers":["wonen"],"en":"Since we have been living in this flat, we have had problems with mould.","note":"We still live there, so sinds takes the present tense: wonen. English uses a perfect here, Dutch does not."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zodra","words":["ik","het huurcontract","heb","ondertekend","krijg","ik","de sleutel"],"answers":["Zodra ik het huurcontract heb ondertekend, krijg ik de sleutel.","Zodra ik het huurcontract ondertekend heb, krijg ik de sleutel."],"en":"As soon as I have signed the tenancy agreement, I get the key.","note":"Both verbs of the perfect go to the end of the sub-clause, in either order. Then the main clause starts with its verb krijg, and ik follows."},{"type":"order","lead":"We","words":["pakken","de dozen","in","terwijl","de kinderen","slapen"],"answers":["We pakken de dozen in terwijl de kinderen slapen."],"en":"We pack the boxes while the children are asleep.","note":"In the main clause the separable verb is split: pakken … in. After terwijl the verb slapen goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put the sub-clause first.","from":"De makelaar belde toen we net thuis waren.","answers":["Toen we net thuis waren, belde de makelaar."],"en":"When we had just got home, the estate agent called.","note":"The sub-clause takes the first place, so the main clause must begin with its verb: belde de makelaar."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences, starting with Hoewel.","from":"De huurprijs is hoog. We willen de woning graag huren.","answers":["Hoewel de huurprijs hoog is, willen we de woning graag huren.","Hoewel de huurprijs hoog is, willen we graag de woning huren."],"en":"Although the rent is high, we would like to rent the flat.","note":"In the sub-clause is moves to the end. The main clause then starts with its verb willen, followed by we."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with zodat.","from":"We ondertekenen vandaag het huurcontract. We kunnen volgende week verhuizen.","answers":["We ondertekenen vandaag het huurcontract, zodat we volgende week kunnen verhuizen.","We ondertekenen vandaag het huurcontract, zodat we volgende week verhuizen kunnen."],"en":"We are signing the tenancy agreement today, so that we can move next week.","note":"After zodat both verbs go to the end. The usual order is the modal first: kunnen verhuizen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Toen ik morgen tijd heb, bel ik de makelaar.","answers":["Als ik morgen tijd heb, bel ik de makelaar.","Wanneer ik morgen tijd heb, bel ik de makelaar.","Zodra ik morgen tijd heb, bel ik de makelaar."],"en":"When I have time tomorrow, I will call the estate agent.","note":"Toen is only for one moment in the past. For the present and the future, when is als or wanneer."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Doordat er een lekkage was, de keuken stond onder water.","answers":["Doordat er een lekkage was, stond de keuken onder water."],"en":"Because there was a leak, the kitchen was under water.","note":"The sub-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with its verb: stond de keuken, not de keuken stond."}]},
  {"id":"b1-02","level":"B1","icon":"🏛️","title":"At the town hall","grammar":"Indirect speech and indirect questions","summary":"A reported statement starts with dat, a reported yes/no question with of, a reported question-word question with the question word. All three are sub-clauses: the verb goes to the end. Never als for whether.","explain":"When you tell what someone said or asked, or ask a question politely, Dutch puts the words in a sub-clause. This is called indirect speech (de indirecte rede). At a counter it is the normal way to ask: `Weet u waar het loket is?` sounds friendlier than `Waar is het loket?`\n\n### Three kinds\n\n| what was said | joined with |\n| a statement | dat |\n| a yes/no question | of |\n| a question with a question word | the question word |\n\nAll three are sub-clauses, so **every verb goes to the end**, as after `omdat` and `toen` (unit B1 · 1).\n\n### A statement: dat\n\n> Hij zegt: 'Ik kom morgen.' — He says: 'I am coming tomorrow.'\n> Hij zegt dat hij morgen komt. — He says that he is coming tomorrow.\n\nEnglish can leave *that* out (*He says he is coming*). Dutch cannot: `dat` is needed.\n\n### A yes/no question: of\n\nA question without a question word becomes a sub-clause with `of` (*whether, if*).\n\n> Ze vraagt: 'Heb je een afspraak?' — She asks: 'Do you have an appointment?'\n> Ze vraagt of ik een afspraak heb. — She asks whether I have an appointment.\n> Ik weet niet of het loket nog open is. — I do not know if the counter is still open.\n\nEnglish *if* is `of` here, **never** `als`. `Als` means *if* only in a condition: `Als het loket open is, ga ik erheen`.\n\n### A question with a question word\n\nThe question word (`waar`, `wanneer`, `hoe`, `hoe laat`, `hoeveel`, `waarom`, `wie`, `wat`) stays and starts the sub-clause. The verb goes to the end, so the order is **not** the order of the question.\n\n> Waar is de ingang? — Where is the entrance?\n> Kunt u mij vertellen waar de ingang is? — Can you tell me where the entrance is?\n> Weet jij hoe laat het gemeentehuis opengaat? — Do you know what time the town hall opens?\n\nWhen `wie` or `wat` is the subject, the verb is normally singular: `Weet u wie hier de aanvragen behandelt?`\n\nPolite openings at a counter: `Weet u …`, `Kunt u mij vertellen …`, `Mag ik vragen …`, `Ik wil graag weten …`. The question mark depends on the main clause: `Weet u waar het is?` is a question, `Ik vraag me af waar het is.` is not.\n\n### What changes\n\n**Persons.** Seen from the speaker who reports, `ik` often becomes `hij` or `zij`, and `mijn` becomes `zijn` or `haar`.\n\n> Anna zegt: 'Mijn paspoort is verlopen.' — Anna says: 'My passport has expired.'\n> Anna zegt dat haar paspoort verlopen is. — Anna says that her passport has expired.\n\n**Tense.** After a main verb in the past (`zei`, `vroeg`), the present tense in the sub-clause usually becomes the simple past. When something is still true, or always true, the present may stay.\n\n> Hij zei dat hij ziek was. — He said that he was ill.\n> Ze vroeg hoeveel een uittreksel kost. — She asked how much an extract costs.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Question order in the sub-clause: *Ik weet niet waar is het loket → `Ik weet niet waar het loket is`.\n- `als` for whether: *Ik weet niet als hij komt → `Ik weet niet of hij komt`.\n- Leaving out dat: *Hij zegt hij komt morgen → `Hij zegt dat hij morgen komt`.\n- Forgetting the person: *Anna zegt dat mijn paspoort verlopen is → `Anna zegt dat haar paspoort verlopen is`.\n- English *what is your name* is usually `hoe` + `heten`: `Mag ik vragen hoe u heet?` (`wat uw naam is` is correct too).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`чи` is `of`: `Я не знаю, чи він прийде` is `Ik weet niet of hij komt`. Ukrainian keeps the tense of the original words (`Він сказав, що він хворий`), while Dutch usually moves it to the past: `Hij zei dat hij ziek was`.","words":[{"nl":"gemeentehuis","art":"het","pl":"gemeentehuizen","en":"town hall","pos":"n"},{"nl":"loket","art":"het","pl":"loketten","en":"counter / desk (at an office)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ambtenaar","art":"de","pl":"ambtenaren","en":"civil servant / official","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verblijfsvergunning","art":"de","pl":"verblijfsvergunningen","en":"residence permit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uittreksel","art":"het","pl":"uittreksels","en":"extract (from a register)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanvraag","art":"de","pl":"aanvragen","en":"application / request","pos":"n"},{"nl":"burgerservicenummer","art":"het","pl":"burgerservicenummers","en":"citizen service number (BSN)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"identiteitsbewijs","art":"het","pl":"identiteitsbewijzen","en":"proof of identity / ID","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pasfoto","art":"de","pl":"pasfoto's","en":"passport photo","pos":"n"},{"nl":"handtekening","art":"de","pl":"handtekeningen","en":"signature","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanvragen","past":"vroeg aan","perf":"heeft aangevraagd","en":"to apply for","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verlengen","past":"verlengde","perf":"heeft verlengd","en":"to renew / to extend","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verlopen","past":"verliep","perf":"is verlopen","en":"to expire","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afhalen","past":"haalde af","perf":"heeft afgehaald","en":"to collect / to pick up","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"geldig","en":"valid","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"verplicht","en":"compulsory / required","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De ambtenaar zegt dat ik mijn verblijfsvergunning op tijd moet verlengen.","en":"The official says that I must renew my residence permit in time.","hint":["ambtenaar","zeggen","verblijfsvergunning","op tijd","moeten","verlengen"],"note":"A reported statement starts with dat, and both verbs go to the end: moet verlengen (verlengen moet is also possible).","alt":["De ambtenaar zegt dat ik mijn verblijfsvergunning op tijd verlengen moet.","De ambtenaar zegt dat ik op tijd mijn verblijfsvergunning moet verlengen.","De ambtenaar zegt dat ik op tijd mijn verblijfsvergunning verlengen moet."]},{"nl":"Weet u waar het loket voor paspoorten is?","en":"Do you (formal) know where the desk for passports is?","hint":["weten","waar","loket","paspoort"],"note":"The question Waar is het loket? becomes a sub-clause after Weet u: waar stays at the front and is goes to the end."},{"nl":"Kunt u mij vertellen hoe lang de aanvraag duurt?","en":"Can you (formal) tell me how long the application takes?","hint":["kunnen","vertellen","hoe lang","aanvraag","duren"],"note":"Hoe lang starts the sub-clause and duurt goes to the end. This is the polite way to ask at a counter.","alt":["Kunt u me vertellen hoe lang de aanvraag duurt?"]},{"nl":"Ik weet niet of mijn identiteitsbewijs nog geldig is.","en":"I do not know whether my ID is still valid.","hint":["weten","of","identiteitsbewijs","nog","geldig"],"note":"A yes/no question (Is het nog geldig?) is reported with of, never als. The verb is moves to the end."},{"nl":"De vrouw achter het loket vraagt of ik mijn burgerservicenummer weet.","en":"The woman behind the counter asks if I know my citizen service number.","hint":["vrouw","loket","vragen","of","burgerservicenummer","weten"],"note":"Her question was Weet u uw burgerservicenummer? Reported by me, u becomes ik and uw becomes mijn; of starts the clause and weet goes to the end."},{"nl":"Mijn buurvrouw zegt dat haar paspoort volgende maand verloopt.","en":"My neighbour (a woman) says that her passport expires next month.","hint":["buurvrouw","zeggen","paspoort","volgende maand","verlopen"],"note":"She said: 'Mijn paspoort verloopt.' In the report mijn becomes haar, because she is the owner. Verloopt goes to the end."},{"nl":"Gisteren zei de ambtenaar dat mijn uittreksel nog niet klaar was.","en":"Yesterday the official said that my extract was not ready yet.","hint":["gisteren","zeggen","ambtenaar","uittreksel","klaar"],"note":"The main verb zei is in the past, so the present of the original words (Het is nog niet klaar) becomes the past: was.","alt":["De ambtenaar zei gisteren dat mijn uittreksel nog niet klaar was."]},{"nl":"Ik wil graag weten wanneer ik mijn paspoort kan afhalen.","en":"I would like to know when I can collect my passport.","hint":["willen","graag","weten","wanneer","paspoort","afhalen"],"note":"Wanneer starts the sub-clause, and the modal kan and the infinitive afhalen go to the end. You will also hear af kan halen; that is correct too.","alt":["Ik wil graag weten wanneer ik mijn paspoort af kan halen.","Ik wil graag weten wanneer ik mijn paspoort afhalen kan."]},{"nl":"Mijn man vroeg de ambtenaar hoeveel een nieuw paspoort kost.","en":"My husband asked the official how much a new passport costs.","hint":["man","vragen","ambtenaar","hoeveel","nieuw","paspoort"],"note":"After vroeg the tense usually moves to the past, but a price that is still the same may stay in the present: kost or kostte.","alt":["Mijn man vroeg de ambtenaar hoeveel een nieuw paspoort kostte.","Mijn man vroeg aan de ambtenaar hoeveel een nieuw paspoort kost.","Mijn man vroeg aan de ambtenaar hoeveel een nieuw paspoort kostte."]},{"nl":"Weet jij wie mijn aanvraag behandelt?","en":"Do you know who is dealing with my application?","hint":["weten","wie","aanvraag","behandelen"],"note":"Wie is the subject of the sub-clause, so the verb is singular, behandelt, and stands at the end.","alt":["Weet je wie mijn aanvraag behandelt?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik weet niet ___ het gemeentehuis op zaterdag open is.","options":["als","of","wat"],"correct":1,"en":"I do not know whether the town hall is open on Saturday.","note":"A reported yes/no question takes of. Als only means if in a condition, and wat asks for a thing."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Weet u waar ___?","options":["is het loket","het loket is","het loket zijn"],"correct":1,"en":"Do you (formal) know where the counter is?","note":"After Weet u the question becomes a sub-clause: the verb goes to the end. Is het loket is the order of a direct question."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Anna zegt: 'Mijn paspoort is verlopen.' Anna zegt dat ___ paspoort verlopen is.","options":["mijn","haar","zijn","jouw"],"correct":1,"en":"Anna says that her passport has expired.","note":"In the report you speak about Anna, so her mijn becomes haar. Mijn would mean that it is your own passport."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Toen ik belde, zei de ambtenaar dat hij het druk ___.","options":["heb","had","hadden"],"correct":1,"en":"When I called, the official said that he was busy.","note":"His words were Ik heb het druk. Reported, ik becomes hij, and because he said it in the past about that moment, the present becomes the past: had. Heb keeps his own ik-form; hadden is plural."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Kunt u mij vertellen hoe laat ___?","options":["begint mijn afspraak","mijn afspraak begint","mijn afspraak beginnen"],"correct":1,"en":"Can you (formal) tell me what time my appointment starts?","note":"Hoe laat starts the sub-clause, so the verb begint goes to the end. The subject mijn afspraak is singular."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze vraagt ___ ik een afspraak heb.","answers":["of"],"en":"She asks whether I have an appointment.","note":"Heeft u een afspraak? has no question word, so it is reported with of."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mag ik vragen ___ u heet?","answers":["hoe"],"en":"May I ask what your name is?","note":"Dutch asks for a name with hoe: Hoe heet u? So the indirect question also starts with hoe, not wat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De ambtenaar vraagt wanneer mijn verblijfsvergunning ___ (verlopen).","answers":["verloopt"],"en":"The official asks when my residence permit expires.","note":"The main verb vraagt is in the present, so the sub-clause stays in the present: verloopt, at the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Weet u wie hier de pasfoto's ___ (maken)?","answers":["maakt"],"en":"Do you (formal) know who takes the passport photos here?","note":"Wie is the subject of the sub-clause, and wie takes a singular verb: maakt, at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Weet","words":["u","of","een afspraak","verplicht","is"],"answers":["Weet u of een afspraak verplicht is?"],"en":"Do you (formal) know whether an appointment is compulsory?","note":"The main clause is a question, Weet u …? Then of starts the sub-clause and is goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["weet","niet","of","ik","hier","mijn handtekening","moet zetten"],"answers":["Ik weet niet of ik hier mijn handtekening moet zetten.","Ik weet niet of ik mijn handtekening hier moet zetten."],"en":"I do not know whether I have to sign here.","note":"After of the verbs moet zetten go to the end. Hier may stand before or after mijn handtekening."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it an indirect question, starting with Weet u.","from":"Waar is het gemeentehuis?","answers":["Weet u waar het gemeentehuis is?"],"en":"Do you (formal) know where the town hall is?","note":"Waar stays at the front of the sub-clause, and is moves to the end. The question mark stays, because Weet u …? is a question."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Report Mark's question, starting with Mark vraagt.","from":"Mark: 'Kan ik hier een uittreksel aanvragen?'","answers":["Mark vraagt of hij hier een uittreksel kan aanvragen.","Mark vraagt of hij hier een uittreksel aan kan vragen.","Mark vraagt of hij hier een uittreksel aanvragen kan."],"en":"Mark asks whether he can apply for an extract here.","note":"A yes/no question becomes of + sub-clause. Mark is now spoken about, so ik becomes hij, and kan aanvragen goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Report it, starting with Ze zei.","from":"Lisa: 'Ik sta in de rij bij het loket.'","answers":["Ze zei dat ze in de rij bij het loket stond.","Ze zei dat zij in de rij bij het loket stond."],"en":"She said that she was standing in the queue at the counter.","note":"Ik becomes ze, and after zei the present sta becomes the past stond, at the end of the sub-clause."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik weet niet als het gemeentehuis vandaag open is.","answers":["Ik weet niet of het gemeentehuis vandaag open is."],"en":"I do not know whether the town hall is open today.","note":"Whether is of. Als means if only in a condition (Als het open is, ga ik erheen)."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Kunt u mij vertellen waar is de ingang?","answers":["Kunt u mij vertellen waar de ingang is?","Kunt u me vertellen waar de ingang is?"],"en":"Can you (formal) tell me where the entrance is?","note":"After waar in an indirect question the verb goes to the end: waar de ingang is. Waar is de ingang is the order of a direct question."}]},
  {"id":"b1-03","level":"B1","icon":"🧑‍💼","title":"People at work","grammar":"Relative clauses: die and dat","summary":"A relative clause says more about a noun and has the verb at the end. die for de-words and all plurals, dat for het-words in the singular. It is never left out: de man die ik zag.","explain":"A relative clause (de betrekkelijke bijzin) tells you more about a noun: *the colleague **who sits next to me***, *the team **that I lead***. In Dutch it starts with `die` or `dat`, and like every sub-clause it has **all its verbs at the end**.\n\n> De collega die naast mij zit, komt uit Polen. — The colleague who sits next to me is from Poland.\n> Het team dat ik leid, is klein. — The team that I lead is small.\n\n### die or dat?\n\nThe choice depends only on the noun it refers to: its article and whether it is plural. It does not matter whether the noun is a person or a thing.\n\n| | singular | plural |\n| de-word | de afdeling die | de afdelingen die |\n| het-word | het team dat | de teams die |\n\n- `die` for every de-word and for **every plural**, also the plural of a het-word: `het team dat`, but `de teams die`.\n- `dat` only for a het-word in the singular. Watch the het-words for people: `het meisje dat`, `het personeel dat`.\n\n> Het meisje dat bij ons stage loopt, is pas achttien. — The girl who is doing an internship with us is only eighteen.\n> De stagiairs die we dit jaar hebben, zijn erg goed. — The interns we have this year are very good.\n\n### Subject or object: the same word\n\nEnglish has *who* and *whom*, Ukrainian has `який`, `якого`, `якому`. Dutch has no case here: `die` is the same whether it is the subject or the object of the relative clause.\n\n> de collega die mij helpt — the colleague who helps me\n> de collega die ik help — the colleague whom I help\n\nThe context decides, and a plural verb can show it. In `de stagiairs die mijn collega begeleidt`, the singular `begeleidt` belongs to `mijn collega`, so the colleague supervises the interns. With `begeleiden` the interns would supervise the colleague.\n\n### Never left out\n\nEnglish can drop *who* or *that*: *the intern **we** trained*. Dutch never can.\n\n> De stagiair die we hebben ingewerkt, doet het goed. — The intern we trained is doing well.\n\n### Where the clause goes\n\nThe relative clause comes **directly after its noun**. If the main clause then goes on, its verb follows the relative clause; a comma there is common in writing.\n\nThere is one exception: when only the final verb of the main clause (a participle or an infinitive) would follow the noun, the relative clause usually moves **behind** that verb. Both orders are correct, but the first is the normal one:\n\n> Ik heb een collega ontmoet die in Gent woont. — I met a colleague who lives in Ghent.\n> Ik heb een collega die in Gent woont ontmoet. — I met a colleague who lives in Ghent.\n\n### Commas\n\nA relative clause that tells you **which** person or thing you mean gets no comma in front: `de collega die naast mij zit`. A clause that only adds extra information about someone already known stands between two commas:\n\n> Onze leidinggevende, die erg collegiaal is, helpt ons vaak. — Our manager, who is very supportive, often helps us.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- dat with a de-word: *de afdeling dat → `de afdeling die`.\n- die with a het-word: *het team die → `het team dat`.\n- The plural of a het-word: *de teams dat → `de teams die`.\n- wie for *who*: *de collega wie hier werkt → `de collega die hier werkt`. (After a noun, `wie` comes only after a preposition: unit B1 · 4.)\n- wat for a het-word: you will hear *het werk wat ik doe, but standard Dutch is `het werk dat ik doe` (`wat` has its own uses, unit B1 · 4).\n- The verb in the middle: *de collega die werkt hier → `de collega die hier werkt`.\n- Leaving it out: *de vervanger ik heb gekozen → `de vervanger die ik heb gekozen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`який`, `яка`, `яке`, `які` change for gender, number and case. Dutch has only two forms, and only the noun decides: de-word or plural `die`, het-word `dat`. The case question (`якого`? `якому`?) does not exist.","words":[{"nl":"afdeling","art":"de","pl":"afdelingen","en":"department","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leidinggevende","art":"de","pl":"leidinggevenden","en":"manager / supervisor","pos":"n"},{"nl":"medewerker","art":"de","pl":"medewerkers","en":"employee / staff member","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stagiair","art":"de","pl":"stagiairs","en":"intern / trainee","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vervanger","art":"de","pl":"vervangers","en":"replacement / stand-in","pos":"n"},{"nl":"functie","art":"de","pl":"functies","en":"position / job (role)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verantwoordelijkheid","art":"de","pl":"verantwoordelijkheden","en":"responsibility","pos":"n"},{"nl":"personeel","art":"het","pl":"","en":"staff / personnel","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"werkplek","art":"de","pl":"werkplekken","en":"workplace / desk","pos":"n"},{"nl":"werkdruk","art":"de","pl":"","en":"pressure of work / workload","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"samenwerken","past":"werkte samen","perf":"heeft samengewerkt","en":"to work together / to cooperate","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"inwerken","past":"werkte in","perf":"heeft ingewerkt","en":"to train (a new colleague) / to show the ropes","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"begeleiden","past":"begeleidde","perf":"heeft begeleid","en":"to supervise / to guide","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overleggen","past":"overlegde","perf":"heeft overlegd","en":"to consult / to discuss","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verantwoordelijk","en":"responsible","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"collegiaal","en":"supportive (as a colleague) / helpful","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De collega die naast mij zit, werkt al tien jaar op deze afdeling.","en":"The colleague who sits next to me has worked in this department for ten years.","hint":["collega","naast","zitten","werken","jaar","afdeling"],"note":"Collega is a de-word, so die. The verb zit goes to the end of the relative clause, and then the main clause goes on with its verb werkt.","alt":["De collega die naast me zit, werkt al tien jaar op deze afdeling."]},{"nl":"Het team dat ik leid, bestaat uit vijf medewerkers.","en":"The team that I lead consists of five staff members.","hint":["team","leiden","bestaan uit","medewerker"],"note":"Team is a het-word in the singular, so dat. Dat is the object here (I lead the team), but the form is the same as for a subject."},{"nl":"De stagiair die we vorige week hebben ingewerkt, doet het heel goed.","en":"The intern we trained last week is doing very well.","hint":["stagiair","vorige week","inwerken","goed"],"note":"English can leave out who, Dutch cannot: die must be there. Both verbs go to the end: hebben ingewerkt or ingewerkt hebben.","alt":["De stagiair die we vorige week ingewerkt hebben, doet het heel goed."]},{"nl":"Mijn leidinggevende is iemand die altijd tijd voor je maakt.","en":"My manager is someone who always makes time for you.","hint":["leidinggevende","iemand","altijd","tijd","maken"],"note":"Iemand takes die, like a de-word. Maakt goes to the end of the relative clause.","alt":["Mijn leidinggevende is iemand die altijd tijd voor jou maakt."]},{"nl":"De medewerkers die op deze afdeling werken, overleggen elke maandag.","en":"The employees who work in this department consult each other every Monday.","hint":["medewerker","afdeling","werken","overleggen","maandag"],"note":"A plural always takes die. The plural verb werken at the end shows that die (the employees) is the subject.","alt":["De medewerkers die op deze afdeling werken, overleggen elke maandag met elkaar."]},{"nl":"Het overleg dat we elke week hebben, duurt vaak te lang.","en":"The meeting we have every week often goes on too long.","hint":["overleg","week","hebben","duren","lang"],"note":"Overleg is a het-word, so dat. Without the relative pronoun the sentence would be wrong, although English leaves it out."},{"nl":"Vandaag hebben we een vervanger gevonden die mijn taken kan overnemen.","en":"Today we found a replacement who can take over my tasks.","hint":["vandaag","vervanger","vinden","taak","kunnen","overnemen"],"note":"Only the participle gevonden would follow the noun, so the relative clause moves behind it. Inside it, kan overnemen stands at the end.","alt":["We hebben vandaag een vervanger gevonden die mijn taken kan overnemen.","Vandaag hebben we een vervanger gevonden die mijn taken over kan nemen.","We hebben vandaag een vervanger gevonden die mijn taken over kan nemen."]},{"nl":"Dit is de collega die mij heeft ingewerkt.","en":"This is the colleague who trained me.","hint":["collega","inwerken"],"note":"Here die is the subject: the colleague trained me. Compare de collega die ik heb ingewerkt, where die is the object. Same word, different meaning.","alt":["Dit is de collega die me heeft ingewerkt.","Dit is de collega die mij ingewerkt heeft.","Dit is de collega die me ingewerkt heeft."]},{"nl":"Onze leidinggevende, die erg collegiaal is, helpt ons als het druk is.","en":"Our manager, who is very supportive, helps us when it is busy.","hint":["leidinggevende","erg","collegiaal","helpen","druk"],"note":"We have only one manager, so the clause just adds information and stands between commas. Is goes to the end of it.","alt":["Onze leidinggevende, die erg collegiaal is, helpt ons wanneer het druk is."]},{"nl":"De teams die goed samenwerken, hebben minder last van werkdruk.","en":"The teams that work well together suffer less from pressure of work.","hint":["team","goed","samenwerken","last","werkdruk"],"note":"Het team, but in the plural de teams, so die. The separable verb samenwerken stays in one piece at the end of the clause."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Het werk ___ ik doe, geeft me veel verantwoordelijkheid.","options":["die","dat","wat"],"correct":1,"en":"The work I do gives me a lot of responsibility.","note":"Werk is a het-word in the singular, so dat. Het werk wat ik doe is often heard, but it is not standard Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De afdeling ___ dit project leidt, zit op de derde verdieping.","options":["dat","die","wie"],"correct":1,"en":"The department that is running this project is on the third floor.","note":"Afdeling is a de-word, so die. After a noun, wie is only used with a preposition (met wie)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De teams ___ aan dit project werken, overleggen elke week.","options":["dat","wie","die"],"correct":2,"en":"The teams that are working on this project consult each other every week.","note":"Het team is a het-word, but every plural takes die: de teams die."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ken de vrouw ___.","options":["die heeft deze functie","die deze functie heeft","dat deze functie heeft"],"correct":1,"en":"I know the woman who has this position.","note":"A relative clause is a sub-clause, so heeft goes to the end. Vrouw is a de-word, so die, not dat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit is het meisje ___ bij ons stage loopt.","answers":["dat"],"en":"This is the girl who is doing an internship with us.","note":"Meisje is a het-word, so dat, even though it is a person. The article decides, not the meaning."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De stagiairs die mijn collega ___ (begeleiden), zijn erg tevreden.","answers":["begeleidt"],"en":"The interns that my colleague supervises are very satisfied.","note":"My colleague is the subject, so the verb is singular: stem begeleid + t = begeleidt. Begeleiden would mean that the interns supervise the colleague."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het personeel ___ hier werkt, krijgt een eigen werkplek.","answers":["dat"],"en":"The staff who work here get their own desk.","note":"Personeel is a het-word and grammatically singular, so dat and a singular verb, werkt, even though it means a group of people."},{"type":"order","lead":"De collega","words":["die","mij","heeft ingewerkt","gaat","met pensioen"],"answers":["De collega die mij heeft ingewerkt, gaat met pensioen."],"en":"The colleague who trained me is retiring.","note":"The relative clause comes right after its noun and ends with its verbs. Then the main clause continues with its own verb gaat."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","gisteren","de vrouw","ontmoet","die","mijn functie","overneemt"],"answers":["Ik heb gisteren de vrouw ontmoet die mijn functie overneemt.","Ik heb de vrouw gisteren ontmoet die mijn functie overneemt.","Ik heb gisteren de vrouw die mijn functie overneemt ontmoet.","Ik heb de vrouw die mijn functie overneemt gisteren ontmoet."],"en":"Yesterday I met the woman who is taking over my position.","note":"The usual order puts the relative clause behind the participle ontmoet. Directly after de vrouw is also correct. Overneemt stays one word at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with die or dat.","from":"Dat is de stagiair. Hij begint morgen.","answers":["Dat is de stagiair die morgen begint."],"en":"That is the intern who starts tomorrow.","note":"Hij is replaced by die (stagiair is a de-word), and begint moves to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with die or dat.","from":"Ik heb een nieuwe werkplek. Ik deel hem met een collega.","answers":["Ik heb een nieuwe werkplek die ik met een collega deel.","Ik heb een nieuwe werkplek die ik deel met een collega."],"en":"I have a new desk that I share with a colleague.","note":"Werkplek is a de-word, so die; here it is the object of deel. The verb deel goes to the end of the relative clause."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the second sentence a relative clause after De medewerker.","from":"De medewerker heeft veel ervaring. Hij begeleidt de stagiairs.","answers":["De medewerker die de stagiairs begeleidt, heeft veel ervaring."],"en":"The staff member who supervises the interns has a lot of experience.","note":"The relative clause comes directly after de medewerker, with begeleidt at its end. Then the main clause continues with heeft."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het team die ik leid, is erg collegiaal.","answers":["Het team dat ik leid, is erg collegiaal."],"en":"The team that I lead is very supportive.","note":"Team is a het-word in the singular, so the relative pronoun is dat."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De vervanger ik heb gekozen, begint maandag.","answers":["De vervanger die ik heb gekozen, begint maandag.","De vervanger die ik gekozen heb, begint maandag."],"en":"The replacement I chose starts on Monday.","note":"English can leave out who or that, but Dutch always needs die or dat. Vervanger is a de-word: die."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik zoek de collega wie verantwoordelijk is voor het rooster.","answers":["Ik zoek de collega die verantwoordelijk is voor het rooster.","Ik zoek de collega die voor het rooster verantwoordelijk is."],"en":"I am looking for the colleague who is responsible for the schedule.","note":"Who in a relative clause is die (or dat), never wie. After a noun, wie is used only with a preposition."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De medewerkers die werken hier, zijn erg collegiaal.","answers":["De medewerkers die hier werken, zijn erg collegiaal."],"en":"The staff members who work here are very supportive.","note":"A relative clause is a sub-clause, so its verb goes to the end: die hier werken."}]},
  {"id":"b1-04","level":"B1","icon":"💻","title":"Things I use every day","grammar":"Relative clauses with prepositions; wat","summary":"With a preposition: for things waar + preposition (waarmee, waarover, also split: waar … mee), for people preposition + wie (met wie). wat after alles, iets, niets, het enige, a superlative, and for a whole sentence.","explain":"In unit B1 · 3 the relative clause started with `die` or `dat`. When the verb in the clause needs a **preposition** (`werken met`, `praten over`, `wachten op`), `die` and `dat` cannot be used. And after words like `alles` or `iets` the relative pronoun is `wat`. The verb still goes to the end.\n\n### Things: waar + preposition\n\nFor a thing, the preposition is joined to `waar`. It makes no difference whether the noun is a de-word, a het-word or a plural.\n\n| preposition | relative |\n| over, op, aan | waarover, waarop, waaraan |\n| in, van, voor | waarin, waarvan, waarvoor |\n| **met** | **waarmee** |\n| **tot** | **waartoe** |\n\n`met` becomes `mee` and `tot` becomes `toe`. Never *waarmet.\n\n> Dit is de laptop waarmee ik werk. — This is the laptop I work with.\n> Het programma waarover ik sprak, is gratis. — The program I talked about is free.\n\n`waar` and the preposition may also be **split**: `waar` starts the clause and the preposition goes just before the verbs at the end. Both forms are correct; the split one is very common in speech.\n\n> Dit is de laptop waar ik mee werk. — This is the laptop I work with.\n\n### People: preposition + wie\n\nFor a person, put the preposition in front of `wie`. `waarmee` and `waar … mee` are also used for people, above all in speech, and are not wrong; but `met wie` is the usual form, certainly in writing.\n\n> De collega met wie ik werk, helpt me met mijn computer. — The colleague I work with helps me with my computer.\n> De monteur aan wie ik mijn laptop gaf, kon hem niet repareren. — The repairman I gave my laptop to could not repair it.\n\nEnglish puts the preposition at the end (*the colleague I work **with***). Dutch puts it at the front: never *de collega die ik mee werk.\n\n### A place: waar\n\nFor a place, `waar` on its own means *where*: `de kamer waar de router staat`, `de stad waar ik woon`. `waarin` is also possible.\n\n### wat instead of dat\n\nUse `wat` after these words, which have no noun of their own:\n\n- `alles`, `iets`, `niets`, `veel`, `weinig`\n- `het enige`, `het eerste`, `het laatste`\n- a superlative used as a noun: `het beste`, `het mooiste`\n\n> Ik heb alles opgeslagen wat op het scherm stond. — I saved everything that was on the screen.\n> Het enige wat niet werkt, is de afstandsbediening. — The only thing that does not work is the remote control.\n\nAfter `alles`, `iets`, `niets`, `het enige`, `het eerste` and a superlative you will also read `dat`; that is accepted, but `wat` is more common. After a real noun the rule of B1 · 3 stays: `het wachtwoord dat`, never *het wachtwoord wat.\n\n`wat` also refers back to a **whole sentence**. Then a comma comes before it, and `dat` is not possible.\n\n> Mijn laptop crashte, wat ik erg vervelend vond. — My laptop crashed, which I found very annoying.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- die or dat with a preposition: *de app met die ik werk → `de app waarmee ik werk`.\n- met inside the word: *waarmet → `waarmee`; split: *waar ik met werk → `waar ik mee werk`.\n- the preposition at the end, as in English: *de vriend die ik mee praat → `de vriend met wie ik praat`.\n- dat for a whole sentence: *Hij kwam te laat, dat ik raar vond → `…, wat ik raar vond`.\n- wat after a noun: *het bestand wat ik zoek → `het bestand dat ik zoek`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian puts the preposition before `який` and changes its case: `ноутбук, з яким я працюю`. Dutch uses `met wie` only for people; for a thing it builds one word with `waar`: `de laptop waarmee ik werk`.","words":[{"nl":"apparaat","art":"het","pl":"apparaten","en":"device / appliance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wachtwoord","art":"het","pl":"wachtwoorden","en":"password","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toetsenbord","art":"het","pl":"toetsenborden","en":"keyboard","pos":"n"},{"nl":"scherm","art":"het","pl":"schermen","en":"screen","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bestand","art":"het","pl":"bestanden","en":"file (on a computer)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"instelling","art":"de","pl":"instellingen","en":"setting","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verbinding","art":"de","pl":"verbindingen","en":"connection","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afstandsbediening","art":"de","pl":"afstandsbedieningen","en":"remote control","pos":"n"},{"nl":"accu","art":"de","pl":"accu's","en":"battery (rechargeable)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"downloaden","past":"downloadde","perf":"heeft gedownload","en":"to download","pos":"v"},{"nl":"installeren","past":"installeerde","perf":"heeft geïnstalleerd","en":"to install","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opslaan","past":"sloeg op","perf":"heeft opgeslagen","en":"to save (a file) / to store","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"inloggen","past":"logde in","perf":"heeft ingelogd","en":"to log in","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verwijderen","past":"verwijderde","perf":"heeft verwijderd","en":"to delete / to remove","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opladen","past":"laadde op","perf":"heeft opgeladen","en":"to charge (a battery)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"draadloos","en":"wireless","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Dit is de laptop waarmee ik elke dag werk.","en":"This is the laptop I work with every day.","hint":["laptop","waarmee","elke dag","werken"],"note":"werken met a thing, so waar + met, and met becomes mee: waarmee. Werk goes to the end.","alt":["Dit is de laptop waar ik elke dag mee werk."]},{"nl":"De app waarover ik het heb, is gratis.","en":"The app I am talking about is free.","hint":["app","waarover","het hebben over","gratis"],"note":"het hebben over means to talk about. For a thing, over joins waar: waarover. It may also be split: waar ik het over heb.","alt":["De app waar ik het over heb, is gratis.","De app waarover ik praat, is gratis.","De app waar ik over praat, is gratis."]},{"nl":"De buurman met wie ik de router deel, is op vakantie.","en":"The neighbour I share the router with is on holiday.","hint":["buurman","met","wie","router","delen","vakantie"],"note":"For a person the preposition goes in front of wie: met wie. English puts with at the end; Dutch does that only in the split form waar … mee.","alt":["De buurman waarmee ik de router deel, is op vakantie.","De buurman waar ik de router mee deel, is op vakantie."]},{"nl":"Het wachtwoord waarmee ik inlog, is veel te kort.","en":"The password I log in with is far too short.","hint":["wachtwoord","waarmee","inloggen","te","kort"],"note":"inloggen met, so waarmee, also for a het-word. The separable verb inlog stays one word at the end.","alt":["Het wachtwoord waar ik mee inlog, is veel te kort."]},{"nl":"Ik heb alles opgeslagen wat op het scherm stond.","en":"I saved everything that was on the screen.","hint":["alles","opslaan","wat","scherm","staan"],"note":"After alles the relative pronoun is wat. The clause moves behind the participle opgeslagen, which is the usual order; directly after alles is also correct.","alt":["Ik heb alles wat op het scherm stond opgeslagen.","Ik heb alles opgeslagen dat op het scherm stond.","Ik heb alles dat op het scherm stond opgeslagen."]},{"nl":"Het enige wat niet werkt, is de afstandsbediening.","en":"The only thing that does not work is the remote control.","hint":["het enige","wat","werken","afstandsbediening"],"note":"Het enige has no noun of its own, so wat is the usual choice; dat is accepted too. Werkt goes to the end.","alt":["Het enige dat niet werkt, is de afstandsbediening."]},{"nl":"Mijn accu was elke middag leeg, wat erg vervelend was.","en":"My battery was flat every afternoon, which was very annoying.","hint":["accu","middag","leeg","wat","vervelend"],"note":"Wat refers back to the whole first sentence, not to one noun. Here only wat is possible, never dat."},{"nl":"Draadloze oortjes zijn het handigste wat ik dit jaar heb gekocht.","en":"Wireless earbuds are the most useful thing I have bought this year.","hint":["draadloos","oortje","handig","wat","jaar","kopen"],"note":"After a superlative used as a noun (het handigste) wat is usual; dat is accepted too. The verbs go to the end in either order.","alt":["Draadloze oortjes zijn het handigste wat ik dit jaar gekocht heb.","Draadloze oortjes zijn het handigste dat ik dit jaar heb gekocht.","Draadloze oortjes zijn het handigste dat ik dit jaar gekocht heb."]},{"nl":"Zet de router in de kamer waar je het meest werkt.","en":"Put the router in the room where you work most.","hint":["zetten","router","kamer","waar","meest","werken"],"note":"For a place, waar on its own means where. It is a relative clause like the others, so werkt goes to the end.","alt":["Zet de router in de kamer waarin je het meest werkt.","Zet de router in de kamer waar jij het meest werkt.","Zet de router in de kamer waar je het meeste werkt."]},{"nl":"De monteur aan wie ik mijn apparaat gaf, kon het niet repareren.","en":"The repairman I gave my device to could not repair it.","hint":["monteur","aan","wie","apparaat","geven","repareren"],"note":"iets aan iemand geven: a person, so aan wie, with the preposition in front. Gaf goes to the end of the clause.","alt":["De monteur aan wie ik mijn apparaat heb gegeven, kon het niet repareren.","De monteur aan wie ik mijn apparaat gegeven heb, kon het niet repareren.","De monteur waaraan ik mijn apparaat gaf, kon het niet repareren.","De monteur waar ik mijn apparaat aan gaf, kon het niet repareren."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Dit is de app ___ ik mijn foto's bewerk.","options":["die","waarmee","met die","met wat"],"correct":1,"en":"This is the app I edit my photos with.","note":"bewerken met a thing: waar + met = waarmee. After a preposition die and wat are not possible."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Alles ___ je downloadt, komt in deze map.","options":["die","wat","waar"],"correct":1,"en":"Everything you download goes into this folder.","note":"Alles has no noun of its own, so the relative pronoun is wat. Die is for a de-word or a plural."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De man ___ ik over de nieuwe router sprak, werkt bij de provider.","options":["met die","met wie","met wat"],"correct":1,"en":"The man I talked to about the new router works for the provider.","note":"spreken met a person: the preposition goes in front of wie. Die and wat cannot follow a preposition."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn laptop deed het niet meer, ___ ik erg vervelend vond.","options":["dat","die","wat"],"correct":2,"en":"My laptop stopped working, which I found very annoying.","note":"What I found annoying is the whole first sentence, not the laptop, so wat. Dat cannot refer to a whole sentence."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit is de kabel ___ ik mijn telefoon oplaad.","answers":["waarmee"],"en":"This is the cable I charge my phone with.","note":"Met a thing: waar + met, and met becomes mee. One word here, because the gap is before the subject."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij heeft al mijn bestanden verwijderd, ___ ik niet leuk vind.","answers":["wat"],"en":"He has deleted all my files, which I do not like.","note":"Wat refers to the whole first sentence: that he deleted them. Dat is not possible here."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Weet je het wachtwoord nog ___ je vorige week hebt gekozen?","answers":["dat"],"en":"Do you still remember the password you chose last week?","note":"Wachtwoord is a real het-noun and there is no preposition, so the rule of unit B1 · 3 applies: dat, not wat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het huis ___ we nu wonen, heeft een snelle verbinding.","answers":["waar","waarin"],"en":"The house where we live now has a fast connection.","note":"For a place, waar means where; waarin (in which) is also correct."},{"type":"order","lead":"Dit","words":["is","het toetsenbord","waar","ik","het liefst","mee","typ"],"answers":["Dit is het toetsenbord waar ik het liefst mee typ."],"en":"This is the keyboard I prefer to type with.","note":"Split form: waar starts the clause and mee stands just before the verb at the end. Met becomes mee here too."},{"type":"order","lead":"De vriend","words":["met wie","ik","games","speel","woont","in Canada"],"answers":["De vriend met wie ik games speel, woont in Canada."],"en":"The friend I play games with lives in Canada.","note":"For a person: met wie, at the start of the clause. Speel goes to the end of the clause, then the main clause continues with woont."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the second sentence a relative clause after De app.","from":"De app is erg handig. Ik betaal mijn rekeningen met de app.","answers":["De app waarmee ik mijn rekeningen betaal, is erg handig.","De app waar ik mijn rekeningen mee betaal, is erg handig."],"en":"The app I pay my bills with is very handy.","note":"met de app becomes waarmee, or split waar … mee. The verb betaal goes to the end of the clause."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with one word, waarover, instead of waar … over.","from":"Dat is de instelling waar ik je over vertelde.","answers":["Dat is de instelling waarover ik je vertelde."],"en":"That is the setting I told you about.","note":"The two forms mean the same. In the one-word form the preposition is fixed to waar and leaves its place before the verb."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with wat.","from":"Mijn telefoon laadt heel snel op. Dat vind ik erg handig.","answers":["Mijn telefoon laadt heel snel op, wat ik erg handig vind."],"en":"My phone charges very quickly, which I find very handy.","note":"Wat takes the place of dat and refers to the whole first sentence. Vind goes to the end."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dit is de laptop met die ik werk.","answers":["Dit is de laptop waarmee ik werk.","Dit is de laptop waar ik mee werk."],"en":"This is the laptop I work with.","note":"After a preposition die is impossible. For a thing use waar + preposition: waarmee."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb het programma geïnstalleerd waarmet je video's kunt maken.","answers":["Ik heb het programma geïnstalleerd waarmee je video's kunt maken.","Ik heb het programma geïnstalleerd waar je video's mee kunt maken."],"en":"I have installed the program you can make videos with.","note":"With waar, met always becomes mee: waarmee. The same goes for tot: waartoe."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dat is het enige die ik niet kan downloaden.","answers":["Dat is het enige wat ik niet kan downloaden.","Dat is het enige dat ik niet kan downloaden."],"en":"That is the only thing I cannot download.","note":"Het enige has no noun, so the usual pronoun is wat (dat is accepted too). Die is wrong: it is not a de-word."}]},
  {"id":"b1-05","level":"B1","icon":"💶","title":"Money matters","grammar":"Er with a preposition: erop, ervan, ermee","summary":"After a preposition, 'it' or 'them' meaning a thing becomes er + preposition: erop, ervan, ermee (met → mee). Er stays near the verb, the preposition goes to the end: Ik heb er lang op gewacht.","explain":"In English you can put *it* or *them* after any preposition: *I am waiting for it, I pay with it*. Dutch cannot do this when *it* is a thing. Instead of preposition + `het`, `hem` or `ze`, Dutch uses **er + preposition**, written as one word: `erop`, `ervan`, `ermee`. The Dutch name is *het voornaamwoordelijk bijwoord*. You need these words in almost every conversation about bills, money and plans.\n\n### Preposition + a thing becomes er + preposition\n\n| preposition | + it / them | example |\n| op | erop | Ik wacht erop. |\n| aan | eraan | Ik denk eraan. |\n| van | ervan | Wat vind je ervan? |\n| voor | ervoor | Ik spaar ervoor. |\n| over | erover | We praten erover. |\n| met | **ermee** | Ik betaal ermee. |\n\n> Ik wacht op de factuur. Ik wacht erop. — I am waiting for the invoice. I am waiting for it.\n> Ik denk aan de rekeningen. Ik denk eraan. — I am thinking about the bills. I am thinking about them.\n\nThe er-word is the same for one thing and for more things, and for de-words and het-words: `erop` is \"on it\" and \"on them\".\n\nTwo prepositions change their form: `met` becomes **mee** and `tot` becomes **toe**. So it is `ermee` and `ertoe`, never *ermet or *ertot. The same goes for `daarmee`, `hiermee` and `waarmee` below.\n\n### People keep the pronoun\n\nThe er-word is for things. For a person the normal pronoun stays after the preposition.\n\n> Ik wacht op mijn broer. Ik wacht op hem. — I am waiting for my brother. I am waiting for him.\n> Je kunt altijd op haar rekenen. — You can always count on her.\n\nSo in standard Dutch `Ik wacht erop` is about a thing, such as a bill, a letter or the bus (in informal speech `er` and `daar` are sometimes used for people too).\n\n### Stressed: daar and hier\n\nWhen you point at something or stress it, use `daar` (that) or `hier` (this) instead of `er`: `daarmee`, `daarvoor`, `hiermee`, `hierop`. A preposition + `dat` or `dit` follows the same rule: not *met dat, but `daarmee`. Only `daar` and `hier` can be put first for emphasis: `Daar wacht ik op`, never *Erop wacht ik.\n\n> Hiermee kun je overal betalen. — You can pay everywhere with this.\n> Daar heb ik geen geld voor. — I have no money for that.\n\nIn speech `daar` is very common, also without much stress.\n\n### Questions: waar\n\nA question word works the same way: standard Dutch uses `waar` + preposition, not `op wat` (heard in informal speech, mostly in Belgium). In speech the two parts are usually split, with the preposition at the end; joined is more written.\n\n> Waar wacht je op? — What are you waiting for?\n> Waarmee heb je betaald? — What did you pay with?\n\n### Splitting: er early, the preposition at the end\n\nAs soon as other words follow, the two parts usually split. `er` (or `daar`, `waar`) stays early, directly after the conjugated verb, or after the subject when that follows the verb (a short pronoun such as `het` comes before it). The preposition goes to the end of the sentence, before the infinitive or participle if there is one.\n\n| verb | er | middle | preposition + end |\n| Ik heb | er | lang | op gewacht. |\n| Ik denk | er | vaak | aan. |\n| Ik kan | er | niet | mee betalen. |\n\n> We hebben er een lening voor afgesloten. — We took out a loan for it.\n\n`niet`, time words and the object stand between the two parts. When nothing comes between them, they are one word again: `Ik heb erop gewacht`.\n\n### Pointing ahead to a clause\n\nThe er-word can also point forward to a `dat`-clause or an `om te`-clause (more on `om te` in unit B1 · 7). With verbs like `rekenen op` and `denken aan` you cannot leave it out.\n\n> Ik reken erop dat je de premie op tijd betaalt. — I am counting on you paying the premium on time.\n> We denken erover om een spaarrekening te openen. — We are thinking about opening a savings account.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No preposition + `het` for a thing: *Ik betaal met het → `Ik betaal ermee`.\n- `met` becomes `mee`: *ermet → `ermee`, also when split: `Ik kan er niet mee betalen`.\n- Not for people: *Ik wacht erop (your brother) → `Ik wacht op hem`.\n- No *op wat: → `Waar wacht je op?` or `Waarop wacht je?`\n- Split in the right places: *Ik heb erop lang gewacht → `Ik heb er lang op gewacht`.\n- Do not drop er before a clause: *Ik reken op dat je komt → `Ik reken erop dat je komt`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian uses the same pronoun for a thing and a person: `чекаю на нього` can be about a bus or about a friend. Dutch makes the difference: `op hem` for the friend, `erop` for the bus.","words":[{"nl":"factuur","art":"de","pl":"facturen","en":"invoice","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verzekering","art":"de","pl":"verzekeringen","en":"insurance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"premie","art":"de","pl":"premies","en":"premium (for insurance)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schuld","art":"de","pl":"schulden","en":"debt","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lening","art":"de","pl":"leningen","en":"loan","pos":"n"},{"nl":"spaarrekening","art":"de","pl":"spaarrekeningen","en":"savings account","pos":"n"},{"nl":"betaalpas","art":"de","pl":"betaalpassen","en":"debit card","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanmaning","art":"de","pl":"aanmaningen","en":"payment reminder","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toeslag","art":"de","pl":"toeslagen","en":"allowance (benefit from the state)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"overmaken","past":"maakte over","perf":"heeft overgemaakt","en":"to transfer (money)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"sparen","past":"spaarde","perf":"heeft gespaard","en":"to save (money)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"afsluiten","past":"sloot af","perf":"heeft afgesloten","en":"to take out (a loan, insurance)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"vergoeden","past":"vergoedde","perf":"heeft vergoed","en":"to reimburse / to cover (costs)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aflossen","past":"loste af","perf":"heeft afgelost","en":"to pay off (a debt)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"rekenen op","past":"rekende op","perf":"heeft gerekend op","en":"to count on","pos":"v"},{"nl":"maandelijks","en":"monthly","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik heb een nieuwe betaalpas, maar ik kan er nog niet mee betalen.","en":"I have a new debit card, but I cannot pay with it yet.","hint":["nieuw","betaalpas","kunnen","nog niet","betalen"],"note":"Met + it (the card) becomes er + mee. The two parts split: er directly after kan, mee at the end before the infinitive betalen.","alt":["Ik heb een nieuwe betaalpas, maar ik kan daar nog niet mee betalen.","Ik heb een nieuwe betaalpas, maar daar kan ik nog niet mee betalen."]},{"nl":"We hebben een spaarrekening voor onze dochter en we zetten er elke maand geld op.","en":"We have a savings account for our daughter and we put money into it every month.","hint":["spaarrekening","dochter","zetten","elke maand","geld"],"note":"Dutch puts money op a rekening, so 'into it' is erop. Er comes right after zetten; the time and the object stand in between, and op goes last.","alt":["We hebben een spaarrekening voor onze dochter en we zetten daar elke maand geld op.","We hebben een spaarrekening voor onze dochter en elke maand zetten we er geld op.","We hebben een spaarrekening voor onze dochter en daar zetten we elke maand geld op."]},{"nl":"Ik reken erop dat je de lening dit jaar aflost.","en":"I am counting on you paying off the loan this year.","hint":["rekenen op","lening","dit jaar","aflossen"],"note":"Erop points forward to the dat-clause. With rekenen op it cannot be left out: never *Ik reken op dat. In the sub-clause aflost stays in one piece at the end.","alt":["Ik reken erop dat jij de lening dit jaar aflost.","Ik reken erop dat je dit jaar de lening aflost.","Ik reken erop dat jij dit jaar de lening aflost."]},{"nl":"Waar heb je dat geld voor nodig?","en":"What do you need that money for?","hint":["waar","hebben","geld","voor","nodig"],"note":"Not *voor wat: a question about a thing uses waar + preposition. In speech waar opens the question and voor moves to the end, before nodig.","alt":["Waarvoor heb je dat geld nodig?","Waar heb jij dat geld voor nodig?","Waarvoor heb jij dat geld nodig?"]},{"nl":"Mijn verzekering vergoedt de tandarts en daar ben ik heel blij mee.","en":"My insurance covers the dentist and I am very happy about that.","hint":["verzekering","vergoeden","tandarts","blij"],"note":"Blij met + that becomes daar + mee. Daar points at the whole fact and stands first after en; mee goes to the end.","alt":["Mijn verzekering vergoedt de tandarts en ik ben daar heel blij mee.","Mijn verzekering vergoedt de tandarts en ik ben er heel blij mee."]},{"nl":"Mijn man regelt de verzekeringen; ik kan altijd op hem rekenen.","en":"My husband arranges the insurance policies; I can always count on him.","hint":["man","regelen","verzekering","altijd","rekenen op"],"note":"Hem is a person, so the pronoun stays after the preposition: op hem. Erop would mean 'on it', a thing.","alt":["Mijn man regelt de verzekeringen; op hem kan ik altijd rekenen."]},{"nl":"Hiermee kun je in de hele wereld betalen.","en":"You can pay all over the world with this. (a card in your hand)","hint":["hiermee","kunnen","wereld","betalen"],"note":"With this becomes hier + mee, never *met dit. Hiermee is stressed, so it can open the sentence; er could not.","alt":["Je kunt hiermee in de hele wereld betalen.","Hier kun je in de hele wereld mee betalen.","Je kunt hier in de hele wereld mee betalen."]},{"nl":"Waar heb je de factuur mee betaald?","en":"What did you pay the invoice with?","hint":["waar","factuur","met","betalen"],"note":"Waar + met becomes waar … mee: waar first, mee at the end before the participle. Joined, waarmee, is also correct.","alt":["Waarmee heb je de factuur betaald?","Waar heb jij de factuur mee betaald?","Waarmee heb jij de factuur betaald?"]},{"nl":"We denken erover om onze schulden sneller af te lossen.","en":"We are thinking about paying off our debts faster.","hint":["denken over","schuld","snel","aflossen"],"note":"Erover points forward to the om te-clause. Te goes inside the separable verb: af te lossen.","alt":["We denken erover na om onze schulden sneller af te lossen."]},{"nl":"De nieuwe keuken was duur, dus we hebben er een lening voor afgesloten.","en":"The new kitchen was expensive, so we took out a loan for it.","hint":["nieuw","keuken","duur","lening","afsluiten"],"note":"Voor + it becomes er + voor. Er stays right after hebben, the object een lening stands between, and voor comes just before the participle.","alt":["De nieuwe keuken was duur, dus we hebben daar een lening voor afgesloten.","De nieuwe keuken was duur, dus daar hebben we een lening voor afgesloten."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb de toeslag nog niet, maar ik wacht ___.","options":["op het","erop","op hem","eraan"],"correct":1,"en":"I do not have the allowance yet, but I am waiting for it.","note":"De toeslag is a thing, so op + it becomes erop. Op het and op hem are not used for things, and wachten goes with op, not aan."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Meneer De Vries van de bank komt om tien uur. We wachten ___.","options":["erop","op hem","op het"],"correct":1,"en":"Mr De Vries from the bank is coming at ten. We are waiting for him.","note":"Meneer De Vries is a person, and for people the pronoun stays: op hem. Erop is only for things."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Dit is de nieuwe app van de bank. Je kunt er ook rekeningen ___ betalen.","options":["met","mee","ermee"],"correct":1,"en":"This is the bank's new app. You can also pay bills with it.","note":"Er is already there after kunt, so only the preposition is missing. Combined with er, met always becomes mee, also when the two parts are split."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ heb je dat geld overgemaakt?","options":["Voor wat","Waarvoor","Ervoor"],"correct":1,"en":"What did you transfer that money for?","note":"A question about a thing uses waar + preposition: waarvoor. Voor wat is informal and mostly Belgian, not standard Netherlands Dutch, and ervoor is not a question word."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik heb geen geld voor een nieuwe fiets, maar ik spaar ___ (voor).","answers":["ervoor","daarvoor"],"en":"I have no money for a new bike, but I am saving for it.","note":"Voor + it (the bike) becomes ervoor. With stress on 'that', daarvoor is also right."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (met) betaal jij meestal: met je pas of met je telefoon?","answers":["Waarmee"],"en":"What do you usually pay with: your card or your phone?","note":"The question word for met + what is waarmee: waar + mee, because met becomes mee. Never *met wat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De maandelijkse premie is veertig euro. Wat vind jij ___ (van)?","answers":["ervan","daarvan"],"en":"The monthly premium is forty euros. What do you think of it?","note":"Van + it (the premium) becomes ervan. Daarvan, with more stress, is also correct; van het is not."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik heb","words":["er","drie weken","op","gewacht"],"answers":["Ik heb er drie weken op gewacht."],"en":"I waited three weeks for it.","note":"Er comes directly after the conjugated verb heb, the time stands in the middle, and op goes to the end, just before the participle."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik reken","words":["er","niet","op","dat","de verzekering","alles","vergoedt"],"answers":["Ik reken er niet op dat de verzekering alles vergoedt."],"en":"I am not counting on the insurance covering everything.","note":"Er … op points forward to the dat-clause. Niet goes between er and op, and the dat-clause comes after the main clause with its verb at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Daar","words":["heb","ik","geen","tijd","voor"],"answers":["Daar heb ik geen tijd voor."],"en":"I have no time for that.","note":"Daar is stressed and opens the sentence, so the verb heb comes second and ik follows it. Voor goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace op de aanmaning with er + preposition.","from":"Ik heb nog niet op de aanmaning gereageerd.","answers":["Ik heb er nog niet op gereageerd."],"en":"I have not responded to it yet.","note":"Er goes up to the place right after heb, and op stays at the end before the participle. Nog niet stands between the two parts."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace op mijn zus with a pronoun.","from":"Ik reken op mijn zus.","answers":["Ik reken op haar."],"en":"I am counting on her.","note":"Mijn zus is a person, so the pronoun stays after the preposition: op haar. Erop would turn her into a thing."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Ik wacht er lang op.","answers":["Ik heb er lang op gewacht."],"en":"I waited for it for a long time.","note":"Er stays after the conjugated verb, now heb. The participle gewacht goes to the very end, and op stands directly before it."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb wat spaargeld, maar ik doe niets met het.","answers":["Ik heb wat spaargeld, maar ik doe er niets mee.","Ik heb wat spaargeld, maar ik doe daar niets mee.","Ik heb wat spaargeld, maar daar doe ik niets mee."],"en":"I have some savings, but I do nothing with them.","note":"Met + het is impossible for a thing: it becomes er + mee. Er goes after doe and mee to the end, with niets in between."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb erop lang gewacht.","answers":["Ik heb er lang op gewacht."],"en":"I waited for it for a long time.","note":"When something stands between them, er and the preposition split: er after heb, op just before the participle."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Op wat wacht je nog?","answers":["Waar wacht je nog op?","Waarop wacht je nog?","Waar wacht jij nog op?","Waarop wacht jij nog?"],"en":"What are you still waiting for?","note":"Preposition + wat becomes waar + preposition. In speech waar opens the question and op goes to the end; waarop together is also correct."}]},
  {"id":"b1-06","level":"B1","icon":"🧾","title":"Ordering online","grammar":"Er with numbers and amounts","summary":"When the noun after a number or amount is left out, er takes its place: Ik heb er drie. Er zijn er geen meer. Not when the noun is there, and not when you mean all the things already named: Ik stuur ze allebei terug.","explain":"In English you can simply say *I have three* or *I ordered two*. Dutch cannot leave the noun out just like that: when a number or an amount stands without its noun, Dutch adds **er**. This er means roughly \"of them\" and is never translated.\n\n> Hoeveel pakketten heb je? Ik heb er drie. — How many parcels have you got? I have three.\n\n### Which words need er\n\nEr is needed after every word that says **how many**, when the noun after it is left out:\n\n- numbers: `Ik heb er twee besteld.`\n- `veel`, `weinig`, `genoeg`, `een paar`, `meer`: `We krijgen er weinig.`\n- `geen`: `Er zijn er geen meer.`\n- `een` (often written `één`) and `nog een`: `Ze sturen me er nog een.`\n- `hoeveel` in a question: `Hoeveel heb je er besteld?`\n\n> Die mokken zijn mooi. Ik neem er vier. — Those mugs are nice. I will take four.\n> Heeft u er nog een op voorraad? — Do you have another one in stock?\n> We hebben dozen genoeg; je mag er een paar meenemen. — We have plenty of boxes; you can take a few.\n\n### When there is no er\n\n- When the noun is there: `Ik heb drie pakketten`, not *Ik heb er drie pakketten.\n- When the noun cannot be counted (`koffie`, `geld`): `Ik heb nog genoeg`, without er.\n- When you mean all of the things you were talking about. Then it is a normal pronoun, `ze` or `het`, often with `allebei` or `alle drie`.\n\n> Ik heb twee jurken ontvangen en ik stuur ze allebei terug. — I received two dresses and I am sending them both back.\n\n`Ik heb er twee` means two of a larger number or of a kind; `Ik heb ze` means exactly those things.\n\n### Where er goes\n\nThis er is never stressed and **never stands first**. It goes in the middle of the sentence:\n\n- directly after the conjugated verb: `Ik heb er drie besteld.`\n- after the subject when the subject follows the verb: `Gisteren heb ik er drie besteld.`\n- after a short pronoun object: `Ze sturen me er nog een.`\n- in a sub-clause, after the subject: `Ik weet niet hoeveel ik er heb besteld.`\n\nThe number keeps its own place, so other words can stand between er and the number: `Ik heb er gisteren drie besteld.`\n\n### Two kinds of er in one sentence\n\nYou know `er` from *there is, there are* (unit A2 · 26). When a sentence starts with that er and the noun after the number is left out, you need **both**:\n\n> Er zitten er twee in de doos. — There are two in the box.\n> De schoenen zijn uitverkocht; er zijn er geen meer. — The shoes are sold out; there are none left.\n\nWhen the two would stand side by side, because something else comes first, you say er only **once**: `Morgen komen er nog twee`, never *er er.\n\n### The kinds of er so far\n\n| kind | example | meaning |\n| place | Ik woon er al jaren. | there (unstressed) |\n| there is | Er ligt een pakket voor de deur. | there is / are |\n| + preposition | Ik wacht erop. | it, after a preposition |\n| + number | Ik heb er drie. | of them |\n\nUnit B2 · 10 puts all of them together.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Do not drop er: *Ik heb twee besteld → `Ik heb er twee besteld`.\n- No er when the noun is there: *Ik heb er twee pakketten → `Ik heb twee pakketten`.\n- Things you already named are `ze`: *Ik stuur er allebei terug → `Ik stuur ze allebei terug`.\n- Er never first: *Er heb ik drie → `Ik heb er drie`.\n- After *there is* a second er: *Er zijn twee beschadigd → `Er zijn er twee beschadigd`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian can simply say the number: `У мене три.` Dutch must add er, much as English adds *one* in *I want another one*: `Ik heb er drie`, `Ik wil er nog een`.","words":[{"nl":"bestelling","art":"de","pl":"bestellingen","en":"order","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pakket","art":"het","pl":"pakketten","en":"parcel","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezorger","art":"de","pl":"bezorgers","en":"delivery person","pos":"n"},{"nl":"webwinkel","art":"de","pl":"webwinkels","en":"online shop","pos":"n"},{"nl":"winkelmandje","art":"het","pl":"winkelmandjes","en":"shopping basket (online)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verzendkosten","art":"de","pl":"","en":"shipping costs","pos":"n","tag":"always plural"},{"nl":"voorraad","art":"de","pl":"voorraden","en":"stock / supply","pos":"n"},{"nl":"exemplaar","art":"het","pl":"exemplaren","en":"copy / item (one of a kind)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"recensie","art":"de","pl":"recensies","en":"review","pos":"n"},{"nl":"klacht","art":"de","pl":"klachten","en":"complaint","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezorgen","past":"bezorgde","perf":"heeft bezorgd","en":"to deliver","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontvangen","past":"ontving","perf":"heeft ontvangen","en":"to receive","pos":"v"},{"nl":"terugsturen","past":"stuurde terug","perf":"heeft teruggestuurd","en":"to send back / to return","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"beschadigd","en":"damaged","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"uitverkocht","en":"sold out","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik verwacht twee pakketten, maar ze hebben er pas één bezorgd.","en":"I am expecting two parcels, but so far they have delivered only one.","hint":["verwachten","pakket","pas","een","bezorgen"],"note":"Eén stands without its noun pakket, so er takes its place, directly after hebben. Without er, *ze hebben pas één bezorgd sounds unfinished.","alt":["Ik verwacht twee pakketten, maar ze hebben er pas een bezorgd."]},{"nl":"Hoeveel heeft u er nog op voorraad?","en":"How many do you (formal) still have in stock?","hint":["hoeveel","hebben","nog","voorraad"],"note":"Hoeveel without a noun needs er. Er stands after the subject u, because in a question the subject follows the verb.","alt":["Hoeveel hebt u er nog op voorraad?"]},{"nl":"Er zitten er twee in de doos, maar ik heb er drie besteld.","en":"There are two in the box, but I ordered three.","hint":["zitten","twee","doos","drie","bestellen"],"note":"The first er is 'there are', the second one stands for the missing noun after twee. Both are needed. In the second half er comes right after heb.","alt":["Er zitten er twee in de doos, maar ik had er drie besteld."]},{"nl":"De schoenen zijn uitverkocht; er zijn er geen meer.","en":"The shoes are sold out; there are none left.","hint":["schoen","uitverkocht","geen","meer"],"note":"Geen without a noun also needs er. Together with er for 'there are' that makes two: er zijn er geen."},{"nl":"Ik heb de twee boeken ontvangen en ik stuur ze allebei terug.","en":"I have received the two books and I am sending them both back.","hint":["twee","boek","ontvangen","allebei","terugsturen"],"note":"These are the books you already named, all of them, so a normal pronoun: ze allebei. Er would mean 'some of them'.","alt":["Ik heb de twee boeken ontvangen en ik stuur ze alle twee terug."]},{"nl":"We hebben genoeg lege dozen in de schuur; je mag er een paar meenemen.","en":"We have plenty of empty boxes in the shed; you can take a few.","hint":["genoeg","leeg","doos","schuur","een paar","meenemen"],"note":"Een paar without its noun dozen needs er, after the modal verb mag. Meenemen stays in one piece at the end.","alt":["We hebben genoeg lege dozen in de schuur; je kunt er een paar meenemen.","We hebben genoeg lege dozen in de schuur; jij mag er een paar meenemen."]},{"nl":"Het eerste exemplaar was beschadigd, dus ze sturen me er nog een.","en":"The first copy was damaged, so they are sending me another one.","hint":["eerste","exemplaar","beschadigd","sturen","nog een"],"note":"Nog een without a noun needs er, the Dutch version of 'one' in 'another one'. A short pronoun object comes before er: me er.","alt":["Het eerste exemplaar was beschadigd, dus ze sturen me er nog één.","Het eerste exemplaar was beschadigd, dus ze sturen mij er nog een."]},{"nl":"Ik weet niet meer hoeveel ik er heb besteld.","en":"I no longer know how many I ordered.","hint":["weten","niet meer","hoeveel","bestellen"],"note":"In a sub-clause er comes directly after the subject ik. The two verbs at the end can stand in either order.","alt":["Ik weet niet meer hoeveel ik er besteld heb."]},{"nl":"Ik bestel vaak jurken online; vorige maand heb ik er drie teruggestuurd.","en":"I often order dresses online; last month I sent three back.","hint":["bestellen","vaak","jurk","online","vorige maand","terugsturen"],"note":"Vorige maand stands first, so heb comes second and ik follows it. Er goes directly after the subject; drie keeps its place before the participle.","alt":["Ik bestel vaak jurken online; ik heb er vorige maand drie teruggestuurd.","Ik bestel vaak jurken online; vorige maand stuurde ik er drie terug."]},{"nl":"Vandaag zijn er drie pakketten gekomen en morgen komen er nog twee.","en":"Three parcels came today and two more are coming tomorrow.","hint":["vandaag","pakket","komen","morgen","nog"],"note":"In the first half the noun is there, so er only means 'there'. In the second half er 'there' and er 'of them' would stand side by side, so there is just one er.","alt":["Vandaag zijn er drie pakketten gekomen en er komen er morgen nog twee.","Vandaag kwamen er drie pakketten en morgen komen er nog twee."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Hoeveel pakketten verwacht je vandaag? Ik verwacht ___ drie.","options":["ze","er","het","–"],"correct":1,"en":"How many parcels are you expecting today? I am expecting three.","note":"Drie stands without its noun, so er must take its place. Ze would mean particular parcels you already know about, and drie alone is not complete Dutch."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb de twee jurken ontvangen, maar ik stuur ___ allebei terug.","options":["er","het","ze"],"correct":2,"en":"I have received the two dresses, but I am sending them both back.","note":"De twee jurken are known things and you mean all of them, so the pronoun ze. Er is for some of them, a number out of a larger group."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Er zitten ___ drie in de doos.","options":["–","ze","er"],"correct":2,"en":"There are three in the box.","note":"The first er means 'there are'; the noun after drie is left out, so a second er is needed: er zitten er drie."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Die mokken zijn mooi en er zijn geen verzendkosten, dus ik heb ___ besteld.","options":["vier er","er vier","vier"],"correct":1,"en":"Those mugs are nice and there are no shipping costs, so I ordered four.","note":"Er goes before the number, directly after the conjugated verb heb. Vier alone, without er, is not complete."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik heb ___ (vijf) in mijn winkelmandje.","answers":["er vijf"],"en":"I have five in my shopping basket.","note":"The noun after vijf is left out, so er stands before it, right after the verb heb."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Krijgt u veel klachten? Nee, we krijgen ___ (weinig).","answers":["er weinig"],"en":"Do you get many complaints? No, we get few.","note":"Amount words such as weinig, veel and genoeg also need er when a noun you can count, like klachten, is left out."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik wil die lamp graag hebben. Heeft u ___ (nog een) op voorraad?","answers":["er nog een","er nog één"],"en":"I would like that lamp. Do you (formal) have another one in stock?","note":"English says 'one', Dutch says er + nog een. Er comes after the subject u, because the subject follows the verb in a question."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoeveel bestellingen heb je deze maand al geplaatst? Ik heb ___ (al vier) geplaatst.","answers":["er al vier"],"en":"How many orders have you placed this month? I have already placed four.","note":"Er stands directly after heb; al and the number come after it. Never *al er vier."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vandaag","words":["heeft","de bezorger","er","al","veertig","gebracht"],"answers":["Vandaag heeft de bezorger er al veertig gebracht."],"en":"Today the delivery man has already brought forty.","note":"Vandaag stands first, so heeft comes second and the subject de bezorger follows. Er goes directly after the subject, then al and the number."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ze","words":["sturen","me","er","morgen","nog een"],"answers":["Ze sturen me er morgen nog een."],"en":"They are sending me another one tomorrow.","note":"A short pronoun object like me comes before er. The time word stands between er and nog een."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik weet niet","words":["hoeveel","ik","er","besteld","heb"],"answers":["Ik weet niet hoeveel ik er besteld heb.","Ik weet niet hoeveel ik er heb besteld."],"en":"I do not know how many I ordered.","note":"In the sub-clause er follows the subject ik and the verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Leave out pakketten and use er.","from":"Ik heb drie pakketten ontvangen.","answers":["Ik heb er drie ontvangen."],"en":"I have received three.","note":"Without the noun drie needs er. Er takes the place right after the conjugated verb heb, before the number."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Er zijn and leave out exemplaren.","from":"Twee exemplaren zijn beschadigd.","answers":["Er zijn er twee beschadigd."],"en":"Two are damaged.","note":"The first er is 'there are'; the second stands for the missing noun after twee. You need both."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De webwinkel had nog vijf lampen en ik heb drie gekocht.","answers":["De webwinkel had nog vijf lampen en ik heb er drie gekocht."],"en":"The online shop had five lamps left and I bought three.","note":"Drie stands without its noun lampen, so er is needed, directly after heb."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb twee recensies gelezen en ik vond er allebei goed.","answers":["Ik heb twee recensies gelezen en ik vond ze allebei goed."],"en":"I read two reviews and I thought they were both good.","note":"You mean both of the reviews you just named, so the pronoun ze. Er goes with a number or amount that stands without its noun (er een, er twee), never with allebei."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Er heb ik drie besteld.","answers":["Ik heb er drie besteld.","Drie heb ik er besteld."],"en":"I ordered three.","note":"This er can never stand first. Put the subject first and er directly after the verb heb."}]},
  {"id":"b1-07","level":"B1","icon":"📝","title":"Looking for a job","grammar":"Te + infinitive, and om te","summary":"Verbs like proberen, vergeten and besluiten take te + infinitive; modal verbs, gaan, laten and blijven take none. A purpose needs om te: Ik bel om een afspraak te maken. Te goes inside a separable verb: op te bellen.","explain":"When one verb depends on another, English often puts *to* in between: *I try to call*, but *I can call*. Dutch does the same with **te**: `Ik probeer te bellen`, but `Ik kan bellen`. Which verbs take te must be learned, but the groups are small. On top of that, Dutch uses **om … te** for a purpose: *in order to*.\n\n### Verbs with te\n\nThese verbs take `te` + infinitive:\n\n- `proberen`, `vergeten`, `beginnen`, `beloven`, `besluiten`, `hopen`, `weigeren`, `durven`, `verwachten`, `lijken`\n- `hoeven`, only with a negation or a limiting word such as `alleen`, `maar` or `pas`: `Je hoeft niet te komen`\n- phrases: `van plan zijn`, `zin hebben`, `tijd hebben`, `het leuk vinden`\n\n> Ik probeer elke week op twee vacatures te solliciteren. — I try to apply for two vacancies every week.\n> Hij durfde niet naar het salaris te vragen. — He did not dare to ask about the salary.\n> Je hoeft geen motivatiebrief te schrijven. — You do not have to write a cover letter.\n\n### Verbs without te\n\n| with te | without te |\n| proberen, vergeten | kunnen, moeten, mogen, willen, zullen |\n| beginnen, besluiten | gaan, komen, blijven |\n| hopen, beloven | laten, zien, horen, voelen |\n| durven, weigeren | leren, helpen |\n\n> Kunt u volgende week al beginnen? — Can you start as early as next week?\n> Ik ga morgen solliciteren. — I am going to apply tomorrow.\n\n`leren` and `helpen` are normally used without te (`Ze leert autorijden`); with a longer group after them you will also meet te.\n\n### Om … te: in order to\n\nTo say **why** someone does something, use `om … te`. Here `om` cannot be left out.\n\n> Ik bel de werkgever om een sollicitatiegesprek af te spreken. — I am calling the employer to arrange a job interview.\n\n`om … te` is also normal after a noun or an adjective:\n\n> Dit is een goede kans om ervaring op te doen. — This is a good chance to gain experience.\n> Het is moeilijk om een goede brief te schrijven. — It is hard to write a good letter.\n\nAfter `proberen`, `vergeten`, `beloven`, `besluiten`, `weigeren` and the phrases, `om` is often added too, and both are correct: `Ik probeer (om) op tijd te komen`, `Ik ben van plan (om) te verhuizen`. After `hoeven`, `durven`, `lijken`, `beginnen` and `verwachten` there is no `om`.\n\n### Word order\n\n- `te` stands **directly before** the infinitive. Objects and other words go before te, and after `om`: `om een afspraak te maken`. A group with a preposition may also follow the infinitive: `om te vragen naar het salaris`.\n- In a separable verb `te` goes **between** the two parts, written as three words: `op te bellen`, `aan te nemen`, `voor te bereiden`.\n- The te-group comes **after** the final verb of the main clause. In the perfect it follows the participle:\n\n> Ze is vergeten haar cv mee te nemen. — She forgot to bring her CV.\n> Het bedrijf heeft besloten haar aan te nemen. — The company has decided to hire her.\n\nWith `proberen` and `beginnen` you will also hear `Ik heb u proberen te bellen`, with an infinitive instead of the participle, and `Ik heb u geprobeerd te bellen`; all three are correct (more in unit B1 · 26). `hoeven` always takes the infinitive there, `durven` normally: `Hij heeft het niet durven vragen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No te after a modal verb: *Ik moet te werken → `Ik moet werken`.\n- No te after gaan: *Ik ga te solliciteren → `Ik ga solliciteren`.\n- Do not forget te after proberen and the others: *Ik probeer bellen → `Ik probeer te bellen`.\n- A purpose needs om: *Ik bel een afspraak te maken → `Ik bel om een afspraak te maken`.\n- Te inside a separable verb: *te opbellen → `op te bellen`.\n- The te-group goes after the participle, not before it: `Ik heb geprobeerd je te bellen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nThe Ukrainian infinitive looks the same after every verb: `хочу працювати`, `намагаюся працювати`. Dutch adds `te` after some verbs and not after others. For a purpose, where Ukrainian uses `щоб`, Dutch uses `om … te`: `Я телефоную, щоб домовитися` is `Ik bel om een afspraak te maken`.","words":[{"nl":"sollicitatiegesprek","art":"het","pl":"sollicitatiegesprekken","en":"job interview","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vacature","art":"de","pl":"vacatures","en":"vacancy","pos":"n"},{"nl":"werkgever","art":"de","pl":"werkgevers","en":"employer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"proeftijd","art":"de","pl":"proeftijden","en":"probation period","pos":"n"},{"nl":"motivatiebrief","art":"de","pl":"motivatiebrieven","en":"cover letter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"cv","art":"het","pl":"cv's","en":"CV","pos":"n"},{"nl":"solliciteren","past":"solliciteerde","perf":"heeft gesolliciteerd","en":"to apply (for a job)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aannemen","past":"nam aan","perf":"heeft aangenomen","en":"to hire / to accept (a job)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"voorbereiden","past":"bereidde voor","perf":"heeft voorbereid","en":"to prepare","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"weigeren","past":"weigerde","perf":"heeft geweigerd","en":"to refuse","pos":"v"},{"nl":"besluiten","past":"besloot","perf":"heeft besloten","en":"to decide","pos":"v"},{"nl":"durven","past":"durfde","perf":"heeft gedurfd","en":"to dare","pos":"v"},{"nl":"gemotiveerd","en":"motivated","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik probeer elke week op twee vacatures te solliciteren.","en":"I try to apply for two vacancies every week.","hint":["proberen","elke week","vacature","solliciteren"],"note":"Proberen takes te + infinitive. Te stands directly before solliciteren; everything that belongs to it comes before te.","alt":["Ik probeer om elke week op twee vacatures te solliciteren.","Elke week probeer ik op twee vacatures te solliciteren."]},{"nl":"Ze is vergeten haar cv mee te nemen.","en":"She forgot to bring her CV.","hint":["vergeten","cv","meenemen"],"note":"Vergeten takes te. The te-group comes after the participle vergeten, and te goes inside the separable verb: mee te nemen.","alt":["Ze is vergeten om haar cv mee te nemen.","Ze heeft vergeten haar cv mee te nemen.","Ze heeft vergeten om haar cv mee te nemen."]},{"nl":"Ik bel de werkgever om een sollicitatiegesprek af te spreken.","en":"I am calling the employer to arrange a job interview.","hint":["bellen","werkgever","sollicitatiegesprek","afspreken"],"note":"This is a purpose, so om … te, and om cannot be left out. The object stands between om and te, and te splits afspreken."},{"nl":"Je hoeft geen motivatiebrief te schrijven.","en":"You do not have to write a cover letter.","hint":["hoeven","geen","motivatiebrief","schrijven"],"note":"Hoeven is the negative of moeten and, unlike moeten, it takes te. It is only used with a negation such as geen or niet, or with a limiting word such as alleen, maar or pas.","alt":["Jij hoeft geen motivatiebrief te schrijven."]},{"nl":"Je moet je goed voorbereiden op het sollicitatiegesprek.","en":"You have to prepare well for the job interview.","hint":["moeten","goed","voorbereiden","sollicitatiegesprek"],"note":"Moeten is a modal verb, so the infinitive comes without te. A separable verb stays in one piece as an infinitive: voorbereiden.","alt":["Je moet het sollicitatiegesprek goed voorbereiden.","Jij moet je goed voorbereiden op het sollicitatiegesprek.","Je moet je goed op het sollicitatiegesprek voorbereiden."]},{"nl":"Hij durfde niet naar het salaris te vragen.","en":"He did not dare to ask about the salary.","hint":["durven","niet","salaris","vragen"],"note":"Durven takes te. Niet belongs to durfde and comes before the te-group; naar het salaris stands before te vragen.","alt":["Hij durfde niet te vragen naar het salaris."]},{"nl":"Ik ben van plan om volgend jaar een andere baan te zoeken.","en":"I am planning to look for another job next year.","hint":["van plan zijn","volgend jaar","ander","baan","zoeken"],"note":"Van plan zijn takes (om) te. Here om is optional, because it is not a purpose; the infinitive zoeken stands last with te.","alt":["Ik ben van plan volgend jaar een andere baan te zoeken."]},{"nl":"Het bedrijf heeft besloten haar aan te nemen.","en":"The company has decided to hire her.","hint":["bedrijf","besluiten","aannemen"],"note":"The te-group follows the participle besloten. Aannemen is separable, so te stands between aan and nemen.","alt":["Het bedrijf heeft besloten om haar aan te nemen."]},{"nl":"Dit is een goede kans om ervaring op te doen.","en":"This is a good chance to gain experience.","hint":["goed","kans","ervaring","opdoen"],"note":"After a noun such as kans Dutch normally uses om … te. Opdoen is separable: op te doen.","alt":["Dit is een goede kans ervaring op te doen."]},{"nl":"Ik heb geprobeerd u gisteren te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","en":"I tried to call you (formal) yesterday, but you did not answer.","hint":["proberen","gisteren","bellen","opnemen"],"note":"In the perfect the te-group comes after the participle geprobeerd. Opnemen is separable, so op goes to the end of the second clause.","alt":["Ik heb gisteren geprobeerd u te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Gisteren heb ik geprobeerd u te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Ik heb u gisteren proberen te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Gisteren heb ik u proberen te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Ik heb u gisteren geprobeerd te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Gisteren heb ik u geprobeerd te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Ik heb geprobeerd om u gisteren te bellen, maar u nam niet op.","Ik heb gisteren geprobeerd om u te bellen, maar u nam niet op."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik probeer elke dag een motivatiebrief ___ schrijven.","options":["te","–","om"],"correct":0,"en":"I try to write a cover letter every day.","note":"Proberen takes te + infinitive. Om can be added before the group (om een motivatiebrief te schrijven), but it never replaces te."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na de proeftijd wil ik graag bij dit bedrijf ___ blijven.","options":["te","–","om te"],"correct":1,"en":"After the probation period I would like to stay with this company.","note":"Willen is a modal verb: the infinitive follows without te. That English says 'to stay' does not matter."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik bel de werkgever ___ naar het salaris te vragen.","options":["–","om","te"],"correct":1,"en":"I am calling the employer to ask about the salary.","note":"The call has a purpose: you call in order to ask. A purpose always needs om … te; te is already there before vragen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze weigerde de functie ___.","options":["te aannemen","aannemen","aan te nemen"],"correct":2,"en":"She refused to accept the position.","note":"Weigeren takes te, and in a separable verb te goes between the two parts: aan te nemen, never *te aannemen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vergeet niet de werkgever een bedankmail ___ (sturen).","answers":["te sturen"],"en":"Do not forget to send the employer a thank-you e-mail.","note":"Vergeten takes te, also in the imperative. Te stands directly before the infinitive."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik heb geprobeerd de vacature ___ (opslaan), maar de site deed het niet.","answers":["op te slaan"],"en":"I tried to save the vacancy, but the site was not working.","note":"Proberen takes te, and opslaan is separable, so te goes in the middle: op te slaan."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tijdens het gesprek liet de manager me een korte test ___ (doen).","answers":["doen"],"en":"During the interview the manager had me do a short test.","note":"Laten belongs to the verbs without te, like the modal verbs: liet … doen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["is","hij","vergeten","zijn cv mee te nemen"],"answers":["Gisteren is hij vergeten zijn cv mee te nemen."],"en":"Yesterday he forgot to bring his CV.","note":"Gisteren stands first, so is comes second and hij follows. The te-group comes after the participle vergeten, at the very end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik bel","words":["om","het gesprek","af","te zeggen"],"answers":["Ik bel om het gesprek af te zeggen."],"en":"I am calling to cancel the interview.","note":"Om opens the purpose group and the object follows it. Te goes between the parts of afzeggen: af te zeggen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik ben","words":["erg","gemotiveerd","om","bij uw bedrijf","te werken"],"answers":["Ik ben erg gemotiveerd om bij uw bedrijf te werken.","Ik ben erg gemotiveerd om te werken bij uw bedrijf."],"en":"I am very motivated to work at your (formal) company.","note":"After an adjective such as gemotiveerd the te-group comes with om. What belongs to werken normally stands between om and te; a group with a preposition, like bij uw bedrijf, may also follow te werken."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Ik probeer.","from":"Ik kom op tijd.","answers":["Ik probeer op tijd te komen.","Ik probeer om op tijd te komen."],"en":"I try to be on time.","note":"Proberen takes te, so komen becomes te komen at the end. After proberen om may be added; it is not a purpose here."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Ze besluit de functie aan te nemen.","answers":["Ze heeft besloten de functie aan te nemen.","Ze heeft besloten om de functie aan te nemen."],"en":"She has decided to accept the position.","note":"Heeft takes second place and the participle besloten goes to the end of the main clause. The te-group stays behind it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say that it is not necessary: use hoeven.","from":"Je moet je diploma's laten zien.","answers":["Je hoeft je diploma's niet te laten zien."],"en":"You do not have to show your diplomas.","note":"Moeten takes no te, hoeven does, and hoeven needs niet. Laten zien is two verbs; te goes before the first: te laten zien."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik moet morgen een cv te sturen.","answers":["Ik moet morgen een cv sturen."],"en":"I have to send a CV tomorrow.","note":"Moeten is a modal verb, and modal verbs never take te: moet … sturen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ze probeert de werkgever te opbellen.","answers":["Ze probeert de werkgever op te bellen.","Ze probeert om de werkgever op te bellen."],"en":"She is trying to call the employer.","note":"Opbellen is separable, so te goes between the two parts: op te bellen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik bel de werkgever te vragen naar de proeftijd.","answers":["Ik bel de werkgever om te vragen naar de proeftijd.","Ik bel de werkgever om naar de proeftijd te vragen."],"en":"I am calling the employer to ask about the probation period.","note":"You call in order to ask: that is a purpose, and a purpose needs om … te. Without om it is not correct Dutch."}]},
  {"id":"b1-08","level":"B1","icon":"🏃","title":"What are they doing?","grammar":"Something is going on: aan het, zitten te, bezig zijn","summary":"The present tense usually covers 'I am doing'. To stress that it is in progress: zijn + aan het + infinitive (Ik ben een boek aan het lezen), zitten/staan/liggen/lopen + te (Hij zit te lezen), or bezig zijn met.","explain":"Dutch has no separate continuous tense. `Ik lees` means both *I read* and *I am reading*, and most of the time that is all you need. When you want to stress that something is **going on right now**, or was going on at a certain moment, Dutch has three constructions.\n\n### 1. zijn + aan het + infinitive\n\nThis is the most common one. `zijn` is conjugated and stands in its normal place; `aan het` + infinitive goes to the end.\n\n> Ik ben aan het koken. — I am cooking.\n> Ze was aan het bellen toen ik binnenkwam. — She was on the phone when I came in.\n\nAn **object** goes **before** `aan het`, never after the infinitive:\n\n> Mijn oma is een sjaal aan het haken. — My grandmother is crocheting a scarf.\n\nA separable verb stays in one piece: `aan het opruimen`, `aan het opwarmen`. `niet` comes before `aan het`: `Ik ben niet aan het werken`. A place may stand before `aan het` or after the infinitive: `Ze zijn op het veld aan het voetballen` and `Ze zijn aan het voetballen op het veld` are both fine.\n\n### 2. zitten, staan, liggen, lopen + te + infinitive\n\nThese say what someone is doing **and** in what position. With `zitten`, `staan` and `liggen` the position is usually real; with `lopen` it is often faint and the phrase sounds a little annoyed: someone *keeps* doing something.\n\n| verb | example | meaning |\n| zitten | Hij zit te lezen. | he is (sitting) reading |\n| staan | Ze staan te wachten. | they are (standing) waiting |\n| liggen | De kat ligt te slapen. | the cat is (lying) asleep |\n| lopen | Hij loopt te zeuren. | he keeps moaning |\n\n> Opa zit in de tuin te puzzelen. — Grandad is sitting in the garden doing a puzzle.\n> Hij loopt al de hele dag te zeuren over zijn spierpijn. — He has been moaning about his sore muscles all day.\n\nThe object goes before `te`: `Mijn moeder zit een boek te lezen`. In the perfect the te usually disappears and you get two infinitives: `Hij heeft de hele avond zitten lezen` (more in unit B1 · 26). `zitten te lezen` is also heard there and is not wrong, but without te is the usual form.\n\n### 3. bezig zijn\n\n`bezig zijn` means *to be busy*. It takes `met` + a noun, or `(om) te` + infinitive.\n\n> Ik ben al een uur bezig met mijn puzzel. — I have been busy with my puzzle for an hour.\n> Mijn man is bezig de schaatsen te slijpen. — My husband is busy sharpening the skates.\n\nWith a bare infinitive `met` also works: `Ik ben bezig met koken`.\n\n### Not with states\n\nAll three are for **activities**, things you do. Verbs that describe a state, such as `weten`, `kennen`, `hebben`, `willen` or `vinden` (think), never take them:\n\n- *Ik ben het antwoord aan het weten → `Ik weet het antwoord`.\n- *Ze is een fiets aan het hebben → `Ze heeft een fiets`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Not zijn + infinitive alone: *Ik ben lezen → `Ik ben aan het lezen` or `Ik lees`. (`Hij is vissen` does exist, but it means that he is away, fishing.)\n- The object before aan het: *Ik ben aan het lezen een boek → `Ik ben een boek aan het lezen`.\n- Do not forget te: *Hij zit lezen → `Hij zit te lezen`.\n- Separable verbs stay whole: *Ik ben aan het ruimen op → `Ik ben aan het opruimen`.\n- In the perfect, zitten becomes an infinitive, not a participle: *Hij heeft gezeten te lezen → `Hij heeft zitten lezen` (usually without te).\n- No mixing: *bezig met de fiets te maken → `bezig de fiets te maken` or `bezig met de fiets`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nThese constructions are about one thing: an activity in progress at a given moment. They are not a translation of the Ukrainian imperfective aspect in general; how Dutch handles aspect is the subject of unit B1 · 22.","words":[{"nl":"sportschool","art":"de","pl":"sportscholen","en":"gym","pos":"n"},{"nl":"spierpijn","art":"de","pl":"","en":"sore muscles / muscle ache","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"kleedkamer","art":"de","pl":"kleedkamers","en":"changing room","pos":"n"},{"nl":"veld","art":"het","pl":"velden","en":"field / pitch","pos":"n"},{"nl":"doelpunt","art":"het","pl":"doelpunten","en":"goal (a score)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"scheidsrechter","art":"de","pl":"scheidsrechters","en":"referee","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toeschouwer","art":"de","pl":"toeschouwers","en":"spectator","pos":"n"},{"nl":"hardlopen","past":"liep hard","perf":"heeft/is hardgelopen","en":"to run (as a sport) / to jog","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"schaatsen","past":"schaatste","perf":"heeft/is geschaatst","en":"to skate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opwarmen","past":"warmde op","perf":"heeft opgewarmd","en":"to warm up","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"juichen","past":"juichte","perf":"heeft gejuicht","en":"to cheer","pos":"v"},{"nl":"haken","past":"haakte","perf":"heeft gehaakt","en":"to crochet","pos":"v"},{"nl":"puzzelen","past":"puzzelde","perf":"heeft gepuzzeld","en":"to do puzzles","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vissen","past":"viste","perf":"heeft gevist","en":"to fish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zeuren","past":"zeurde","perf":"heeft gezeurd","en":"to moan / to nag","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zweten","past":"zweette","perf":"heeft gezweet","en":"to sweat","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik kan nu niet praten, want ik ben aan het hardlopen.","en":"I cannot talk now, because I am out running.","hint":["kunnen","nu","praten","want","hardlopen"],"note":"Zijn + aan het + infinitive stresses that it is going on right now. Ben is the conjugated verb, aan het hardlopen goes to the end."},{"nl":"De spelers zijn op het veld aan het opwarmen.","en":"The players are warming up on the pitch.","hint":["speler","veld","opwarmen"],"note":"Opwarmen is separable, but after aan het it stays in one piece. The place can stand before aan het or after the infinitive.","alt":["De spelers zijn aan het opwarmen op het veld.","Op het veld zijn de spelers aan het opwarmen."]},{"nl":"Mijn oma is een sjaal aan het haken voor mijn dochter.","en":"My grandmother is crocheting a scarf for my daughter.","hint":["oma","sjaal","haken","dochter"],"note":"The object een sjaal goes before aan het, never after the infinitive. Voor mijn dochter may follow the infinitive.","alt":["Mijn oma is voor mijn dochter een sjaal aan het haken."]},{"nl":"Opa zit in de tuin te puzzelen.","en":"Grandad is sitting in the garden doing a puzzle.","hint":["opa","zitten","tuin","puzzelen"],"note":"Zitten + te + infinitive: what he is doing and that he is sitting. Te stands directly before puzzelen, at the end.","alt":["In de tuin zit opa te puzzelen.","Opa zit te puzzelen in de tuin."]},{"nl":"Bij de ingang staan veel toeschouwers te wachten.","en":"Lots of spectators are standing at the entrance waiting.","hint":["ingang","staan","veel","toeschouwer","wachten"],"note":"Staan + te + infinitive. Bij de ingang stands first, so staan comes second and the subject veel toeschouwers follows it.","alt":["Er staan veel toeschouwers bij de ingang te wachten.","Er staan bij de ingang veel toeschouwers te wachten.","Veel toeschouwers staan bij de ingang te wachten."]},{"nl":"Toen ik binnenkwam, lag mijn zoon op de bank te slapen.","en":"When I came in, my son was lying on the sofa asleep.","hint":["toen","binnenkomen","liggen","zoon","bank","slapen"],"note":"Liggen + te + infinitive also works in the past: lag … te slapen. It shows what was going on at that moment.","alt":["Toen ik binnenkwam, lag mijn zoon te slapen op de bank."]},{"nl":"Hij loopt al de hele dag te zeuren over zijn spierpijn.","en":"He has been moaning about his sore muscles all day.","hint":["lopen","de hele dag","zeuren","spierpijn"],"note":"Lopen + te here has nothing to do with walking: it means he keeps doing it, and it sounds annoyed. Al + present tense says how long it has been going on.","alt":["Hij loopt al de hele dag over zijn spierpijn te zeuren."]},{"nl":"Ik ben al een uur bezig met mijn puzzel.","en":"I have been busy with my puzzle for an hour.","hint":["al","uur","bezig zijn","puzzel"],"note":"Bezig zijn takes met + a noun. Al een uur + present tense: it started an hour ago and is still going on.","alt":["Ik ben al een uur met mijn puzzel bezig."]},{"nl":"Mijn man is bezig om de schaatsen te slijpen.","en":"My husband is busy sharpening the skates.","hint":["man","bezig zijn","schaats","slijpen"],"note":"With a verb, bezig zijn takes (om) te + infinitive. The object stands before te, not after it.","alt":["Mijn man is bezig de schaatsen te slijpen."]},{"nl":"Iedereen stond te juichen toen het doelpunt viel.","en":"Everyone was standing there cheering when the goal was scored.","hint":["iedereen","staan","juichen","toen","doelpunt","vallen"],"note":"Stond te juichen: staan + te in the simple past, for what was going on at that moment. In Dutch a goal valt.","alt":["Toen het doelpunt viel, stond iedereen te juichen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Bel straks maar terug, ik ben nu ___.","options":["koken","aan het koken","te koken"],"correct":1,"en":"Call back later, I am cooking right now.","note":"After zijn an activity in progress needs aan het: ben aan het koken. Ben te koken is not Dutch, and zijn + a bare infinitive (hij is vissen) means that someone is away doing it, not that it is going on now."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn buurman ___ al twee uur aan de waterkant te vissen.","options":["is","zit","heeft"],"correct":1,"en":"My neighbour has been sitting by the water fishing for two hours.","note":"Te + infinitive goes with zitten, staan, liggen or lopen. Zijn needs aan het, not te: is aan het vissen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"In de sauna van de sportschool zit hij flink ___.","options":["te zweten","zweten","aan het zweten"],"correct":0,"en":"In the sauna at the gym he is sitting there sweating a lot.","note":"Zitten takes te + infinitive. Aan het goes with zijn (hij is aan het zweten), not with zitten, and without te the sentence is wrong."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ het antwoord.","options":["ben aan het weten","weet","zit te weten"],"correct":1,"en":"I know the answer.","note":"Weten is a state, not an activity, so it never takes aan het or zitten te. The plain present is the only option."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De scheidsrechter is met de spelers ___ (praten).","answers":["aan het praten"],"en":"The referee is talking to the players.","note":"Is … aan het praten: zijn + aan het + infinitive, with met de spelers before aan het."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Kom je mee? Nee, ik ben mijn kamer ___ (opruimen).","answers":["aan het opruimen"],"en":"Are you coming along? No, I am tidying my room.","note":"The object mijn kamer stands before aan het, and the separable verb opruimen stays in one piece."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ze staan al een half uur bij de schaatsbaan ___ (wachten).","answers":["te wachten"],"en":"They have been standing by the ice rink waiting for half an hour.","note":"Staan takes te + infinitive. Te wachten goes to the end of the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik ben","words":["een trui","aan het","haken"],"answers":["Ik ben een trui aan het haken."],"en":"I am crocheting a jumper.","note":"The object een trui must come before aan het; the infinitive closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mijn moeder","words":["zit","een boek","te","lezen"],"answers":["Mijn moeder zit een boek te lezen."],"en":"My mother is sitting reading a book.","note":"Zit is the conjugated verb in second place. The object een boek goes before te, and te directly before lezen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Waarom","words":["sta","je","daar","te juichen"],"answers":["Waarom sta je daar te juichen?"],"en":"Why are you standing there cheering?","note":"After the question word comes the verb sta, without t because je follows. Te juichen goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Stress that it is going on right now: use aan het.","from":"De spelers warmen op.","answers":["De spelers zijn aan het opwarmen."],"en":"The players are warming up.","note":"The plural subject takes zijn. After aan het the separable verb comes together again: opwarmen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use aan het.","from":"Ze leest een tijdschrift.","answers":["Ze is een tijdschrift aan het lezen."],"en":"She is reading a magazine.","note":"Leest becomes is … aan het lezen. The object een tijdschrift moves in front of aan het."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with zitten + te.","from":"Opa kijkt naar de wedstrijd.","answers":["Opa zit naar de wedstrijd te kijken.","Opa zit te kijken naar de wedstrijd."],"en":"Grandad is sitting watching the match.","note":"Zit takes the place of kijkt, and te kijken goes to the end. Naar de wedstrijd usually stands before te, but may also follow."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik zag hem in de kleedkamer; hij stond met de trainer praten.","answers":["Ik zag hem in de kleedkamer; hij stond met de trainer te praten."],"en":"I saw him in the changing room; he was standing talking to the coach.","note":"In the present and the simple past, staan needs te before the infinitive: stond … te praten."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn man is bezig met de schaatsen te slijpen.","answers":["Mijn man is bezig de schaatsen te slijpen.","Mijn man is bezig om de schaatsen te slijpen."],"en":"My husband is busy sharpening the skates.","note":"Bezig zijn takes met + a noun or (om) te + infinitive, but not met together with te. Here there is a te-group, so met must go."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Hij zat de hele avond te gamen.","answers":["Hij heeft de hele avond zitten gamen.","Hij heeft de hele avond zitten te gamen."],"en":"He sat gaming all evening.","note":"In the perfect zitten becomes an infinitive too, not gezeten, and te usually disappears: heeft … zitten gamen. With te is also heard and not wrong."}]},
  {"id":"b1-09","level":"B1","icon":"🧳","title":"A journey with surprises","grammar":"The past perfect: had gewerkt, was gegaan","summary":"had / hadden or was / waren + past participle, with the same auxiliary as the perfect. Use it for what had already happened before another moment in the past: Toen we aankwamen, was de trein al vertrokken.","explain":"The past perfect (*de voltooid verleden tijd*) is the Dutch \"had done\". You use it when you are already telling something in the past and want to look **one step further back**: to something that was already finished before that moment.\n\n### The form\n\nTake the perfect and put the auxiliary in the simple past: `heeft` becomes `had` / `hadden`, `is` becomes `was` / `waren`. The past participle stays at the end, exactly as in the perfect.\n\n| | boeken | vertrekken |\n| ik, jij / je, u | had geboekt | was vertrokken |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | had geboekt | was vertrokken |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | hadden geboekt | waren vertrokken |\n\n> Ik had de kamer al geboekt. — I had already booked the room.\n> De bus was net vertrokken. — The bus had just left.\n\nThe choice between `hebben` and `zijn` is the same as in the perfect (unit A2 · 14). If the perfect is `heeft gewijzigd`, the past perfect is `had gewijzigd`; if it is `is vertrokken`, it is `was vertrokken`. Verbs of a change of place or state take `zijn`: `gaan`, `komen`, `vertrekken`, `aankomen`, `beginnen`, `worden`, `verlopen`, `uitvallen`. So do `blijven` and `zijn` itself: `ik was er al eens geweest`.\n\n> Mijn paspoort was verlopen. — My passport had expired.\n\n### When to use it\n\nThere are two moments in the past, and the one that came **first** gets the past perfect. Compare:\n\n> Toen we aankwamen, was de trein al vertrokken. — When we arrived, the train had already left.\n> Toen we aankwamen, vertrok de trein. — When we arrived, the train left.\n\nIn the first sentence we missed the train; in the second it left just as we got there. Words that often go with the past perfect: `al` (already), `net` (just), `nog niet` (not yet), `nog nooit` (never before).\n\n> Ik had nog nooit gevlogen. — I had never flown before.\n\nAfter `nadat` (after) the past perfect is the normal form when the main clause is in the past. You will also meet the simple past there, but not everyone accepts it, so use the past perfect yourself. With `voordat` (before) it is the other way round: the voordat-clause has the simple past, and the main clause may take the past perfect. The simple past is also fine there, because `voordat` already shows the order.\n\n> Nadat we hadden gegeten, gingen we terug naar het hotel. — After we had eaten, we went back to the hotel.\n> Hij had zijn reis al omgeboekt voordat de staking begon. — He had already rebooked his trip before the strike began.\n\nDo **not** use it for a row of events that you tell in the order they happened: `Ik stond op, ontbeet en ging naar het station`. Each step is simple past. The past perfect is only for a jump back.\n\n### Word order\n\nIn a main clause `had` or `was` stands in second place (first in a yes/no question), and the participle goes to the end. A separable verb stays one word: `omgeboekt`, `uitgevallen`.\n\nIn a sub-clause both verbs go to the end, and both orders are correct:\n\n> We misten de bus, omdat we ons hadden verslapen. — We missed the bus because we had overslept.\n\nThe same clause can end in `verslapen hadden`. When a sub-clause comes first, the main clause starts with its verb: `Toen we aankwamen, was …`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The auxiliary: *De trein had vertrokken → `De trein was vertrokken`.\n- No t after jij: *jij hadt → `jij had`.\n- English has one \"had\", Dutch two forms: *We had geboekt → `We hadden geboekt`.\n- After nadat, not the perfect: *Nadat we hebben gegeten, gingen we → `Nadat we hadden gegeten, gingen we`.\n- The participle goes last: *Ik had vergeten mijn paspoort → `Ik had mijn paspoort vergeten`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian rarely uses its own past perfect (давноминулий час); normally it takes the ordinary past, often with `уже`: `Коли ми приїхали, потяг уже поїхав`. Dutch has to show the step back in the verb: `was al vertrokken`. A Ukrainian perfective verb is not automatically a Dutch past perfect: use it only when the event lies before another moment in the past.\n\nYou will also meet `had` + participle in unreal sentences such as `Als ik dat had geweten …`; that is unit B2 · 2.","words":[{"nl":"boeking","art":"de","pl":"boekingen","en":"booking","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bevestiging","art":"de","pl":"bevestigingen","en":"confirmation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"reisverzekering","art":"de","pl":"reisverzekeringen","en":"travel insurance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"staking","art":"de","pl":"stakingen","en":"strike (by workers)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"accommodatie","art":"de","pl":"accommodaties","en":"accommodation / place to stay","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bezienswaardigheid","art":"de","pl":"bezienswaardigheden","en":"sight (worth seeing)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rondleiding","art":"de","pl":"rondleidingen","en":"guided tour","pos":"n"},{"nl":"omweg","art":"de","pl":"omwegen","en":"detour","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gids","art":"de","pl":"gidsen","en":"guide (person or book)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"omboeken","past":"boekte om","perf":"heeft omgeboekt","en":"to rebook","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"uitvallen","past":"viel uit","perf":"is uitgevallen","en":"to be cancelled (train, bus, lesson)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"wijzigen","past":"wijzigde","perf":"heeft gewijzigd","en":"to change / to alter","pos":"v"},{"nl":"volgeboekt","en":"fully booked","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"inmiddels","en":"by then / by now","pos":"adv"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Toen we op het station aankwamen, was de trein al vertrokken.","en":"When we arrived at the station, the train had already left.","hint":["toen","station","aankomen","trein","al","vertrekken"],"note":"The train left before we arrived, so it gets the past perfect. Vertrekken takes zijn, so was vertrokken; the toen-clause comes first, so was stands right after it.","alt":["De trein was al vertrokken toen we op het station aankwamen.","Toen we bij het station aankwamen, was de trein al vertrokken.","De trein was al vertrokken toen we bij het station aankwamen."]},{"nl":"Mijn paspoort was verlopen, dus ik mocht niet aan boord.","en":"My passport had expired, so I was not allowed on board.","hint":["paspoort","verlopen","dus","mogen","aan boord"],"note":"Verlopen is a change of state and takes zijn: is verlopen, so in the past perfect was verlopen. It had happened before the moment at the gate.","alt":["Mijn paspoort was verlopen, dus ik mocht niet aan boord gaan."]},{"nl":"We konden niet vertrekken, omdat alle treinen door een staking waren uitgevallen.","en":"We could not leave, because all the trains had been cancelled because of a strike.","hint":["kunnen","vertrekken","omdat","trein","staking","uitvallen"],"note":"Uitvallen takes zijn, and the subject alle treinen is plural: waren uitgevallen. In the omdat-clause both verbs stand at the end, in either order.","alt":["We konden niet vertrekken, omdat alle treinen door een staking uitgevallen waren.","We konden niet vertrekken, omdat alle treinen vanwege een staking waren uitgevallen.","We konden niet vertrekken, omdat alle treinen vanwege een staking uitgevallen waren."]},{"nl":"Nadat we de bevestiging hadden ontvangen, boekten we ook een rondleiding.","en":"After we had received the confirmation, we also booked a guided tour.","hint":["nadat","bevestiging","ontvangen","boeken","ook","rondleiding"],"note":"After nadat the past perfect is the normal form: hadden ontvangen. The main clause is in the simple past and starts with its verb, because the nadat-clause comes first.","alt":["Nadat we de bevestiging ontvangen hadden, boekten we ook een rondleiding.","We boekten ook een rondleiding nadat we de bevestiging hadden ontvangen.","We boekten ook een rondleiding nadat we de bevestiging ontvangen hadden.","Nadat we de bevestiging hadden gekregen, boekten we ook een rondleiding.","Nadat we de bevestiging gekregen hadden, boekten we ook een rondleiding."]},{"nl":"Had je nog nooit eerder gevlogen?","en":"Had you never flown before?","hint":["hebben","nog nooit","eerder","vliegen"],"note":"A yes/no question starts with the auxiliary. It is jij had, without t, so had je stays the same. Vliegen without a destination takes hebben.","alt":["Had jij nog nooit eerder gevlogen?","Had je nog nooit gevlogen?","Had jij nog nooit gevlogen?"]},{"nl":"De rondleiding was net begonnen toen we bij het museum aankwamen.","en":"The guided tour had just started when we arrived at the museum.","hint":["rondleiding","net","beginnen","toen","museum","aankomen"],"note":"Beginnen always takes zijn: is begonnen, so was begonnen. Net (just) shows that it started a moment before we arrived.","alt":["Toen we bij het museum aankwamen, was de rondleiding net begonnen."]},{"nl":"Bij aankomst zagen we dat het hotel onze boeking had gewijzigd.","en":"On arrival we saw that the hotel had changed our booking.","hint":["aankomst","zien","hotel","boeking","wijzigen"],"note":"The change happened before we saw it, so had gewijzigd. In the dat-clause had and gewijzigd both stand at the end, in either order.","alt":["Bij aankomst zagen we dat het hotel onze boeking gewijzigd had.","We zagen bij aankomst dat het hotel onze boeking had gewijzigd.","We zagen bij aankomst dat het hotel onze boeking gewijzigd had."]},{"nl":"Omdat we de afslag hadden gemist, moesten we een lange omweg maken.","en":"Because we had missed the exit, we had to make a long detour.","hint":["omdat","afslag","missen","moeten","lang","omweg"],"note":"Missing the exit came first, so hadden gemist. The omdat-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with moesten.","alt":["Omdat we de afslag gemist hadden, moesten we een lange omweg maken.","We moesten een lange omweg maken, omdat we de afslag hadden gemist.","We moesten een lange omweg maken, omdat we de afslag gemist hadden."]},{"nl":"Mijn ouders hadden de bezienswaardigheden nog niet gezien, maar ze moesten al naar huis.","en":"My parents had not seen the sights yet, but they already had to go home.","hint":["ouders","bezienswaardigheid","nog niet","zien","moeten","huis"],"note":"Mijn ouders is plural, so hadden, not had. Nog niet stands before the participle at the end.","alt":["Mijn ouders hadden de bezienswaardigheden nog niet gezien, maar zij moesten al naar huis."]},{"nl":"Hij had zijn reis al omgeboekt voordat de staking begon.","en":"He had already rebooked his trip before the strike began.","hint":["reis","al","omboeken","voordat","staking","beginnen"],"note":"With voordat the main clause can take the past perfect: had omgeboekt. The participle of a separable verb is one word, with ge in the middle: omgeboekt.","alt":["Voordat de staking begon, had hij zijn reis al omgeboekt."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze waren erg moe, want ze ___ in het vliegtuig niet geslapen.","options":["hadden","waren","was"],"correct":0,"en":"They were very tired, because they had not slept on the plane.","note":"Slapen takes hebben (heeft geslapen), so the past perfect uses had or hadden. Ze is plural here (ze waren), so hadden."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Toen ik je belde, ___ jij de boeking al gewijzigd.","options":["hadt","was","had"],"correct":2,"en":"When I called you, you had already changed the booking.","note":"Wijzigen takes hebben, and jij had has no t: hadt is an old form that is not used any more. Was would need a verb that takes zijn."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik dacht dat mijn paspoort nog geldig was, maar toen ik keek, ___ het al verlopen.","options":["had","was","is"],"correct":1,"en":"I thought my passport was still valid, but when I looked, it had already expired.","note":"Verlopen takes zijn: is verlopen. It had expired before the moment I looked, so the past perfect: was verlopen. Is belongs to the present, not to a story in the past."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik stond om zes uur op, ___ snel en ging naar het vliegveld.","options":["had ontbeten","ontbeet","was ontbeten"],"correct":1,"en":"I got up at six, had a quick breakfast and went to the airport.","note":"These are steps told in the order they happened, so each one is simple past. The past perfect is only for something that happened before another past moment."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De laatste trein was ___ (uitvallen), dus we namen een taxi.","answers":["uitgevallen"],"en":"The last train had been cancelled, so we took a taxi.","note":"The participle of a separable verb is one word with ge between the parts: uit + ge + vallen. Uitvallen takes zijn, which is why the sentence has was."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Nadat de gids alles ___ (hebben) uitgelegd, mochten we zelf rondkijken.","answers":["had"],"en":"After the guide had explained everything, we were allowed to look around by ourselves.","note":"After nadat the past perfect is the normal form when the main clause is in the past. De gids is one person, so had; heeft would be the perfect and does not fit a story in the past."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Toen we eindelijk een hotel vonden, was het inmiddels donker ___ (worden).","answers":["geworden"],"en":"When we finally found a hotel, it had got dark by then.","note":"Worden is a change of state and takes zijn: is geworden, so was geworden. Its participle is irregular: geworden."},{"type":"order","lead":"Toen we aankwamen,","words":["was","de bus","al","vertrokken"],"answers":["Toen we aankwamen, was de bus al vertrokken."],"en":"When we arrived, the bus had already left.","note":"The toen-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with the verb was, then the subject. Al comes before the participle at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het hotel was al volgeboekt,","words":["omdat","we","te laat","hadden","geboekt"],"answers":["Het hotel was al volgeboekt, omdat we te laat hadden geboekt.","Het hotel was al volgeboekt, omdat we te laat geboekt hadden."],"en":"The hotel was already fully booked, because we had booked too late.","note":"In an omdat-clause both verbs go to the end, and both orders are correct: hadden geboekt or geboekt hadden."},{"type":"order","lead":"Voordat we vertrokken,","words":["hadden","we","de route","al","goed","bekeken"],"answers":["Voordat we vertrokken, hadden we de route al goed bekeken."],"en":"Before we left, we had already had a good look at the route.","note":"The voordat-clause is in the simple past, and the main clause takes the past perfect. It starts with hadden, because the voordat-clause fills the first place."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the past perfect.","from":"Ik sloot een reisverzekering af.","answers":["Ik had een reisverzekering afgesloten."],"en":"I had taken out travel insurance.","note":"Afsluiten takes hebben (heeft afgesloten), so had afgesloten. The participle of the separable verb is one word and goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with omdat. The second thing happened first.","from":"Ik kon niet betalen. Ik verloor mijn portemonnee.","answers":["Ik kon niet betalen, omdat ik mijn portemonnee had verloren.","Ik kon niet betalen, omdat ik mijn portemonnee verloren had.","Ik kon niet betalen, omdat ik mijn portemonnee was verloren.","Ik kon niet betalen, omdat ik mijn portemonnee verloren was."],"en":"I could not pay, because I had lost my wallet.","note":"Losing the wallet came before the moment of paying, so it gets the past perfect: had verloren (verliezen also takes zijn, so was verloren is correct too). In the omdat-clause both verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences, starting with Nadat. Leave out daarna.","from":"We bekeken de bezienswaardigheden. Daarna gingen we terug naar de accommodatie.","answers":["Nadat we de bezienswaardigheden hadden bekeken, gingen we terug naar de accommodatie.","Nadat we de bezienswaardigheden bekeken hadden, gingen we terug naar de accommodatie.","Nadat we de bezienswaardigheden bekeken, gingen we terug naar de accommodatie."],"en":"After we had seen the sights, we went back to our accommodation.","note":"What happened first goes into the nadat-clause in the past perfect: hadden bekeken (the simple past bekeken is also heard, but not everyone accepts it). The main clause then starts with its verb: gingen we."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De trein had al vertrokken toen ik op het perron kwam.","answers":["De trein was al vertrokken toen ik op het perron kwam."],"en":"The train had already left when I got to the platform.","note":"Vertrekken takes zijn in the perfect (is vertrokken), so in the past perfect it takes was, not had."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"We waren moe, omdat we hadden slecht geslapen.","answers":["We waren moe, omdat we slecht hadden geslapen.","We waren moe, omdat we slecht geslapen hadden."],"en":"We were tired because we had slept badly.","note":"In a sub-clause the auxiliary does not stand second; it goes to the end together with the participle: slecht hadden geslapen or slecht geslapen hadden."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Nadat we hebben ingecheckt, gingen we de stad in.","answers":["Nadat we hadden ingecheckt, gingen we de stad in.","Nadat we ingecheckt hadden, gingen we de stad in.","Nadat we incheckten, gingen we de stad in."],"en":"After we had checked in, we went into town.","note":"The main clause is in the past (gingen), so the nadat-clause takes the past perfect: hadden ingecheckt, not the perfect hebben ingecheckt. The simple past incheckten is also heard, but not everyone accepts it."}]},
  {"id":"b1-10","level":"B1","icon":"📰","title":"In the news","grammar":"The passive: worden + past participle","summary":"Passive: worden + past participle at the end. Present wordt / worden, past werd / werden. The thing that undergoes the action is the subject; the doer is left out or added with door.","explain":"In an active sentence the subject does something: `De gemeente repareert de brug`. In a passive sentence (*de lijdende vorm*) the subject is the thing that **undergoes** the action, and the doer moves to the background or disappears: `De brug wordt gerepareerd`. News reports are full of it.\n\n### The form\n\nThe passive is a form of `worden` + the past participle at the end. `worden` agrees with the subject, as any verb does.\n\n| | present | simple past |\n| ik | word | werd |\n| jij / je, u | wordt | werd |\n| hij, zij / ze, het | wordt | werd |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | worden | werden |\n\n> De brug wordt gerepareerd. — The bridge is being repaired.\n> De nieuwe plannen worden morgen gepresenteerd. — The new plans will be presented tomorrow.\n> Het museum werd vorig jaar geopend. — The museum was opened last year.\n\nThe same rule as for every verb: `jij wordt`, but `word jij` when jij comes after the verb.\n\nEnglish `is repaired` (as something that happens) and `is being repaired` are both `wordt gerepareerd`; `was opened` (an event at a moment in the past) is `werd geopend`. English `has been repaired` is unit B1 · 11.\n\n### From active to passive\n\n1. The object of the active sentence becomes the subject.\n2. `worden` agrees with that new subject: singular `wordt` / `werd`, plural `worden` / `werden`.\n3. The main verb becomes a participle at the end.\n4. The old subject is left out, or added with `door` (by).\n\n> De buren kochten het huis. → Het huis werd door de buren gekocht. — The house was bought by the neighbours.\n> De omroep zendt de wedstrijden live uit. → De wedstrijden worden live uitgezonden. — The matches are broadcast live.\n\nThe door-part usually stands before the participle, but it may also come after it: `Het huis werd gekocht door de buren`.\n\n### When Dutch uses it\n\n- The doer is unknown: `De auto werd 's nachts gestolen`.\n- The doer is obvious or unimportant: `De krant wordt elke ochtend bezorgd`.\n- News, rules and descriptions of a process: `De groente wordt eerst gewassen`.\n\nIn spoken Dutch you can often say the same with `ze` (they): `Ze repareren de brug`. In written Dutch and the news, the passive is the normal choice.\n\nWhen the new subject has no definite article (`een`, a plural without `de`), Dutch usually adds `er`: `Er werd een man gearresteerd`. That is unit B1 · 11; in this unit the subjects are definite.\n\n### Word order\n\n`wordt` or `werd` stands in second place, or first in a yes/no question; the participle goes to the end. In a sub-clause both go to the end, in either order.\n\n> Wordt de wedstrijd live uitgezonden? — Is the match being broadcast live?\n> Ik weet niet of het artikel morgen wordt gepubliceerd. — I do not know whether the article will be published tomorrow.\n\nThe last sentence may also end in `gepubliceerd wordt`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The verb agrees with the new subject: *De uitslagen wordt bekendgemaakt → `De uitslagen worden bekendgemaakt`.\n- \"By\" is `door`, not `van`: *Het werd van de burgemeester geopend → `door de burgemeester`.\n- A participle, not an infinitive: *Het wordt morgen presenteren → `Het wordt morgen gepresenteerd`.\n- `wordt` + participle is the action going on; `is` + participle says that it is done: `De brug is gerepareerd` (unit B1 · 11).\n- `worden` without a participle means \"to become\": `Mijn zus wordt journalist` (she is going to be a journalist), not a passive.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian often uses a form without a subject (`Міст ремонтують`) or a reflexive verb (`Міст ремонтується`). Dutch cannot use a reflexive verb here: *De brug repareert zich is wrong. Use `worden`: `De brug wordt gerepareerd`.","words":[{"nl":"artikel","art":"het","pl":"artikelen","en":"article (in a newspaper)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitzending","art":"de","pl":"uitzendingen","en":"broadcast / programme","pos":"n"},{"nl":"krantenkop","art":"de","pl":"krantenkoppen","en":"headline","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gebeurtenis","art":"de","pl":"gebeurtenissen","en":"event / something that happens","pos":"n"},{"nl":"evenement","art":"het","pl":"evenementen","en":"event (organised)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"demonstratie","art":"de","pl":"demonstraties","en":"demonstration (protest)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"prijsuitreiking","art":"de","pl":"prijsuitreikingen","en":"award ceremony","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verslag","art":"het","pl":"verslagen","en":"report / account","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kijker","art":"de","pl":"kijkers","en":"viewer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitzenden","past":"zond uit","perf":"heeft uitgezonden","en":"to broadcast","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"publiceren","past":"publiceerde","perf":"heeft gepubliceerd","en":"to publish","pos":"v"},{"nl":"aankondigen","past":"kondigde aan","perf":"heeft aangekondigd","en":"to announce","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"uitreiken","past":"reikte uit","perf":"heeft uitgereikt","en":"to present (a prize) / to hand out","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"onthullen","past":"onthulde","perf":"heeft onthuld","en":"to unveil / to reveal","pos":"v"},{"nl":"plaatselijk","en":"local","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"landelijk","en":"national (across the country)","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Elke ochtend worden de belangrijkste krantenkoppen op de radio voorgelezen.","en":"Every morning the most important headlines are read out on the radio.","hint":["elke ochtend","belangrijk","krantenkop","radio","voorlezen"],"note":"The subject de krantenkoppen is plural, so worden. Elke ochtend stands first, so worden comes second and the subject follows; the participle voorgelezen goes to the end.","alt":["De belangrijkste krantenkoppen worden elke ochtend op de radio voorgelezen.","Elke ochtend worden de belangrijkste krantenkoppen voorgelezen op de radio.","De belangrijkste krantenkoppen worden elke ochtend voorgelezen op de radio."]},{"nl":"Het artikel werd gisteren op de website van de krant gepubliceerd.","en":"The article was published on the newspaper's website yesterday.","hint":["artikel","gisteren","website","krant","publiceren"],"note":"Simple past passive: werd + participle. Het artikel is singular, so werd, not werden. Who published it does not matter here, so there is no door.","alt":["Gisteren werd het artikel op de website van de krant gepubliceerd.","Het artikel werd gisteren gepubliceerd op de website van de krant.","Gisteren werd het artikel gepubliceerd op de website van de krant."]},{"nl":"De prijzen worden vanavond door de burgemeester uitgereikt.","en":"The prizes will be presented by the mayor this evening.","hint":["prijs","vanavond","burgemeester","uitreiken"],"note":"The doer is added with door. The participle of the separable verb uitreiken is one word: uitgereikt.","alt":["Vanavond worden de prijzen door de burgemeester uitgereikt.","De prijzen worden vanavond uitgereikt door de burgemeester.","Vanavond worden de prijzen uitgereikt door de burgemeester."]},{"nl":"Wordt de wedstrijd morgen live uitgezonden?","en":"Is the match being broadcast live tomorrow?","hint":["worden","wedstrijd","morgen","live","uitzenden"],"note":"A yes/no question starts with the conjugated verb, here wordt. The participle uitgezonden stays at the end."},{"nl":"Het nieuwe standbeeld werd vorige week door een bekende schrijver onthuld.","en":"The new statue was unveiled last week by a well-known writer.","hint":["nieuw","standbeeld","vorige week","bekend","schrijver","onthullen"],"note":"An event at a moment in the past: werd onthuld. Onthullen has the unstressed prefix ont-, so the participle has no ge: onthuld.","alt":["Vorige week werd het nieuwe standbeeld door een bekende schrijver onthuld.","Het nieuwe standbeeld werd vorige week onthuld door een bekende schrijver.","Vorige week werd het nieuwe standbeeld onthuld door een bekende schrijver."]},{"nl":"Weet jij of het evenement wordt afgelast?","en":"Do you know whether the event is being called off?","hint":["weten","of","evenement","afgelasten"],"note":"In the of-clause both verbs go to the end, and both orders are correct: wordt afgelast or afgelast wordt.","alt":["Weet je of het evenement wordt afgelast?","Weet jij of het evenement afgelast wordt?","Weet je of het evenement afgelast wordt?"]},{"nl":"De nieuwe maatregelen werden gisteren door de regering aangekondigd.","en":"The new measures were announced by the government yesterday.","hint":["nieuw","maatregel","gisteren","regering","aankondigen"],"note":"De maatregelen is plural, so werden. Aankondigen is separable: aan + ge + kondigd.","alt":["Gisteren werden de nieuwe maatregelen door de regering aangekondigd.","De nieuwe maatregelen werden gisteren aangekondigd door de regering.","Gisteren werden de nieuwe maatregelen aangekondigd door de regering."]},{"nl":"De uitzending werd door meer dan een miljoen kijkers bekeken.","en":"The broadcast was watched by more than a million viewers.","hint":["uitzending","meer dan","miljoen","kijker","bekijken"],"note":"The kijkers did the watching, so they come with door. The subject is de uitzending, singular, so werd, even though the kijkers are many.","alt":["De uitzending werd bekeken door meer dan een miljoen kijkers.","De uitzending werd door meer dan één miljoen kijkers bekeken.","De uitzending werd bekeken door meer dan één miljoen kijkers."]},{"nl":"Mijn zus wordt journalist en haar eerste artikel wordt volgende week gepubliceerd.","en":"My sister is going to be a journalist, and her first article will be published next week.","hint":["zus","worden","journalist","eerste","artikel","publiceren"],"note":"The first wordt has no participle and means 'becomes'. The second wordt goes with gepubliceerd at the end, so it is a passive.","alt":["Mijn zus wordt journalist en volgende week wordt haar eerste artikel gepubliceerd."]},{"nl":"De naam van de winnaar wordt nog niet bekendgemaakt.","en":"The name of the winner is not being announced yet.","hint":["naam","winnaar","nog niet","bekendmaken"],"note":"Nog niet stands before the participle at the end. The subject is de naam, singular, so wordt."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De artikelen ___ vorige week in een landelijke krant gepubliceerd.","options":["werd","werden","worden"],"correct":1,"en":"The articles were published in a national newspaper last week.","note":"Vorige week asks for the past, and de artikelen is plural: werden. Werd is the singular."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Op dit moment ___ het podium voor het concert opgebouwd.","options":["is","werd","wordt"],"correct":2,"en":"The stage for the concert is being set up right now.","note":"Something that is going on now is wordt + participle. Is opgebouwd would mean that it is already finished, and werd is the past."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het festival werd ___ de burgemeester geopend.","options":["van","door","bij","met"],"correct":1,"en":"The festival was opened by the mayor.","note":"The doer in a passive sentence comes with door. English 'by' is never van here."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Tijdens de prijsuitreiking van gisteren ___ (worden) de winnaars door de minister gefeliciteerd.","answers":["werden"],"en":"During yesterday's award ceremony the winners were congratulated by the minister.","note":"Gisteren asks for the simple past, and the subject de winnaars is plural: werden."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het nieuwe ziekenhuis wordt morgen officieel ___ (openen).","answers":["geopend"],"en":"The new hospital will be officially opened tomorrow.","note":"After worden comes the past participle, not the infinitive: ge + open + d: the stem open does not end in a letter of 't kofschip, so the ending is d."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De demonstratie werd een week van tevoren ___ (aankondigen).","answers":["aangekondigd"],"en":"The demonstration was announced a week in advance.","note":"The participle of a separable verb is one word with ge between the parts: aan + ge + kondigd."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (worden) jij morgen ook geïnterviewd voor de plaatselijke krant?","answers":["Word"],"en":"Are you also being interviewed for the local paper tomorrow?","note":"It is jij wordt, but when jij comes after the verb the t disappears: Word jij."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["werd","het verslag","van de vergadering","gepubliceerd"],"answers":["Gisteren werd het verslag van de vergadering gepubliceerd."],"en":"Yesterday the report of the meeting was published.","note":"Gisteren is the first element, so werd comes second and then the subject het verslag van de vergadering. The participle closes the sentence."},{"type":"order","lead":"Op de website staat","words":["dat","de uitzending van vanavond","wordt","uitgesteld"],"answers":["Op de website staat dat de uitzending van vanavond wordt uitgesteld.","Op de website staat dat de uitzending van vanavond uitgesteld wordt."],"en":"The website says that this evening's broadcast is being postponed.","note":"In a dat-clause both verbs go to the end, and both orders are correct: wordt uitgesteld or uitgesteld wordt."},{"type":"order","lead":"Het evenement","words":["wordt","elk jaar","door vrijwilligers","georganiseerd"],"answers":["Het evenement wordt elk jaar door vrijwilligers georganiseerd.","Het evenement wordt elk jaar georganiseerd door vrijwilligers.","Het evenement wordt door vrijwilligers elk jaar georganiseerd."],"en":"The event is organised by volunteers every year.","note":"Wordt stands second and the participle near the end. The door-part usually comes before the participle, but after it is also correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it passive. Leave out the doer.","from":"De krant publiceert het interview morgen.","answers":["Het interview wordt morgen gepubliceerd.","Morgen wordt het interview gepubliceerd."],"en":"The interview will be published tomorrow.","note":"The object het interview becomes the subject, so wordt (singular). Publiceert becomes the participle gepubliceerd at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it passive and keep the doer with door.","from":"De burgemeester onthulde het monument.","answers":["Het monument werd door de burgemeester onthuld.","Het monument werd onthuld door de burgemeester."],"en":"The monument was unveiled by the mayor.","note":"Onthulde is the simple past, so the passive uses werd. Het monument is the new subject, and the old subject comes back with door."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Weet je of and keep the passive.","from":"De gebeurtenis wordt in het journaal besproken.","answers":["Weet je of de gebeurtenis in het journaal wordt besproken?","Weet je of de gebeurtenis in het journaal besproken wordt?"],"en":"Do you know whether the event is being discussed on the news?","note":"In the of-clause wordt moves to the end with the participle, in either order. The sentence becomes a question, so it ends with a question mark."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De uitslagen wordt morgen bekendgemaakt.","answers":["De uitslagen worden morgen bekendgemaakt."],"en":"The results will be announced tomorrow.","note":"Worden agrees with the subject, and de uitslagen is plural: worden."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het concert werd van duizenden mensen bezocht.","answers":["Het concert werd door duizenden mensen bezocht."],"en":"The concert was attended by thousands of people.","note":"The doer of a passive sentence comes with door. Van is a typical mistake: it looks like 'of' or 'from', but for the doer only door is right."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De prijsuitreiking wordt dit jaar door een bekende zangeres presenteren.","answers":["De prijsuitreiking wordt dit jaar door een bekende zangeres gepresenteerd."],"en":"This year the award ceremony is being presented by a well-known singer.","note":"The passive is worden + past participle, not worden + infinitive: gepresenteerd."}]},
  {"id":"b1-11","level":"B1","icon":"🚨","title":"Safety first","grammar":"The passive: is gedaan, and er wordt","summary":"Perfect passive: zijn + participle, normally without geworden (De dader is gearresteerd); past perfect: was / waren. For an activity without a subject: er wordt + participle (Er wordt gebeld; Er is ingebroken).","explain":"In unit B1 · 10 you met the passive with `worden`: `De dader wordt gearresteerd`, `De dader werd gearresteerd`. This unit adds the perfect and the past perfect of the passive, and the passive with `er` for an activity where nobody in particular is the subject.\n\n### The perfect: zijn + participle\n\nThe perfect of the passive is a form of `zijn` + the participle. Normally there is **no** `geworden`.\n\n> De dader is gearresteerd. — The offender has been arrested.\n> De ramen zijn vervangen. — The windows have been replaced.\n> De brand was al geblust. — The fire had already been put out.\n\nThe auxiliary is always `zijn`, even when the active verb takes `hebben`: `De brandweer heeft de brand geblust`, but `De brand is geblust`.\n\nAll the forms together:\n\n| passive | English |\n| wordt gemaakt | is being made, gets made |\n| werd gemaakt | was being made, got made |\n| is gemaakt | has been made, was made |\n| was gemaakt | had been made |\n\nEnglish \"was made\" can be `werd gemaakt` or `is gemaakt`. The choice is the same as between the simple past and the perfect in an active sentence: news and short reports of a single event usually take the perfect (`Gisteren is er een man gearresteerd`), a story told step by step takes `werd`.\n\n### The same form as a state\n\n`is` + participle can also describe how something **is now**, the result: `De winkel is gesloten` (the shop is closed). Often both readings fit, and the meaning hardly differs: `De deur is op slot gedaan` (someone did it, and now it is locked).\n\n> Het terrein is goed beveiligd. — The site is well secured.\n\n### Er wordt: an activity without a subject\n\nSome sentences are only about an activity: there is no object that could become the subject. Dutch then puts `er` in the subject's place and uses `wordt` / `werd` / `is` + participle, always singular.\n\n> Er wordt gebeld. — Someone is ringing the doorbell.\n> Er wordt hier niet gerookt. — There is no smoking here.\n> Er is ingebroken. — There has been a break-in.\n> Er werd veel gelachen. — There was a lot of laughing.\n\nWhen another element comes first, `er` may be left out, and very often it is: `Hier wordt niet gerookt`. `Hier wordt er niet gerookt` is also correct. In a sub-clause `er` stays when nothing else takes its place: `Ik hoorde dat er was ingebroken`.\n\n### Er with an indefinite subject\n\nWhen the subject is indefinite (`een`, `veel`, a number, a plural without `de`), the passive usually starts with `er`, and the verb agrees with the subject that comes later:\n\n> Er worden hier veel fietsen gestolen. — A lot of bikes are stolen here.\n> Er is een man gearresteerd. — A man has been arrested.\n\nWhen something else comes first, `er` may stay or go: `In deze straat worden (er) veel fietsen gestolen`; both are correct.\n\n### Word order\n\nThe auxiliary stands second, the participle at the end. In a sub-clause both go to the end, in either order: `dat de dader is gearresteerd` or `dat de dader gearresteerd is`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Leave out geworden: say `De dader is gearresteerd`. `De dader is gearresteerd geworden` is not wrong, but in standard Dutch it is unusual and sounds heavy.\n- zijn, not hebben: *De brand heeft geblust → `De brand is geblust`.\n- Agreement after er: *Er wordt veel fietsen gestolen → `Er worden veel fietsen gestolen`.\n- Not het: *Het wordt hier niet gerookt → `Er wordt hier niet gerookt`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has a short form for a finished action: `Злочинця затримано`, `Вікна замінено`. Dutch says it with `is` / `zijn` + participle: `De dader is gearresteerd`. And where Ukrainian says `Тут не курять` without a subject, Dutch needs `er`: `Er wordt hier niet gerookt`.\n\nThe passive with a modal verb (`moet worden gedaan`) is unit B2 · 1.","words":[{"nl":"dader","art":"de","pl":"daders","en":"offender / culprit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"inbraak","art":"de","pl":"inbraken","en":"burglary / break-in","pos":"n"},{"nl":"getuige","art":"de","pl":"getuigen","en":"witness","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aangifte","art":"de","pl":"aangiftes","en":"report (to the police)","pos":"n","tag":"aangifte doen"},{"nl":"nooduitgang","art":"de","pl":"nooduitgangen","en":"emergency exit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rookmelder","art":"de","pl":"rookmelders","en":"smoke alarm","pos":"n"},{"nl":"boete","art":"de","pl":"boetes","en":"fine (to pay)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verbod","art":"het","pl":"verboden","en":"ban / prohibition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"arresteren","past":"arresteerde","perf":"heeft gearresteerd","en":"to arrest","pos":"v"},{"nl":"blussen","past":"bluste","perf":"heeft geblust","en":"to put out (a fire)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontruimen","past":"ontruimde","perf":"heeft ontruimd","en":"to evacuate (a building)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"inbreken","past":"brak in","perf":"heeft ingebroken","en":"to break in / to burgle","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"waarschuwen","past":"waarschuwde","perf":"heeft gewaarschuwd","en":"to warn / to alert","pos":"v"},{"nl":"redden","past":"redde","perf":"heeft gered","en":"to rescue / to save","pos":"v"},{"nl":"beveiligen","past":"beveiligde","perf":"heeft beveiligd","en":"to secure / to protect","pos":"v"},{"nl":"gewond","en":"injured / wounded","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De dader is inmiddels door de politie gearresteerd.","en":"The offender has now been arrested by the police.","hint":["dader","inmiddels","politie","arresteren"],"note":"The perfect of the passive is zijn + participle: is gearresteerd, without geworden. Arresteren itself takes hebben, but the passive always takes zijn.","alt":["De dader is inmiddels gearresteerd door de politie.","Inmiddels is de dader door de politie gearresteerd.","Inmiddels is de dader gearresteerd door de politie."]},{"nl":"De brand was al geblust toen de bewoners thuiskwamen.","en":"The fire had already been put out when the residents came home.","hint":["brand","al","blussen","toen","bewoner","thuiskomen"],"note":"Past perfect of the passive: was + participle. The fire was out before the residents came home.","alt":["Toen de bewoners thuiskwamen, was de brand al geblust."]},{"nl":"Alle bewoners zijn op tijd gered.","en":"All the residents have been rescued in time.","hint":["alle","bewoner","op tijd","redden"],"note":"Alle bewoners is plural, so zijn. The participle of redden is gered: the stem red ends in d, so no extra d is added."},{"nl":"Het terrein is sinds de inbraak beter beveiligd.","en":"Since the break-in the site has been better secured.","hint":["terrein","sinds","inbraak","beter","beveiligen"],"note":"Is beveiligd describes the result, how the site is now, and also that someone did it. Beveiligen has the unstressed prefix be-, so the participle has no ge.","alt":["Sinds de inbraak is het terrein beter beveiligd.","Het terrein is beter beveiligd sinds de inbraak."]},{"nl":"Vannacht is er bij onze buren ingebroken.","en":"There was a break-in at our neighbours' house last night.","hint":["vannacht","bij","buren","inbreken"],"note":"Inbreken has no object that could become the subject, so the passive uses er. The perfect takes is, even though the active is heeft ingebroken.","alt":["Er is vannacht bij onze buren ingebroken.","Vannacht is bij onze buren ingebroken.","Er is bij onze buren vannacht ingebroken.","Vannacht is er ingebroken bij onze buren.","Er is vannacht ingebroken bij onze buren."]},{"nl":"In dit gebouw wordt niet gerookt.","en":"There is no smoking in this building.","hint":["gebouw","niet","roken"],"note":"An activity without a subject: wordt + participle. In dit gebouw comes first, so er may be left out; with er it is also correct.","alt":["In dit gebouw wordt er niet gerookt.","Er wordt in dit gebouw niet gerookt.","Er wordt niet gerookt in dit gebouw."]},{"nl":"Er worden in deze straat veel fietsen gestolen.","en":"A lot of bikes are stolen in this street.","hint":["er","straat","veel","fiets","stelen"],"note":"Veel fietsen is an indefinite subject, so the sentence starts with er. The verb agrees with fietsen, which come later: worden, plural.","alt":["In deze straat worden veel fietsen gestolen.","In deze straat worden er veel fietsen gestolen.","Er worden veel fietsen gestolen in deze straat.","Er worden veel fietsen in deze straat gestolen."]},{"nl":"Het gebouw is binnen tien minuten ontruimd.","en":"The building was evacuated within ten minutes.","hint":["gebouw","binnen","minuut","ontruimen"],"note":"A short report of one event: the perfect is ontruimd. Werd ontruimd is also correct. Ontruimen has the prefix ont-, so there is no ge.","alt":["Het gebouw werd binnen tien minuten ontruimd.","Binnen tien minuten is het gebouw ontruimd.","Binnen tien minuten werd het gebouw ontruimd."]},{"nl":"Is er al aangifte gedaan van de inbraak?","en":"Has the burglary been reported to the police yet?","hint":["er","al","aangifte","doen","inbraak"],"note":"Aangifte has no article, so it is an indefinite subject and the sentence needs er. In the question the auxiliary is comes first.","alt":["Is er al aangifte van de inbraak gedaan?","Is er van de inbraak al aangifte gedaan?"]},{"nl":"De politie zegt dat er al twee getuigen zijn ondervraagd.","en":"The police say that two witnesses have already been questioned.","hint":["politie","zeggen","al","getuige","ondervragen"],"note":"Twee getuigen is indefinite, so er comes in. In the dat-clause zijn and ondervraagd stand at the end, in either order; de politie is singular in Dutch, so zegt.","alt":["De politie zegt dat er al twee getuigen ondervraagd zijn."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"In het hele gebouw zijn nieuwe rookmelders ___.","options":["geophangen","opgehangen","ophangen"],"correct":1,"en":"New smoke alarms have been put up in the whole building.","note":"The perfect of the passive is zijn + participle: zijn opgehangen. Ophangen is separable, so ge goes between op and hangen, not in front (*geophangen); the infinitive ophangen cannot follow zijn here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De brand ___ binnen een uur geblust.","options":["heeft","is","hebben"],"correct":1,"en":"The fire was put out within an hour.","note":"The fire did not put anything out; it was put out, so this is a passive, and the passive perfect takes zijn. De brandweer heeft de brand geblust would be active."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ werd tijdens de brand hard geschreeuwd.","options":["Het","Er","Hij"],"correct":1,"en":"During the fire there was loud shouting.","note":"Schreeuwen here has no object, so nothing can become the subject: er takes its place. Het and hij cannot stand there, because there is nothing that is shouted."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Er ___ vorig jaar in deze stad veel boetes uitgedeeld.","options":["is","zijn","werd"],"correct":1,"en":"A lot of fines were handed out in this city last year.","note":"After er the verb agrees with the real subject, veel boetes, which is plural: zijn. Is and werd are singular."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De nooduitgang ___ (zijn) met dozen geblokkeerd, dus niemand kon naar buiten.","answers":["was"],"en":"The emergency exit had been blocked with boxes, so nobody could get out.","note":"The story is in the past (kon), and the blocking had happened before: was + participle. Is would belong to a story in the present."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het verbod op vuurwerk is vorig jaar ___ (invoeren).","answers":["ingevoerd"],"en":"The ban on fireworks was introduced last year.","note":"Invoeren is separable, so the participle is in + ge + voerd, one word. Is + participle is the perfect of the passive."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Zijn de buren al ___ (waarschuwen)?","answers":["gewaarschuwd"],"en":"Have the neighbours been warned yet?","note":"Zijn + participle, the perfect of the passive. Waarschuwen has no unstressed prefix, so the participle gets ge: gewaarschuwd."},{"type":"order","lead":"Vannacht","words":["is","er","in de school","ingebroken"],"answers":["Vannacht is er in de school ingebroken.","Vannacht is er ingebroken in de school."],"en":"Last night there was a break-in at the school.","note":"Vannacht is the first element, so is comes second, then er. The participle ingebroken goes to the end; the place may also follow it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik heb gehoord","words":["dat","de dader","is","gearresteerd"],"answers":["Ik heb gehoord dat de dader is gearresteerd.","Ik heb gehoord dat de dader gearresteerd is."],"en":"I have heard that the offender has been arrested.","note":"In a dat-clause both verbs go to the end, and both orders are correct: is gearresteerd or gearresteerd is."},{"type":"order","lead":"Er","words":["zijn","twee gewonde fietsers","naar het ziekenhuis","gebracht"],"answers":["Er zijn twee gewonde fietsers naar het ziekenhuis gebracht."],"en":"Two injured cyclists have been taken to hospital.","note":"The subject twee gewonde fietsers is indefinite, so the sentence starts with er. Zijn agrees with the plural subject, and the participle closes the sentence."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"De dader werd door de politie gearresteerd.","answers":["De dader is door de politie gearresteerd.","De dader is gearresteerd door de politie.","De dader is door de politie gearresteerd geworden.","De dader is gearresteerd geworden door de politie."],"en":"The offender has been arrested by the police.","note":"Werd becomes is, and the participle stays. Geworden is normally left out: is gearresteerd geworden is not wrong, but unusual."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it passive, starting with Er.","from":"Mensen roken hier niet.","answers":["Er wordt hier niet gerookt."],"en":"There is no smoking here.","note":"Roken here has no object, so there is nothing to become the subject: er + wordt + participle. Wordt is always singular in this kind of sentence."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with In deze wijk.","from":"Er worden in deze wijk veel fietsen gestolen.","answers":["In deze wijk worden veel fietsen gestolen.","In deze wijk worden er veel fietsen gestolen."],"en":"A lot of bikes are stolen in this neighbourhood.","note":"In deze wijk takes the first place, so worden comes second. Er may stay or go; both are correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De gewonde man heeft door een buurman gered.","answers":["De gewonde man is door een buurman gered.","De gewonde man werd door een buurman gered.","De gewonde man is gered door een buurman.","De gewonde man werd gered door een buurman."],"en":"The injured man was rescued by a neighbour.","note":"The man did not rescue anyone, he was rescued: a passive, and the perfect of the passive takes zijn (is gered), even though redden itself takes hebben. Werd gered is also correct."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Er wordt in de zomer veel fietsen gestolen.","answers":["Er worden in de zomer veel fietsen gestolen."],"en":"A lot of bikes are stolen in the summer.","note":"Er is not the subject. The verb agrees with veel fietsen, which is plural: worden."}]},
  {"id":"b1-12","level":"B1","icon":"🌟","title":"If I could choose","grammar":"Zou: polite requests, wishes, advice and 'what if'","summary":"zou / zouden + infinitive at the end: a polite request, a wish, advice, a careful opinion. What if: Als + simple past, zou + infinitive (Als ik tijd had, zou ik meegaan). The simple past here is not past time.","explain":"`zou` is formally the past of `zullen`, but you will mostly use it as the Dutch **\"would\"**: for things that are not real, and to make a request or an opinion softer.\n\n### The form\n\n| | zullen: past |\n| ik, jij / je, u, hij, zij / ze, het | zou |\n| wij / we, jullie, zij / ze | zouden |\n\n`zou` is the conjugated verb, so it stands in second place (first in a yes/no question). The infinitive goes to the **end**, without `te`. Often there are two infinitives at the end: a modal verb and then the main verb: `zou … kunnen helpen`, `zou … willen hebben`.\n\n### Four everyday uses\n\n**A polite request.** `Zou u …?` or `Zou je …?` with `kunnen` or `willen` is more polite than `Kunt u …?`.\n\n> Zou u mij kunnen helpen? — Could you help me?\n> Zou je het raam willen dichtdoen? — Would you close the window, please?\n\nA separable verb may stay together at the end or take the modal between its parts: `willen dichtdoen` or `dicht willen doen`.\n\n**A wish**, mostly with `graag` or `wel … willen`:\n\n> Ik zou graag naar Japan gaan. — I would like to go to Japan.\n\n**Advice**, with `moeten` or with `Als ik jou was`:\n\n> Je zou eens naar de dokter moeten gaan. — You ought to go to the doctor.\n> Als ik jou was, zou ik het niet doen. — If I were you, I would not do it.\n\n**A careful opinion**: `Ik zou zeggen dat …` (I would say that …).\n\n### What if: als + simple past, zou + infinitive\n\nTo imagine a situation that is **not real now**, Dutch puts the als-clause in the simple past and the main clause with `zou` + infinitive.\n\n> Als ik meer tijd had, zou ik een cursus volgen. — If I had more time, I would take a course.\n\nThe simple past `had` does **not** mean past time here; it means \"not real\". Compare a real possibility: `Als ik tijd heb, ga ik mee` (perhaps I will have time) against `Als ik tijd had, zou ik meegaan` (I do not have it).\n\nTwo variants are also correct:\n\n- In speech the als-clause may take `zou` as well: `Als ik meer tijd zou hebben, zou ik …`.\n- The main clause may take the simple past instead of zou: `Als ik rijk was, kocht ik een huis`.\n\nWith `zijn`, the fixed phrase is `Als ik jou was`, never *Als ik jou ben.\n\n### Word order\n\nIn the als-clause the verb goes to the end: `als ik meer tijd had`. When the als-clause comes first, it is the first element, so the main clause **starts with the verb**: `…, zou ik …`. The als-clause may also come second: `Ik zou meegaan als ik tijd had`.\n\n> Als ik de loterij won, zou ik een wereldreis maken. — If I won the lottery, I would travel round the world.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Verb first after the als-clause: *Als ik rijk was, ik zou … → `Als ik rijk was, zou ik …`.\n- No te: *Ik zou graag te reizen → `Ik zou graag reizen`.\n- `was`, not `ben`: *Als ik jou ben → `Als ik jou was`.\n- English \"would\" for a habit in the past is not zou: *Vroeger zouden we elke zomer kamperen → `Vroeger kampeerden we elke zomer`.\n- Plural: *Wij zou graag … → `Wij zouden graag …`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian builds the same idea with `би` and the past form: `Якби я мав час, я б поїхав`. Dutch works much the same way: the past form `had` in the als-clause, and `zou` where Ukrainian has `б`.\n\nThe unreal past (`Als ik dat had geweten, zou ik …`) is unit B2 · 2, and `zou` for what someone else claims is unit B2 · 9.","words":[{"nl":"wens","art":"de","pl":"wensen","en":"wish","pos":"n"},{"nl":"advies","art":"het","pl":"adviezen","en":"advice (often from an expert) / recommendation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"raad","art":"de","pl":"","en":"advice / counsel","pos":"n","tag":"raad geven"},{"nl":"keuze","art":"de","pl":"keuzes","en":"choice","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wereldreis","art":"de","pl":"wereldreizen","en":"trip round the world","pos":"n"},{"nl":"loterij","art":"de","pl":"loterijen","en":"lottery","pos":"n"},{"nl":"pensioen","art":"het","pl":"pensioenen","en":"retirement / pension","pos":"n","tag":"met pensioen gaan"},{"nl":"droombaan","art":"de","pl":"droombanen","en":"dream job","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanraden","past":"raadde aan","perf":"heeft aangeraden","en":"to advise / to recommend","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"zich voorstellen","past":"stelde me voor","perf":"heeft zich voorgesteld","en":"to imagine","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opgeven","past":"gaf op","perf":"heeft opgegeven","en":"to give up","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"uitkomen","past":"kwam uit","perf":"is uitgekomen","en":"to come true (a dream, a wish)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"ideaal","en":"ideal","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"stiekem","en":"secretly","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"zonde","en":"a pity / a waste","pos":"adj","tag":"het is zonde"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Zou u mij even kunnen helpen met dit formulier?","en":"Could you (formal) help me with this form for a moment?","hint":["zullen","even","kunnen","helpen","formulier"],"note":"A polite request: zou + kunnen + infinitive. Zou stands first in the question; the two infinitives kunnen helpen stand at the end, before the extra part met dit formulier.","alt":["Zou u me even kunnen helpen met dit formulier?","Zou u mij even met dit formulier kunnen helpen?","Zou u me even met dit formulier kunnen helpen?"]},{"nl":"Zou je me wat raad willen geven?","en":"Would you give me some advice?","hint":["zullen","raad","willen","geven"],"note":"Zou je … willen …? is a friendly, polite request. It is jij zou, without t, so zou je does not change.","alt":["Zou jij me wat raad willen geven?","Zou je mij wat raad willen geven?","Zou jij mij wat raad willen geven?"]},{"nl":"Ik zou graag een wereldreis maken.","en":"I would like to travel round the world.","hint":["zullen","graag","wereldreis","maken"],"note":"A wish: zou graag + infinitive. The infinitive maken goes to the end, without te.","alt":["Ik zou graag een wereldreis willen maken."]},{"nl":"Wat zou jouw droombaan zijn?","en":"What would your dream job be?","hint":["wat","zullen","droombaan","zijn"],"note":"In a question with a question word, zou comes second, right after wat. The infinitive zijn closes the question.","alt":["Wat zou je droombaan zijn?"]},{"nl":"Ik zou je aanraden om eerst wat geld te sparen.","en":"I would advise you to save some money first.","hint":["zullen","aanraden","eerst","geld","sparen"],"note":"Ik zou … aanraden is a soft way to give advice. The separable verb aanraden stays in one piece at the end of the main part; then om … te sparen follows.","alt":["Ik zou je aanraden eerst wat geld te sparen.","Ik zou jou aanraden om eerst wat geld te sparen.","Ik zou jou aanraden eerst wat geld te sparen."]},{"nl":"Je zou je droom niet zo snel moeten opgeven.","en":"You should not give up your dream so quickly. (advice with zou)","hint":["zullen","droom","niet","snel","moeten","opgeven"],"note":"zou + moeten is advice, softer than je moet. The two infinitives stand at the end, the modal first: moeten opgeven.","alt":["Jij zou je droom niet zo snel moeten opgeven.","Je zou je droom niet zo snel op moeten geven."]},{"nl":"Als ik jou was, zou ik die baan aannemen.","en":"If I were you, I would take that job.","hint":["als","zijn","zullen","baan","aannemen"],"note":"Als ik jou was is a fixed phrase for advice, with was, not ben. The als-clause is the first element, so the main clause starts with zou.","alt":["Als ik jou was, nam ik die baan aan.","Ik zou die baan aannemen als ik jou was."]},{"nl":"Als ik de loterij won, zou ik meteen stoppen met werken.","en":"If I won the lottery, I would stop working straight away.","hint":["als","loterij","winnen","zullen","meteen","stoppen"],"note":"Won is the simple past, but it is not about the past: it says that this is not real. Then zou + infinitive in the main clause.","alt":["Als ik de loterij zou winnen, zou ik meteen stoppen met werken.","Ik zou meteen stoppen met werken als ik de loterij won.","Ik zou meteen stoppen met werken als ik de loterij zou winnen.","Als ik de loterij won, stopte ik meteen met werken.","Als ik de loterij won, zou ik meteen met werken stoppen.","Als ik de loterij zou winnen, zou ik meteen met werken stoppen.","Ik zou meteen met werken stoppen als ik de loterij won.","Ik zou meteen met werken stoppen als ik de loterij zou winnen."]},{"nl":"Wat zou je doen als je grootste droom morgen uitkwam?","en":"What would you do if your biggest dream came true tomorrow?","hint":["wat","zullen","doen","als","droom","uitkomen"],"note":"Uitkwam is the simple past for an imagined situation, even though it is about tomorrow. In the als-clause the separable verb stays together at the end: uitkwam.","alt":["Wat zou jij doen als je grootste droom morgen uitkwam?","Wat zou je doen als je grootste droom morgen zou uitkomen?","Wat zou je doen als je grootste droom morgen uit zou komen?","Wat zou jij doen als jouw grootste droom morgen uitkwam?","Wat zou jij doen als je grootste droom morgen zou uitkomen?","Wat zou jij doen als je grootste droom morgen uit zou komen?"]},{"nl":"Ik zou zeggen dat dat plan ideaal is voor jullie.","en":"I would say that plan is ideal for you (plural).","hint":["zullen","zeggen","plan","ideaal","jullie"],"note":"Ik zou zeggen makes an opinion more careful. The first dat joins the clauses, the second belongs to plan: dat plan.","alt":["Ik zou zeggen dat dat plan voor jullie ideaal is."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ stiekem best eerder met pensioen willen gaan.","options":["zouden","zou","zal"],"correct":1,"en":"Secretly I would quite like to retire earlier.","note":"Ik takes zou; zouden is the plural. Zal is a promise or a guess about the future, not a wish."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Als ik meer tijd ___, zou ik een cursus Japans volgen.","options":["heb","had","zal hebben"],"correct":1,"en":"If I had more time, I would take a Japanese course.","note":"An unreal situation: the als-clause takes the simple past, had. Heb would make it a real possibility, and that does not fit zou in the main clause."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Als ik rijk was, ___ een huis aan zee.","options":["ik kocht","kocht ik","ik zou kopen"],"correct":1,"en":"If I were rich, I would buy a house by the sea.","note":"The main clause may take the simple past instead of zou. Because the als-clause comes first, the verb must come first: kocht ik."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Je ___ eens advies moeten vragen aan een expert.","options":["zou","zouden","wou"],"correct":0,"en":"You ought to ask an expert for advice.","note":"Advice is zou + moeten, and je takes zou. Zouden is the plural, and wou is an informal past of willen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij ___ (zullen) graag een keer met de hele familie op reis gaan; dat is onze grootste wens.","answers":["zouden"],"en":"We would like to go on a trip with the whole family some time; that is our biggest wish.","note":"Wij is plural, so zouden. The infinitive gaan goes to the end."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Als ik durfde, ___ (zullen) ik zangeres worden.","answers":["zou"],"en":"If I dared, I would become a singer.","note":"The als-clause is the first element, so zou comes first in the main clause, before ik. Ik takes zou."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Als ik niet zo moe ___ (zijn), zou ik vanavond meegaan.","answers":["was","zou zijn"],"en":"If I were not so tired, I would come along tonight.","note":"The als-clause of an unreal situation takes the simple past: was. In speech zou zijn is also correct."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (zullen) zonde zijn als je nu opgaf.","answers":["zou"],"en":"It would be a pity if you gave up now.","note":"Het zou zonde zijn: zou + infinitive for something imagined. In the als-clause opgaf is the simple past of opgeven, joined because the verb stands at the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Als ik meer geld had,","words":["zou","ik","een wereldreis","maken"],"answers":["Als ik meer geld had, zou ik een wereldreis maken."],"en":"If I had more money, I would travel round the world.","note":"The als-clause counts as the first element, so zou comes next and ik follows. The infinitive maken goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zou","words":["u","even","de deur","willen","openhouden"],"answers":["Zou u even de deur willen openhouden?","Zou u de deur even willen openhouden?"],"en":"Would you (formal) hold the door open for a moment?","note":"A polite request starts with zou and u. The two infinitives go to the end, the modal first: willen openhouden."},{"type":"order","lead":"Zou","words":["jij","je","kunnen","voorstellen","om in het buitenland te wonen"],"answers":["Zou jij je kunnen voorstellen om in het buitenland te wonen?"],"en":"Could you imagine living abroad?","note":"Jij is the subject and je is the reflexive pronoun of zich voorstellen; it comes right after the subject. The infinitives stand at the end, and the om-part follows them."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it unreal (you do not have the choice), with zou in the main clause.","from":"Als ik de keuze heb, ga ik in Spanje wonen.","answers":["Als ik de keuze had, zou ik in Spanje gaan wonen.","Als ik de keuze zou hebben, zou ik in Spanje gaan wonen."],"en":"If I had the choice, I would go and live in Spain.","note":"Heb becomes the simple past had, and the main clause gets zou with the infinitives at the end: zou ik … gaan wonen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Give the same advice, starting with Als ik jou was.","from":"Doe dat niet.","answers":["Als ik jou was, zou ik dat niet doen.","Als ik jou was, deed ik dat niet."],"en":"If I were you, I would not do that.","note":"After the als-clause the main clause starts with its verb: zou ik. Niet stands before the infinitive doen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Als ik rijk was, ik zou een groot huis kopen.","answers":["Als ik rijk was, zou ik een groot huis kopen."],"en":"If I were rich, I would buy a big house.","note":"The als-clause is the first element, so the verb comes next: zou ik, not ik zou."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Als ik jou ben, zou ik meer rust nemen.","answers":["Als ik jou was, zou ik meer rust nemen."],"en":"If I were you, I would take more rest.","note":"The phrase for advice is Als ik jou was: an unreal situation takes the simple past, even though it is about now."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Vroeger zouden we elke zomer bij mijn opa logeren.","answers":["Vroeger logeerden we elke zomer bij mijn opa."],"en":"When I was young, we would stay with my grandad every summer.","note":"English 'would' for a habit in the past is the simple past in Dutch: logeerden. Zouden would mean that it was planned or imagined."}]},
  {"id":"b1-13","level":"B1","icon":"✉️","title":"Writing an e-mail","grammar":"D or t? The spelling of verb forms","summary":"Build the form, do not trust your ear. Present: stem + t for jij, u, hij (hij wordt), no t when jij/je follows (word je). Past and participle: -te/-t after 't kofschip, -de/-d after other sounds (praatte, antwoordde; gewerkt, gebeurd).","explain":"At the end of a Dutch word a d sounds exactly like a t, so `ik word`, `hij wordt` and `het werd` end in the same sound. Spelling does not follow the sound: it shows how the form is **built**, from the stem and the ending the rule asks for. Readers of your e-mails notice these mistakes first, so it pays to build every form on purpose.\n\n### Step 1: find the stem\n\nThe stem is the ik-form (unit A2 · 1). Keep the d of the stem, even though you hear a t: `worden` → `word`, `vinden` → `vind`, `antwoorden` → `antwoord`.\n\n### The present tense\n\n| | worden | praten |\n| ik | word | praat |\n| jij, u | wordt | praat |\n| hij, zij, het | wordt | praat |\n| wij, jullie, zij | worden | praten |\n\n- jij, u, hij, zij and het: stem + t, also after a d: `hij wordt`, `u vindt`, `zij verzendt`.\n- A stem that already ends in t gets no second t: `hij zit`, `zij praat`, `hij print`.\n- When jij or je comes directly after the verb, the t goes: `Word je`, `Vind jij`. After hij and u it stays: `Wordt hij`, `Vindt u`.\n\n> Mijn collega beantwoordt zijn mail altijd binnen een uur. — My colleague always answers his e-mail within an hour.\n> Vind je het onderwerp duidelijk? — Do you find the subject line clear?\n> In de bijlage vindt u het contract. — You will find the contract in the attachment.\n\n**The trick**: put `lopen` or `werken` in the same place. Where you hear a t, write one: `hij loopt` → `hij wordt`; `loop jij` → `word jij`; `ik loop` → `ik word`.\n\n### The simple past: -te or -de\n\nRegular verbs add **-te** when the stem ends in a sound of 't kofschip (t, k, f, s, ch, p) and **-de** after every other sound. Judge by the infinitive: `verhuizen` and `leven` have z and v, so they take -de, although the stem is written verhuis, leef.\n\n| infinitive | stem | past |\n| praten | praat | praatte |\n| antwoorden | antwoord | antwoordde |\n| mailen | mail | mailde |\n| verhuizen | verhuis | verhuisde |\n\nAfter a stem in t you write tt, after a stem in d you write dd: the letter of the stem plus the letter of the ending. Plural: `wij antwoordden`. Strong verbs change their vowel and add no ending: `werd`, `vond`, `verzond`.\n\n> Gisteren beantwoordde ik vijftig mails. — Yesterday I answered fifty e-mails.\n\n### The past participle: -t or -d\n\nThe same rule: if the past has -te, the participle ends in t; if it has -de, in d. A stem in t or d gets nothing extra: `gepraat`, `geantwoord`. Verbs with the unstressed prefix be-, ver-, ont-, her- or ge- get no ge- (`beantwoord`, `vermeld`).\n\n| past | participle |\n| werkte | gewerkt |\n| gebeurde | gebeurd |\n| verhuisde | verhuisd |\n\nIn many verbs the hij-form and the participle sound the same. Ask: is there a form of hebben, zijn or worden that goes with it? Then it is the participle. Or use the trick: `het loopt` is present, `het is gelopen` is a participle.\n\n> Dat gebeurt elke week. — That happens every week.\n> Wat is er gebeurd? — What has happened?\n\n### Participles before a noun, and the imperative\n\nBefore a noun a participle follows the adjective rule (unit A2 · 10): `de verhuisde collega`, `de gewenste datum`, `het gewijzigde rooster`, but `een gewijzigd rooster`. `vergroot` + e is `vergrote` (open syllable, one o): `de vergrote foto`.\n\nThe imperative is the stem, without t: `Word lid!`, `Antwoord snel.`\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *ik wordt → `ik word`; *hij antwoord → `hij antwoordt`.\n- *Wordt je teamleider? → `Word je teamleider?` But `Wordt je collega teamleider?` is right: there je means *your*, and the subject is je collega.\n- *hij printt → `hij print`.\n- *antwoorde → `antwoordde`; *verhuiste → `verhuisde`.\n- *het is gebeurt → `het is gebeurd`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nIn Ukrainian a final д stays voiced (`сад`, `мед`), so you can mostly write what you hear. In Dutch every final d sounds like t, so your ear cannot help you: build the form.","words":[{"nl":"bijlage","art":"de","pl":"bijlagen","en":"attachment (to an e-mail)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"onderwerp","art":"het","pl":"onderwerpen","en":"subject / subject line","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ontvanger","art":"de","pl":"ontvangers","en":"recipient","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afzender","art":"de","pl":"afzenders","en":"sender","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verzoek","art":"het","pl":"verzoeken","en":"request","pos":"n"},{"nl":"herinnering","art":"de","pl":"herinneringen","en":"reminder / memory","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beantwoorden","past":"beantwoordde","perf":"heeft beantwoord","en":"to answer / to reply to","pos":"v"},{"nl":"doorsturen","past":"stuurde door","perf":"heeft doorgestuurd","en":"to forward","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"bevestigen","past":"bevestigde","perf":"heeft bevestigd","en":"to confirm","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vermelden","past":"vermeldde","perf":"heeft vermeld","en":"to mention / to state","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verzenden","past":"verzond","perf":"heeft verzonden","en":"to send / to dispatch","pos":"v"},{"nl":"dringend","en":"urgent","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"alvast","en":"in advance / already (for now)","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"hierbij","en":"herewith / with this (in a letter)","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"zo spoedig mogelijk","en":"as soon as possible","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Mijn collega beantwoordt zijn mail altijd binnen een uur.","en":"My colleague always answers his e-mail within an hour.","hint":["collega","beantwoorden","mail","altijd","uur"],"note":"The stem is beantwoord, with the d of the verb. Mijn collega is a hij-subject, so stem + t: beantwoordt.","alt":["Mijn collega beantwoordt zijn e-mail altijd binnen een uur."]},{"nl":"Vanaf maandag word ik jullie contactpersoon.","en":"From Monday I am becoming your (plural) contact person.","hint":["vanaf","maandag","worden","contactpersoon"],"note":"Ik takes the bare stem: word, with no t. You hear a t, but the d belongs to the stem of worden.","alt":["Ik word vanaf maandag jullie contactpersoon.","Ik word jullie contactpersoon vanaf maandag."]},{"nl":"Vind je het onderwerp van deze mail duidelijk genoeg?","en":"Do you find the subject line of this e-mail clear enough?","hint":["vinden","onderwerp","mail","duidelijk","genoeg"],"note":"Je comes directly after the verb, so the t of jij vindt disappears: vind je. The d of the stem stays.","alt":["Vind jij het onderwerp van deze mail duidelijk genoeg?","Vind je het onderwerp van deze e-mail duidelijk genoeg?","Vind jij het onderwerp van deze e-mail duidelijk genoeg?"]},{"nl":"Vindt u het goed als ik u alvast een voorstel stuur?","en":"Is it all right with you (formal) if I send you a proposal in advance?","hint":["vinden","goed","alvast","voorstel","sturen"],"note":"Only jij and je lose the t after the verb. U keeps it, so vindt u, with dt.","alt":["Vindt u het goed dat ik u alvast een voorstel stuur?"]},{"nl":"De klant wordt boos als we niet snel antwoorden.","en":"The client gets angry if we do not reply quickly.","hint":["klant","worden","boos","snel","antwoorden"],"note":"De klant is a hij-subject: stem word + t = wordt. Antwoorden with we is the plural, the same as the infinitive.","alt":["De klant wordt boos als we niet snel reageren."]},{"nl":"Gisteren beantwoordde ik meer dan vijftig mails.","en":"Yesterday I answered more than fifty e-mails.","hint":["gisteren","beantwoorden","meer dan","vijftig","mail"],"note":"The stem beantwoord ends in d, which is not in 't kofschip, so the past ending is -de: beantwoord + de gives two d's.","alt":["Ik beantwoordde gisteren meer dan vijftig mails.","Gisteren beantwoordde ik meer dan vijftig e-mails.","Ik beantwoordde gisteren meer dan vijftig e-mails."]},{"nl":"Onze contactpersoon is vorige maand naar het buitenland verhuisd.","en":"Our contact person moved abroad last month.","hint":["contactpersoon","vorig","maand","buitenland","verhuizen"],"note":"Verhuizen has a z, which is not in 't kofschip: past verhuisde, participle verhuisd. It takes zijn, because it is a move from one place to another.","alt":["Vorige maand is onze contactpersoon naar het buitenland verhuisd.","Onze contactpersoon is vorige maand verhuisd naar het buitenland.","Onze contactpersoon verhuisde vorige maand naar het buitenland.","Vorige maand verhuisde onze contactpersoon naar het buitenland."]},{"nl":"Ik weet niet wat er met de herinnering is gebeurd.","en":"I do not know what has happened to the reminder.","hint":["weten","gebeuren","herinnering"],"note":"Is goes with it, so this is the participle: gebeurde has -de, so gebeurd ends in d. The present would be het gebeurt.","alt":["Ik weet niet wat er met de herinnering gebeurd is."]},{"nl":"Wilt u de gewenste datum bevestigen?","en":"Would you (formal) confirm the desired date?","hint":["willen","wensen","datum","bevestigen"],"note":"Wensen has an s, which is in 't kofschip: wenste, gewenst. Before de datum the participle takes -e like an adjective: de gewenste datum.","alt":["Kunt u de gewenste datum bevestigen?","Zou u de gewenste datum willen bevestigen?","Wil u de gewenste datum bevestigen?","Kan u de gewenste datum bevestigen?"]},{"nl":"Antwoord zo spoedig mogelijk op dit verzoek.","en":"Reply to this request as soon as possible.","hint":["antwoorden","zo spoedig mogelijk","verzoek"],"note":"The imperative is the bare stem: antwoord, with d and without t. Never *antwoordt here.","alt":["Antwoord op dit verzoek zo spoedig mogelijk.","Beantwoord dit verzoek zo spoedig mogelijk."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Wanneer ___ je teamleider?","options":["wordt","word","wort"],"correct":1,"en":"When do you become team leader?","note":"Je comes directly after the verb, so the t of jij wordt disappears: word je. The d stays, because it belongs to the stem."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ je collega ook teamleider?","options":["Word","Worden","Wordt"],"correct":2,"en":"Is your colleague becoming team leader too?","note":"Here je means 'your', and the subject is je collega: a hij-subject, so stem + t. Only the pronoun jij/je after the verb takes away the t."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Vorige week ___ ik alle mails van de klant door.","options":["stuurde","stuurte","stuurden"],"correct":0,"en":"Last week I forwarded all the client's e-mails.","note":"The stem stuur ends in r, which is not in 't kofschip, so -de: stuurde. Stuurden is the plural and does not go with ik."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het ___ vaak dat een ontvanger niet antwoordt.","options":["gebeurd","gebeurt","gebeurdt"],"correct":1,"en":"It often happens that a recipient does not reply.","note":"There is no hebben or zijn, so this is the present tense: het + stem + t = gebeurt. Gebeurd is the participle (het is gebeurd)."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij ___ (verzenden) de herinneringen altijd op vrijdag.","answers":["verzendt"],"en":"He always sends out the reminders on Fridays.","note":"The stem verzend ends in d; hij adds t: verzendt. Replace it by loopt and you hear the t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"In de bijlage ___ (vinden) u het contract.","answers":["vindt"],"en":"You (formal) will find the contract in the attachment.","note":"U takes stem + t, also when u comes after the verb: vindt u. Only jij and je lose the t there."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Gisteren ___ (antwoorden) ik meteen, maar ik kreeg geen reactie.","answers":["antwoordde"],"en":"Yesterday I replied immediately, but I got no response.","note":"The stem antwoord ends in d, not a 't kofschip sound, so add -de: antwoordde, with two d's. *Antwoorde is missing the d of the stem."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heeft u de datum al ___ (bevestigen)?","answers":["bevestigd"],"en":"Have you (formal) already confirmed the date?","note":"Heeft goes with it, so this is the participle. The past is bevestigde (g is not in 't kofschip), so the participle ends in d. Be- verbs get no ge-."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hierbij stuur ik u het ___ (wijzigen) rooster.","answers":["gewijzigde"],"en":"Herewith I send you (formal) the changed schedule.","note":"The participle is gewijzigd (wijzigde has -de). Before a noun after het it follows the adjective rule and takes -e: het gewijzigde rooster."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (worden) nu lid en ontvang elke maand onze nieuwsbrief!","answers":["Word"],"en":"Become a member now and receive our newsletter every month!","note":"The imperative is the bare stem, without t: Word lid! Ontvang is an imperative too."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a yes/no question.","from":"Jij vindt de bijlage niet.","answers":["Vind jij de bijlage niet?","Vind je de bijlage niet?"],"en":"Can't you find the attachment?","note":"In the question jij comes directly after the verb, so the t goes: vind jij. The d of the stem stays."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a yes/no question.","from":"Hij wordt morgen gebeld.","answers":["Wordt hij morgen gebeld?"],"en":"Is he being called tomorrow?","note":"With hij the t stays, wherever hij stands: wordt hij. Only jij and je make it disappear."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"De klant wijzigt de afspraak.","answers":["De klant heeft de afspraak gewijzigd."],"en":"The client has changed the appointment.","note":"Wijzigt is the present: stem + t. The participle is built differently: wijzigde has -de, so gewijzigd ends in d."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the simple past.","from":"De afzender vermeldt het ordernummer niet.","answers":["De afzender vermeldde het ordernummer niet."],"en":"The sender did not mention the order number.","note":"The stem vermeld ends in d, so the past ending is -de: vermeldde. Both d's are written, the stem's and the ending's."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik wordt zenuwachtig van dringende mails.","answers":["Ik word zenuwachtig van dringende mails.","Ik word zenuwachtig van dringende e-mails."],"en":"Urgent e-mails make me nervous.","note":"Ik takes only the stem: word. The t is for jij, u and hij (compare ik loop, hij loopt)."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hij printt alle bijlagen voor de vergadering.","answers":["Hij print alle bijlagen voor de vergadering."],"en":"He prints all the attachments for the meeting.","note":"The stem print already ends in t, and Dutch never writes a second t in the present: hij print, like hij zit."}]},
  {"id":"b1-14","level":"B1","icon":"🥘","title":"Food and traditions","grammar":"More about adjectives: iets lekkers, de gesloten deur, de zieke","summary":"After iets, niets, wat, veel and weinig an adjective with no noun after it takes -s (iets lekkers). Adjectives in -en never take -e (houten, gebakken, eigen); weak participles do (gekookte). As a noun: de zieke, de rijken, het leuke.","explain":"You know the basic rule from unit A2 · 10: an adjective before a noun takes -e, except before a het-word in the singular after een, geen or no article at all (`een warm gerecht`). This unit is about the cases where the adjective does something else: it takes -s, it never changes, or it becomes a noun itself.\n\n### iets lekkers: -s after words of quantity\n\nAfter `iets`, `niets`, `wat` (informal for iets), `veel`, `weinig`, `genoeg` and `allerlei`, an adjective with **no noun after it** takes **-s**.\n\n| word | adjective | together |\n| iets | lekker | iets lekkers |\n| niets | bijzonder | niets bijzonders |\n| veel | goed | veel goeds |\n| iets | beter | iets beters |\n\n- An adjective that already ends in s gets no second s: `iets fris`, `niets vers`.\n- `iets anders` (something else) is a fixed form.\n- When a noun follows, the normal rule is back: `veel lekkere gerechten`, `weinig verse groente`.\n\n> Heb je iets zoets voor bij de koffie? — Do you have something sweet to go with the coffee?\n> Er stond niets bijzonders op het menu. — There was nothing special on the menu.\n> Ik heb veel goeds over dat restaurant gehoord. — I have heard a lot of good things about that restaurant.\n\n### Adjectives that never take -e\n\n**Adjectives ending in unstressed -en** stay the same in every position. These are materials, participles that end in -en, and a few others like `eigen` and `open`.\n\n| type | examples |\n| material | houten, zilveren, gouden, wollen |\n| strong participle | gebakken, gebraden, gesneden, gesloten |\n| others | eigen, open, verlegen |\n\n> Het zilveren bestek gebruiken we alleen met kerst. — We only use the silver cutlery at Christmas.\n\nAlso unchanged: a few adjectives from other languages, such as `oranje`, `roze`, `lila`, `gratis`, `prima`, `plastic` and `extra` (`de roze taart`, `gratis koffie`). `rechter` and `linker` are written together with their noun: `de linkerhand`, `het rechteroog`.\n\nParticiples of **weak** verbs end in -d or -t and follow the normal rule: `de gekookte aardappelen`, `de bestelde pizza`, `de gestoofde peren`.\n\n> We aten gebraden kip met gekookte aardappelen. — We ate roast chicken with boiled potatoes.\n\nA few fixed combinations with a het-word have no -e even after het: `het openbaar vervoer`, `het voortgezet onderwijs`, `het Centraal Station`. Learn them as wholes.\n\n### The adjective as a noun\n\nWith de or een and the -e, an adjective can stand for a **person**: `de zieke` (the sick person), `de jarige` (the person whose birthday it is), `een blinde`. The plural takes **-n**: `de zieken`, `de rijken`, `de ouderen`.\n\nWith **het** it stands for a thing or an idea: `het goede`, `het ergste`, `het leuke is dat …`.\n\n> Vroeger konden alleen de rijken suiker betalen. — In the past only the rich could afford sugar.\n> Het leuke van deze traditie is dat iedereen iets meeneemt. — The nice thing about this tradition is that everyone brings something.\n\n### allerbeste: the strongest superlative\n\n`aller-` in front of a superlative makes it even stronger, written as one word: `de allerbeste`, `het allerleukste`. The comparative follows the normal rule before a noun: `een grotere taart`, `een groter feest`.\n\n> Mijn oma bakte de allerlekkerste appeltaart. — My grandmother baked the most delicious apple pie of all.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *iets lekker or *iets lekkere → `iets lekkers`.\n- *iets friss → `iets fris`.\n- *de houtene plank → `de houten plank`; *het gebakkene ei → `het gebakken ei`.\n- *de gekookt aardappelen → `de gekookte aardappelen`: gekookt is a weak participle.\n- *de rijkes or *de rijke (for more people) → `de rijken`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian also uses an adjective as a noun: `хворий` is `de zieke`. For \"something tasty\" Ukrainian puts the adjective in the neuter (`щось смачне`); Dutch adds -s instead: `iets lekkers`.","words":[{"nl":"smaak","art":"de","pl":"smaken","en":"taste / flavour","pos":"n"},{"nl":"traditie","art":"de","pl":"tradities","en":"tradition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gewoonte","art":"de","pl":"gewoonten / gewoontes","en":"custom / habit","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gebak","art":"het","pl":"","en":"pastries / cakes","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"kruid","art":"het","pl":"kruiden","en":"herb","pos":"n"},{"nl":"ingrediënt","art":"het","pl":"ingrediënten","en":"ingredient","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bijgerecht","art":"het","pl":"bijgerechten","en":"side dish","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bestek","art":"het","pl":"","en":"cutlery","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"jarige","art":"de","pl":"jarigen","en":"person whose birthday it is","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kruidig","en":"spicy / well-seasoned","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"pittig","en":"hot (spicy) / lively","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"zelfgemaakt","en":"homemade","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"overheerlijk","en":"delicious","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"stoven","past":"stoofde","perf":"heeft gestoofd","en":"to stew","pos":"v"},{"nl":"braden","past":"braadde","perf":"heeft gebraden","en":"to roast / to fry (meat)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"smullen","past":"smulde","perf":"heeft gesmuld","en":"to tuck in / to enjoy one's food","pos":"v"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Heb je iets zoets voor bij de koffie?","en":"Do you have something sweet to go with the coffee?","hint":["hebben","iets","zoet","koffie"],"note":"After iets an adjective with no noun after it takes -s: iets zoets. Not *iets zoete.","alt":["Heb jij iets zoets voor bij de koffie?"]},{"nl":"Er stond niets bijzonders op het menu, maar alles smaakte goed.","en":"There was nothing special on the menu, but everything tasted good.","hint":["staan","niets","bijzonder","menu","smaken"],"note":"Niets works like iets: niets bijzonders, with -s. Goed after smaakte stands on its own and gets nothing.","alt":["Op het menu stond niets bijzonders, maar alles smaakte goed."]},{"nl":"Bij ons op kantoor is het een gewoonte dat de jarige iets lekkers meeneemt.","en":"At our office it is a custom that the person whose birthday it is brings something tasty.","hint":["kantoor","gewoonte","jarige","iets","lekker","meenemen"],"note":"De jarige is an adjective used as a noun for a person, with -e. Iets lekkers has the -s, because no noun follows.","alt":["Het is bij ons op kantoor een gewoonte dat de jarige iets lekkers meeneemt."]},{"nl":"Na al dat zoete gebak heb ik zin in iets fris.","en":"After all those sweet pastries I fancy something fresh.","hint":["na","zoet","gebak","zin hebben in","iets","fris"],"note":"Fris already ends in s, so it gets no second s: iets fris. Dat zoete gebak follows the normal rule: after dat the adjective takes -e.","alt":["Ik heb na al dat zoete gebak zin in iets fris."]},{"nl":"We aten gebraden kip met gekookte aardappelen.","en":"We ate roast chicken with boiled potatoes.","hint":["eten","braden","kip","koken","aardappel"],"note":"Gebraden is a participle in -en (braden, braadde, gebraden), so it never takes -e. Gekookt is weak (koken, kookte), so it does: gekookte."},{"nl":"Het zilveren bestek gebruiken we alleen met kerst.","en":"We only use the silver cutlery at Christmas.","hint":["zilveren","bestek","gebruiken","alleen","kerst"],"note":"Zilveren ends in -en, so even after het there is no -e: het zilveren bestek, never *zilverene.","alt":["We gebruiken het zilveren bestek alleen met kerst."]},{"nl":"Het leuke van deze traditie is dat iedereen iets zelfgemaakts meeneemt.","en":"The nice thing about this tradition is that everyone brings something homemade.","hint":["leuk","traditie","iedereen","zelfgemaakt","meenemen"],"note":"Het leuke is an adjective used as a noun: the nice thing. Zelfgemaakt after iets takes -s like any adjective: iets zelfgemaakts.","alt":["Het leuke aan deze traditie is dat iedereen iets zelfgemaakts meeneemt."]},{"nl":"Vroeger konden alleen de rijken suiker betalen.","en":"In the past only the rich could afford sugar.","hint":["vroeger","kunnen","alleen","rijk","suiker","betalen"],"note":"De rijken means 'the rich people'. An adjective used as a noun for more people takes -n, not -s.","alt":["Alleen de rijken konden vroeger suiker betalen."]},{"nl":"Mijn oma bakte de allerlekkerste appeltaart van het dorp.","en":"My grandmother baked the most delicious apple pie in the village.","hint":["oma","bakken","lekker","appeltaart","dorp"],"note":"Aller- makes the superlative even stronger and is written together with it: allerlekkerste, with -e after de.","alt":["Mijn oma bakte de allerlekkerste appeltaart in het dorp."]},{"nl":"De kinderen smulden van de gestoofde peren.","en":"The children tucked into the stewed pears.","hint":["kind","smullen","stoven","peer"],"note":"Stoven is a weak verb (stoofde), so the participle gestoofd ends in d and takes -e before the noun: de gestoofde peren."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Heb je iets ___ voor de jarige gekocht?","options":["leuk","leuke","leuks"],"correct":2,"en":"Have you bought something nice for the person whose birthday it is?","note":"After iets an adjective with no noun behind it takes -s: iets leuks. The -e is only for an adjective before a noun."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De kaas ligt op een ___ plank.","options":["houtene","houten","houts"],"correct":1,"en":"The cheese is lying on a wooden board.","note":"Houten ends in -en, like most adjectives for materials, and never takes -e: een houten plank, de houten plank."},{"type":"choose","nl":"We aten de ___ aardappelen met jus.","options":["gekookt","gekookte","gekookten"],"correct":1,"en":"We ate the boiled potatoes with gravy.","note":"Koken is a weak verb, so the participle gekookt ends in t and follows the normal rule: after de, -e. Only participles in -en stay unchanged."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Wat is het ___ gerecht dat je ooit hebt gegeten?","options":["allerlekkerst","allerlekkerste","aller lekkerste"],"correct":1,"en":"What is the most delicious dish you have ever eaten?","note":"Aller- is written together with the superlative, and before a noun after het the superlative takes -e: het allerlekkerste gerecht."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De smaak was goed, maar er stond niets ___ (nieuw) op het menu.","answers":["nieuws"],"en":"The taste was good, but there was nothing new on the menu.","note":"After niets the adjective takes -s when no noun follows: niets nieuws."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (bakken) ei ligt al op je bord.","answers":["gebakken"],"en":"The fried egg is already on your plate.","note":"The participle of bakken is gebakken (the past is regular: bakte). It ends in -en and never takes -e, not even after het."},{"type":"gap","nl":"In de keuken lag het ___ (snijden) brood al klaar.","answers":["gesneden"],"en":"The sliced bread was already waiting in the kitchen.","note":"Snijden is a strong verb (sneed, gesneden). A participle in -en stays the same before the noun: het gesneden brood."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ziekenhuis brengt de ___ (ziek) drie keer per dag een maaltijd.","answers":["zieken"],"en":"The hospital brings the sick a meal three times a day.","note":"Ziek used as a noun for people: de zieke for one, de zieken for more. The plural takes -n, not -s."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Het ___ (erg) van een feest is de afwas.","answers":["ergste"],"en":"The worst thing about a party is the washing-up.","note":"Het + superlative + -e is used as a noun: het ergste, 'the worst thing'. Compare het leuke, het goede."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik heb vanavond zin in iets ___ (kruidig).","answers":["kruidigs"],"en":"Tonight I fancy something spicy.","note":"Iets + adjective without a noun: the adjective takes -s, iets kruidigs."},{"type":"order","lead":"Als bijgerecht","words":["we","iets","serveren","eenvoudigs"],"answers":["Als bijgerecht serveren we iets eenvoudigs."],"en":"As a side dish we serve something simple.","note":"The verb stays second after Als bijgerecht. The adjective comes after iets and takes -s: iets eenvoudigs."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use iets instead of een … gerecht.","from":"Ik maak een lekker gerecht.","answers":["Ik maak iets lekkers."],"en":"I am making something tasty.","note":"Without the noun, lekker follows iets and takes -s: iets lekkers."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Use niets instead of geen … ingrediënten.","from":"Je hebt geen bijzondere ingrediënten nodig.","answers":["Je hebt niets bijzonders nodig."],"en":"You do not need anything special.","note":"With the noun the adjective takes -e: bijzondere ingrediënten. After niets, with no noun, it takes -s: niets bijzonders."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Die kruiden geven de soep iets pittig.","answers":["Die kruiden geven de soep iets pittigs."],"en":"Those herbs give the soup something spicy.","note":"After iets an adjective without a noun takes -s: iets pittigs."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Je moeder heeft iets overheerlijk meegebracht.","answers":["Je moeder heeft iets overheerlijks meegebracht."],"en":"Your mother has brought something delicious.","note":"Overheerlijk after iets, with no noun following, takes -s: iets overheerlijks."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ze serveerde de gestoofde peren in een zilverene schaal.","answers":["Ze serveerde de gestoofde peren in een zilveren schaal."],"en":"She served the stewed pears in a silver dish.","note":"Zilveren ends in -en and never takes -e. Gestoofde is right: a weak participle follows the normal rule."}]},
  {"id":"b1-15","level":"B1","icon":"🤝","title":"Neighbours and community","grammar":"Indefinite words: iemand, alles, elk, sommige, men","summary":"iemand/niemand, iets/niets, ergens/nergens, ooit/nooit: one negation only. elk huis, elke straat. de meeste with de. allemaal and allebei in the middle. iedereen, niemand and men take a singular verb.","explain":"Indefinite words talk about people, things or amounts without naming them: someone, everything, every, some, most. Dutch has fixed pairs and fixed places for them, and a few traps for English and Ukrainian speakers.\n\n### Someone and no one\n\n| | positive | negative |\n| person | iemand | niemand |\n| thing | iets | niets |\n| place | ergens | nergens |\n| time | ooit | nooit |\n\nEnglish *anyone, anything, anywhere, ever* in a question is the positive word: `Heeft iemand…?` In a negative sentence Dutch uses **one** negative word, and nothing more.\n\n> Weet iemand of de bijeenkomst doorgaat? — Does anyone know whether the meeting is going ahead?\n> Niemand heeft ooit over overlast geklaagd. — Nobody has ever complained about nuisance.\n> Ik kon nergens een parkeerplek vinden. — I could not find a parking space anywhere.\n\n### All and every\n\n- `alles` stands alone: `Alles is klaar.`\n- `alle` goes with a plural or uncountable noun: `alle buren`, `alle hulp`. Before an article or dit/dat/die: `al het werk`, `al die mensen`.\n- `allemaal` (all of them) stands in the **middle**, after the conjugated verb and the subject: `We gaan allemaal mee.` `Ze zijn allemaal gekomen.` In formal writing, for people: `allen` (`Ik dank u allen`).\n- `elk` and `ieder` follow the -e rule of de and het: `elke straat`, `iedere straat`, but `elk huis`, `ieder huis`.\n- `iedereen` is everyone.\n\n> Elke bewoner krijgt een brief van het bestuur. — Every resident gets a letter from the board.\n> De bewoners hebben allemaal geld ingezameld. — The residents all collected money.\n\n### Part of a group\n\n- `sommige` + plural: some, but not all: `Sommige buren helpen altijd.`\n- `enkele`, `een paar`: a few. `verschillende`: several. `meerdere`: more than one.\n- `de meeste` + plural: most, **always with de**: `de meeste mensen`. (With an uncountable het-word: `het meeste geld`.)\n- `veel`, `weinig`: many/much, few/little.\n\n> De meeste vrijwilligers zijn met pensioen. — Most volunteers are retired.\n\n### Two: beide and allebei\n\n`beide` stands before a noun: `beide buren`. `allebei` stands in the middle, like allemaal: `Ze komen allebei.` Never *allebei buren.\n\n> Mijn buurvrouw en ik doen allebei vrijwilligerswerk. — My neighbour and I both do volunteer work.\n\n### People in general: je, men, ze\n\n- `je` is the normal word in speech and most writing: `Als je je aanmeldt, krijg je een gesprek.`\n- `men` is formal and written: `Vroeger hielp men elkaar meer.`\n- `ze` means *they*, the people in charge: `Ze bouwen hier een nieuwe school.`\n\n### wat\n\nIn speech `wat` means \"some\" before an uncountable noun (`wat koffie`) and \"something\" on its own (`Wil je wat drinken?`).\n\n### The verb\n\n`iedereen`, `niemand`, `iemand`, `alles`, `iets` and `men` take a **singular** verb: `iedereen is`, `niemand heeft`. With a plural noun the verb is plural: `de meeste mensen zijn`, `sommige buren helpen`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Niemand heeft niets gezegd → `Niemand heeft iets gezegd`.\n- *Ik heb niet iemand gezien (for \"I did not see anyone\") → `Ik heb niemand gezien`.\n- *Iedereen zijn welkom → `Iedereen is welkom`.\n- *Meeste buren → `De meeste buren`.\n- *elke huis → `elk huis`.\n- *Alle is klaar → `Alles is klaar`.\n- For two people: `allebei`, not allemaal.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian stacks the negatives: `Ніхто нічого не сказав`. Dutch allows only one: `Niemand heeft iets gezegd`. Translate the first negative word and turn the others into iets, iemand, ergens or ooit.","words":[{"nl":"vrijwilliger","art":"de","pl":"vrijwilligers","en":"volunteer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vrijwilligerswerk","art":"het","pl":"","en":"volunteer work","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"bewoner","art":"de","pl":"bewoners","en":"resident","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bijeenkomst","art":"de","pl":"bijeenkomsten","en":"meeting / gathering","pos":"n"},{"nl":"initiatief","art":"het","pl":"initiatieven","en":"initiative","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bestuur","art":"het","pl":"besturen","en":"board (of a club) / committee","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voedselbank","art":"de","pl":"voedselbanken","en":"food bank","pos":"n"},{"nl":"buurtapp","art":"de","pl":"buurtapps","en":"neighbourhood chat group","pos":"n"},{"nl":"overlast","art":"de","pl":"","en":"nuisance / disturbance","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"zich inzetten voor","past":"zette zich in","perf":"heeft zich ingezet","en":"to work hard for / to commit oneself to","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"ondersteunen","past":"ondersteunde","perf":"heeft ondersteund","en":"to support","pos":"v"},{"nl":"inzamelen","past":"zamelde in","perf":"heeft ingezameld","en":"to collect (money, goods)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"zich aanmelden","past":"meldde zich aan","perf":"heeft zich aangemeld","en":"to sign up / to register","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"behulpzaam","en":"helpful","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"betrokken","en":"involved / engaged","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Weet iemand of de bijeenkomst vanavond doorgaat?","en":"Does anyone know whether the meeting is going ahead tonight?","hint":["weten","iemand","bijeenkomst","vanavond","doorgaan"],"note":"English 'anyone' in a question is iemand in Dutch. Iemand takes a singular verb: weet."},{"nl":"Niemand heeft ooit over overlast geklaagd.","en":"Nobody has ever complained about nuisance.","hint":["niemand","ooit","overlast","klagen"],"note":"One negative word is enough: niemand. 'Ever' is ooit, not nooit, because the sentence is already negative.","alt":["Niemand heeft ooit geklaagd over overlast."]},{"nl":"Iedereen is welkom, ook bewoners die geen lid zijn.","en":"Everyone is welcome, including residents who are not members.","hint":["iedereen","welkom","ook","bewoner","lid"],"note":"Iedereen means many people, but it takes a singular verb: iedereen is, never *iedereen zijn."},{"nl":"Elke bewoner krijgt een brief van het bestuur.","en":"Every resident gets a letter from the board.","hint":["elk","bewoner","krijgen","brief","bestuur"],"note":"De bewoner is a de-word, so elke, with -e. With a het-word it would be elk: elk huis.","alt":["Iedere bewoner krijgt een brief van het bestuur."]},{"nl":"Sommige buren zetten zich elke week in voor de voedselbank.","en":"Some neighbours work hard for the food bank every week.","hint":["sommige","buur","zich inzetten voor","week","voedselbank"],"note":"Sommige means some, but not all, and goes with a plural noun; the verb is plural too: zetten.","alt":["Sommige buren zetten zich elke week voor de voedselbank in."]},{"nl":"De meeste vrijwilligers zijn met pensioen.","en":"Most volunteers are retired.","hint":["meest","vrijwilliger","zijn","pensioen"],"note":"Most + plural is de meeste, always with de. The subject is plural, so the verb is plural: zijn.","alt":["De meeste vrijwilligers zijn gepensioneerd."]},{"nl":"De bewoners hebben allemaal geld ingezameld.","en":"The residents all collected money.","hint":["bewoner","allemaal","geld","inzamelen"],"note":"Allemaal stands in the middle, directly after the conjugated verb hebben, not before the subject as English 'all the residents'.","alt":["De bewoners zamelden allemaal geld in."]},{"nl":"Mijn buurvrouw en ik doen allebei vrijwilligerswerk.","en":"My neighbour and I both do volunteer work.","hint":["buurvrouw","allebei","vrijwilligerswerk"],"note":"For two people: allebei, in the middle after the verb. Allemaal would mean more than two.","alt":["Mijn buurvrouw en ik doen beiden vrijwilligerswerk."]},{"nl":"Als je je aanmeldt als vrijwilliger, krijg je eerst een gesprek.","en":"If you sign up as a volunteer, you first get an interview.","hint":["als","zich aanmelden","vrijwilliger","krijgen","eerst","gesprek"],"note":"Je here means people in general, not one person you are talking to. This is the normal way to say it; men would be formal.","alt":["Als je je als vrijwilliger aanmeldt, krijg je eerst een gesprek."]},{"nl":"Kun je wat koffie meenemen voor de vrijwilligers?","en":"Can you bring some coffee for the volunteers?","hint":["kunnen","wat","koffie","meenemen","vrijwilliger"],"note":"Before an uncountable noun, wat means 'some' in everyday Dutch: wat koffie.","alt":["Kun jij wat koffie meenemen voor de vrijwilligers?","Kan je wat koffie meenemen voor de vrijwilligers?","Kun je voor de vrijwilligers wat koffie meenemen?","Kun jij voor de vrijwilligers wat koffie meenemen?","Kan jij wat koffie meenemen voor de vrijwilligers?","Kan je voor de vrijwilligers wat koffie meenemen?","Kan jij voor de vrijwilligers wat koffie meenemen?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Heeft ___ mijn kat gezien?","options":["niemand","iemand","iets"],"correct":1,"en":"Has anyone seen my cat?","note":"English 'anyone' in a question is iemand. Niemand would mean 'nobody', and iets is a thing, not a person."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ huis in deze straat heeft een tuin.","options":["Elke","Elk","Alle"],"correct":1,"en":"Every house in this street has a garden.","note":"Het huis is a het-word, so elk without -e. Alle needs a plural: alle huizen hebben."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Iedereen in de straat ___ erg betrokken.","options":["zijn","is","ben"],"correct":1,"en":"Everyone in the street is very involved.","note":"Iedereen takes a singular verb: iedereen is."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ bewoners ondersteunen het plan voor een nieuwe speeltuin.","options":["Meeste","Het meeste","De meeste"],"correct":2,"en":"Most residents support the plan for a new playground.","note":"Most + plural noun is always de meeste, with de. Het meeste is for an uncountable amount: het meeste geld."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn broer en ik zijn ___ lid van de tennisclub.","options":["allemaal","allebei","alle"],"correct":1,"en":"My brother and I are both members of the tennis club.","note":"Two people: allebei. Allemaal is for three or more, and alle needs a noun after it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik kon ___ (nowhere) een parkeerplek vinden.","answers":["nergens"],"en":"I could not find a parking space anywhere.","note":"Nowhere / not anywhere is nergens. The sentence needs no niet as well: one negation is enough."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heb je ___ (ever) vrijwilligerswerk gedaan?","answers":["ooit"],"en":"Have you ever done volunteer work?","note":"Ever is ooit. Nooit means never and would make it a negative question."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (elk) straat heeft een eigen buurtapp.","answers":["Elke"],"en":"Every street has its own neighbourhood chat group.","note":"De straat is a de-word, so elk takes -e: elke straat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We hebben ___ (all) hulp nodig die we kunnen krijgen.","answers":["alle"],"en":"We need all the help we can get.","note":"Alle goes with a plural or uncountable noun such as hulp. Alles stands on its own and never before a noun."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vroeger hielp ___ (one, formal) elkaar meer dan nu.","answers":["men"],"en":"In the past people helped each other more than now.","note":"Men is the formal, written word for people in general. In speech you would usually say je or mensen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["zijn","de vrijwilligers","allemaal","gekomen"],"answers":["Gisteren zijn de vrijwilligers allemaal gekomen."],"en":"Yesterday the volunteers all came.","note":"After Gisteren come the verb and the subject. Allemaal stands after the subject in the middle, and the participle goes last."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative with niemand.","from":"Iemand heeft het initiatief genomen.","answers":["Niemand heeft het initiatief genomen."],"en":"Nobody took the initiative.","note":"Niemand replaces iemand and is the only negative word. The verb stays singular: heeft."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative with nergens.","from":"Ik heb de folder ergens gezien.","answers":["Ik heb de folder nergens gezien."],"en":"I have not seen the leaflet anywhere.","note":"Ergens becomes nergens, and that is the whole negation. Do not add niet."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with de bewoners … allemaal instead of alle bewoners.","from":"Alle bewoners zijn naar de bijeenkomst gekomen.","answers":["De bewoners zijn allemaal naar de bijeenkomst gekomen."],"en":"The residents all came to the meeting.","note":"Alle stands before the noun, allemaal in the middle: after the conjugated verb zijn and before the rest."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Niemand heeft niets gezegd over de overlast.","answers":["Niemand heeft iets gezegd over de overlast.","Niemand heeft iets over de overlast gezegd."],"en":"Nobody said anything about the nuisance.","note":"Dutch has only one negation per sentence. Niemand is negative already, so 'anything' is iets."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Meeste buren zijn erg behulpzaam.","answers":["De meeste buren zijn erg behulpzaam."],"en":"Most neighbours are very helpful.","note":"Most + plural is always de meeste, with de, unlike English 'most'."}]},
  {"id":"b1-16","level":"B1","icon":"❤️","title":"Feelings and relationships","grammar":"Verbs with a fixed preposition","summary":"Many verbs take one fixed preposition: wachten op, denken aan, houden van. Learn it with the verb. A thing becomes er + preposition (Ik reken erop), a person a pronoun (op hem). Niet usually goes before the preposition.","explain":"Many Dutch verbs are tied to one preposition: `wachten op`, `denken aan`, `houden van`. The preposition has no meaning of its own here; it simply belongs to the verb, and the part that follows it is called the *prepositional object* (het voorzetselvoorwerp). Which preposition it is cannot be worked out from English or Ukrainian, so learn every such verb as a pair.\n\n### The most common ones, by preposition\n\n- **op**: wachten op, rekenen op, vertrouwen op; boos, verliefd, trots, jaloers zijn op; zich verheugen op\n- **aan**: denken aan, twijfelen aan, wennen aan, lijden aan (an illness), zich ergeren aan\n- **naar**: luisteren naar, kijken naar, verlangen naar\n- **om**: lachen om, vragen om (ask for)\n- **voor**: zorgen voor, kiezen voor, bang zijn voor, zich interesseren voor\n- **van**: houden van, genieten van\n- **in**: geloven in\n- **over** and **met**: praten over (about), praten met (with), omgaan met, zich zorgen maken over\n\n> Ze is al twee jaar verliefd op haar collega. — She has been in love with her colleague for two years.\n> Ik denk vaak aan mijn oude vrienden. — I often think of my old friends.\n\n### Not the English and not the Ukrainian preposition\n\n| Dutch | English | Ukrainian |\n| wachten op | wait for | чекати на |\n| denken aan | think of | думати про |\n| houden van | love | любити |\n| luisteren naar | listen to | слухати |\n| boos zijn op | be angry with | сердитися на |\n| lachen om | laugh at | сміятися з |\n\nSometimes the preposition changes the meaning: `denken aan` (think of, remember) and `denken over` (have an opinion on, consider); `vragen om` (ask for) and `vragen naar` (ask about); `lachen om` (laugh at) and `lachen naar` (smile at someone).\n\n### A thing: er + preposition. A person: a pronoun\n\nWhen you replace a **thing** or a whole idea, you use `er` + the preposition (unit B1 · 5): `erop`, `eraan`, `ernaar`, `erover`. For a **person**, keep the preposition and use a pronoun.\n\n> Je kunt altijd op mijn steun rekenen. — You can always count on my support.\n> Ik reken erop. — I am counting on it.\n> Ik reken op hem. — I am counting on him.\n\nWith a sub-clause the er-word comes first: `Ik reken erop dat je komt`. When other words stand in the middle, er and the preposition split: `Ik twijfel er nooit aan`, `Ik heb er nooit aan getwijfeld`. In questions about a thing, `waar … aan`, `waar … op`; about a person, `op wie`, `aan wie`.\n\n> Waar lachen jullie om? — What are you laughing about?\n> Op wie is hij jaloers? — Who is he jealous of?\n\n### Where it goes\n\nThe prepositional object stands **late** in the clause, just before the verbs at the end. In the normal order `niet` comes **before** it.\n\n> Gisteren heb ik niet op hem gewacht. — Yesterday I did not wait for him.\n\nA prepositional object with a noun may also follow the final verb: `Hoe kun je met verdriet omgaan?` and `Hoe kun je omgaan met verdriet?` are both fine. With a short pronoun like `op hem`, keep it before the verb.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The English preposition: *verliefd met → `verliefd op`; *trots van → `trots op`; *luisteren aan → `luisteren naar`.\n- Do not go by English: `houden van` (to love) needs van although English has no preposition, and `iemand vertrouwen` (to trust someone) needs none.\n- Not *op het for a thing: *Ik reken op het → `Ik reken erop`.\n- Niet before the preposition: `Ik heb niet op hem gewacht` is the normal order. `Ik heb op hem niet gewacht` is also possible, but less usual.\n\nYou already know the reflexive ones from unit A2 · 25. Adjectives and nouns with a fixed preposition come back in B2.","words":[{"nl":"vertrouwen op","past":"vertrouwde","perf":"heeft vertrouwd","en":"to rely on","pos":"v"},{"nl":"twijfelen aan","past":"twijfelde","perf":"heeft getwijfeld","en":"to doubt / to have doubts about","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verlangen naar","past":"verlangde","perf":"heeft verlangd","en":"to long for","pos":"v"},{"nl":"wennen aan","past":"wende","perf":"is gewend","en":"to get used to","pos":"v"},{"nl":"geloven in","past":"geloofde","perf":"heeft geloofd","en":"to believe in","pos":"v"},{"nl":"lachen om","past":"lachte","perf":"heeft gelachen","en":"to laugh at / to laugh about","pos":"v"},{"nl":"omgaan met","past":"ging om","perf":"is omgegaan","en":"to deal with / to get on with","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"het uitmaken","past":"maakte het uit","perf":"heeft het uitgemaakt","en":"to break up / to end a relationship","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"verliefd op","en":"in love with","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"jaloers op","en":"jealous of","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"trots op","en":"proud of","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"relatie","art":"de","pl":"relaties","en":"relationship","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verdriet","art":"het","pl":"","en":"grief / sorrow","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"steun","art":"de","pl":"","en":"support","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik denk vaak aan mijn oude vrienden.","en":"I often think of my old friends.","hint":["denken aan","vaak","oud","vriend"],"note":"Thinking of someone is denken aan, not *denken van or *denken over (that means 'to have an opinion on')."},{"nl":"Je kunt altijd op mijn steun rekenen.","en":"You can always count on my support.","hint":["kunnen","altijd","steun","rekenen op"],"note":"Rekenen op: the preposition is part of the verb. The prepositional object stands just before the infinitive at the end, or after it.","alt":["Je kunt altijd rekenen op mijn steun.","Jij kunt altijd op mijn steun rekenen.","Jij kunt altijd rekenen op mijn steun.","Op mijn steun kun je altijd rekenen.","Je kan altijd op mijn steun rekenen.","Je kan altijd rekenen op mijn steun.","Jij kan altijd op mijn steun rekenen.","Jij kan altijd rekenen op mijn steun."]},{"nl":"Ze is al twee jaar verliefd op haar collega.","en":"She has been in love with her colleague for two years.","hint":["al","jaar","verliefd op","collega"],"note":"In love with is verliefd op, never *verliefd met. Al + present tense says how long it has been going on.","alt":["Ze is al twee jaar op haar collega verliefd."]},{"nl":"Hij is jaloers op iedereen die met zijn vriendin praat.","en":"He is jealous of everyone who talks to his girlfriend.","hint":["jaloers op","iedereen","praten met","vriendin"],"note":"Two fixed prepositions: jaloers op (not *van) and praten met. English 'talk to' is praten met or praten tegen; met is the usual one.","alt":["Hij is jaloers op iedereen die praat met zijn vriendin."]},{"nl":"Ik ben eraan gewend dat hij altijd te laat komt.","en":"I am used to him always being late.","hint":["gewend","eraan","altijd","te laat","komen"],"note":"Wennen aan, gewend zijn aan. The thing you are used to is a whole sub-clause, so er + aan comes first: eraan … dat. With gewend zijn, eraan may also be left out.","alt":["Ik ben gewend dat hij altijd te laat komt."]},{"nl":"Ze verlangt naar haar familie in het buitenland.","en":"She longs for her family abroad.","hint":["verlangen naar","familie","buitenland"],"note":"Verlangen naar, with naar where English has 'for'. The prepositional object comes at the end here, because there is no second verb."},{"nl":"Waar lachen jullie om?","en":"What are you (plural) laughing about?","hint":["waar","lachen om","jullie"],"note":"Lachen om: in a question about a thing, waar goes first and om stays at the end. Waarom would mean 'why'."},{"nl":"We hebben gisteren niet op hem gewacht.","en":"We did not wait for him yesterday.","hint":["wachten op","gisteren","niet"],"note":"Niet stands before the prepositional object op hem, and op hem stands before the participle at the end.","alt":["Gisteren hebben we niet op hem gewacht.","We wachtten gisteren niet op hem.","Gisteren wachtten we niet op hem.","We hebben gisteren op hem niet gewacht.","Gisteren hebben we op hem niet gewacht."]},{"nl":"Hoe kun je het beste met verdriet omgaan?","en":"What is the best way to deal with grief?","hint":["hoe","kunnen","best","verdriet","omgaan met"],"note":"Omgaan met: after the modal kun, the infinitive omgaan stays in one piece at the end. Met verdriet may stand before it or after it.","alt":["Hoe kun je het beste omgaan met verdriet?","Hoe kan je het beste met verdriet omgaan?","Hoe kan je het beste omgaan met verdriet?","Hoe kun jij het beste met verdriet omgaan?","Hoe kun jij het beste omgaan met verdriet?","Hoe ga je het beste met verdriet om?","Hoe ga je het beste om met verdriet?","Hoe kun je het best met verdriet omgaan?","Hoe kun je het best omgaan met verdriet?"]},{"nl":"Ze heeft het uitgemaakt, omdat ze niet meer in de relatie geloofde.","en":"She broke it off because she no longer believed in the relationship.","hint":["het uitmaken","omdat","niet meer","geloven in","relatie"],"note":"Geloven in, as in English. In the sub-clause niet meer stands before in de relatie, and the verb goes to the end.","alt":["Ze heeft het uitgemaakt, omdat ze niet meer geloofde in de relatie."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik wacht al een half uur ___ mijn vriend.","options":["voor","op","naar"],"correct":1,"en":"I have been waiting for my friend for half an hour.","note":"Wachten op, never *wachten voor: English 'wait for' leads you to the wrong word."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Luister je wel ___ me?","options":["op","aan","naar"],"correct":2,"en":"Are you listening to me?","note":"Luisteren naar. English has 'to' and Ukrainian no preposition at all (слухати мене), so this one has to be learned."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik twijfel niet ___ zijn liefde.","options":["aan","op","in"],"correct":0,"en":"I do not doubt his love.","note":"Twijfelen aan: to doubt something. English 'doubt' has no preposition at all."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mijn ouders zijn trots ___ mij.","options":["van","op","over"],"correct":1,"en":"My parents are proud of me.","note":"Trots op, like jaloers op and boos op. 'Of' tempts you to van, but van is wrong here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Hij lacht altijd ___ mijn grapjes.","options":["naar","op","om"],"correct":2,"en":"He always laughs at my jokes.","note":"Lachen om: laugh at or about something funny. Lachen naar iemand means 'to smile at someone'."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik reken ___ (on it) dat je komt.","answers":["erop"],"en":"I am counting on you coming.","note":"What you count on is the sub-clause dat je komt, a thing, not a person. So rekenen op becomes erop, followed by the dat-clause."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij verlangt ___ (for it) om weer thuis te zijn.","answers":["ernaar"],"en":"He longs to be home again.","note":"Verlangen naar. The thing he longs for is the om te-part, so naar becomes ernaar."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wil je ___ (about it) praten?","answers":["erover","daarover"],"en":"Do you want to talk about it?","note":"Praten over. For 'it', a thing, Dutch uses er + over: erover (daarover, 'about that', is also right). Never *over het."},{"type":"gap","nl":"___ (of whom) is hij jaloers?","answers":["Op wie"],"en":"Who is he jealous of?","note":"Jaloers op. In a question about a person the preposition goes with wie to the front: op wie."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","niet","op hem","gewacht"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik niet op hem gewacht.","Gisteren heb ik op hem niet gewacht."],"en":"Yesterday I did not wait for him.","note":"In the normal order niet goes before the prepositional object, and op hem stands before the participle gewacht at the end. Op hem niet gewacht is also possible, but less usual."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","er","nooit","aan","getwijfeld"],"answers":["Ik heb er nooit aan getwijfeld."],"en":"I have never doubted it.","note":"With nooit in the middle, eraan splits: er stands early in the middle, aan just before the participle, and nooit between them."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace op het feest by er + preposition.","from":"Ik verheug me op het feest.","answers":["Ik verheug me erop."],"en":"I am looking forward to it.","note":"Het feest is a thing, so op het feest becomes erop. Never *op het."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace op mijn beste vriendin by a pronoun.","from":"Ik vertrouw volledig op mijn beste vriendin.","answers":["Ik vertrouw volledig op haar."],"en":"I rely completely on her.","note":"For a person you keep the preposition and use a pronoun: op haar. Erop is only for things."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Ask what he doubts: start with Waar.","from":"Hij twijfelt aan zijn relatie.","answers":["Waar twijfelt hij aan?","Waaraan twijfelt hij?"],"en":"What does he have doubts about?","note":"In a question about a thing, waar goes to the front and aan usually stays at the end. Waaraan together is more formal."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ze is verliefd met haar buurman.","answers":["Ze is verliefd op haar buurman.","Ze is op haar buurman verliefd."],"en":"She is in love with her neighbour.","note":"In love with is verliefd op. The English 'with' does not carry over."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik reken op het.","answers":["Ik reken erop."],"en":"I am counting on it.","note":"For a thing, preposition + het becomes er + preposition: erop. Op het is not Dutch here."}]},
  {"id":"b1-17","level":"B1","icon":"🚆","title":"Getting around","grammar":"Separable verbs in every form","summary":"Main clause: the prefix goes to the end (De trein komt om acht uur aan). Perfect: aangekomen. With te: aan te komen. Sub-clause and after a modal: one word (omdat hij aankomt). Stress on the verb part: never split (voorkómen).","explain":"A separable verb (*een scheidbaar werkwoord*) has a prefix such as `aan`, `in`, `uit`, `op` or `over` that sometimes stands apart from the verb. Unit A2 · 12 did the present tense; here is every form, and how to spot the verbs that only look separable.\n\n### One rule behind every form\n\nThe prefix is split off only when the verb itself stands in **second place** in a main clause (or first: a question, an order). Everywhere else the verb is written as **one word**, or with `ge` or `te` in the middle.\n\n| form | overstappen (to change trains) |\n| present, main clause | ik stap … over |\n| simple past, main clause | ik stapte … over |\n| perfect | ik ben overgestapt |\n| with te | om over te stappen |\n| sub-clause | … dat ik overstap |\n| after a modal verb | ik moet overstappen |\n\n### Main clause: present and simple past\n\nThe verb takes its normal place, and the prefix goes to the **end** of the clause, where an infinitive or participle would stand. Objects, `niet` and time words stay in front of it.\n\n> De trein komt om acht uur aan. — The train arrives at eight o'clock.\n> Hij stapte gisteren in Utrecht over. — He changed trains in Utrecht yesterday.\n\nA phrase with a preposition may also follow the prefix: `Ik stap in Utrecht over` and `Ik stap over in Utrecht` are both correct; the second is common in speech.\n\n> We zaten een uur vast in de file. — We were stuck in a jam for an hour.\n\n### The perfect: ge in the middle\n\nThe past participle is one word, with `ge` between the prefix and the verb: `aankomen → aangekomen`, `overstappen → overgestapt`, `inchecken → ingecheckt`. The auxiliary is chosen as usual: `zijn` for a change of place or state (`is overgestapt`), otherwise mostly `hebben` (`heeft ingecheckt`).\n\nWhen the verb part has the prefix `be-`, `ver-` or `ont-`, no `ge` is added: `voorbereiden → voorbereid`.\n\n> De laatste bus is uitgevallen. — The last bus has been cancelled.\n\n### With te: three words\n\n`te` goes between the prefix and the verb: three words, `over te stappen`, `uit te checken`.\n\n> Vergeet niet uit te checken. — Don't forget to check out.\n> Je hoeft in Utrecht niet over te stappen. — You don't have to change trains in Utrecht.\n\n### Sub-clauses and modal verbs: one word\n\nIn a sub-clause the verb goes to the end, and there the prefix joins it again: `… omdat de trein te laat aankomt`. After a modal verb the infinitive is one word too: `Je moet in Utrecht overstappen`.\n\nWith two verbs at the end, both orders are correct:\n\n> Ik weet niet of hij al is aangekomen. — I don't know whether he has arrived yet.\n> Ik weet niet of hij al aangekomen is. — I don't know whether he has arrived yet.\n\nYou will also see the prefix in front of the verb group (`… dat hij aan is gekomen`). That is correct too, but keep the verb together yourself.\n\n### Separable or not? Listen to the stress\n\nWith prefixes such as `over`, `door`, `onder`, `voor` and `om` the stress decides. **Stress on the prefix**: separable, with `ge` in the participle. **Stress on the verb part**: never split, and no `ge`.\n\n| | stress on the prefix | stress on the verb |\n| voorkomen | Dat komt vaak voor. (occurs) | Dat voorkomt problemen. (prevents) |\n| doorlopen | Loop maar door. (walk on) | Ze doorliep de opleiding. (went through) |\n| ondergaan | De zon gaat onder. (sets) | Hij onderging een operatie. (underwent) |\n\nThe participles: `is voorgekomen` against `heeft voorkomen`, `is doorgelopen` against `heeft doorlopen`, `is ondergegaan` against `heeft ondergaan`.\n\nNever split: `overtuigen` (to convince: heeft overtuigd), `overlijden` (to die: is overleden), `onderzoeken` (to investigate: heeft onderzocht), `voorspellen` (to predict: heeft voorspeld). `aan` is nearly always separable, but `aanvaarden` (to accept) is not: `Hij aanvaardde de baan`. On the other hand `overnemen` (to take over) does split: `Zij nam mijn dienst over`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Only the verb part stands second: *Ik overstap in Utrecht → `Ik stap in Utrecht over`.\n- `ge` in the middle, not in front: *geoverstapt → `overgestapt`.\n- `te` in the middle: *te overstappen → `over te stappen`.\n- One word in a sub-clause: *omdat de bus niet komt aan → `omdat de bus niet aankomt`.\n- No `ge` when the stress is on the verb: *heeft een ongeluk voorgekomen → `heeft een ongeluk voorkomen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nA Ukrainian prefix is fixed to its verb: `пересісти` is one word in every form. A Dutch separable prefix behaves like a separate word that rejoins the verb only at the end of the clause.","words":[{"nl":"overstappen","past":"stapte over","perf":"is overgestapt","en":"to change (trains, buses)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"inchecken","past":"checkte in","perf":"heeft ingecheckt","en":"to check in (with a travel card)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"uitchecken","past":"checkte uit","perf":"heeft uitgecheckt","en":"to check out (with a travel card)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"inhalen","past":"haalde in","perf":"heeft ingehaald","en":"to overtake / to make up (a delay)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"vastzitten","past":"zat vast","perf":"heeft vastgezeten","en":"to be stuck","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"voorkomen","past":"voorkwam","perf":"heeft voorkomen","en":"to prevent","pos":"v","tag":"stress on komen; not separable"},{"nl":"aansluiting","art":"de","pl":"aansluitingen","en":"connection (to another train or bus)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"spitsuur","art":"het","pl":"spitsuren","en":"rush hour","pos":"n"},{"nl":"file","art":"de","pl":"files","en":"traffic jam","pos":"n"},{"nl":"perron","art":"het","pl":"perrons","en":"platform (at a station)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"dienstregeling","art":"de","pl":"dienstregelingen","en":"timetable (of public transport)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"storing","art":"de","pl":"storingen","en":"fault / breakdown (technical)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"omleiding","art":"de","pl":"omleidingen","en":"diversion (of traffic)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"abonnement","art":"het","pl":"abonnementen","en":"season ticket / subscription","pos":"n"},{"nl":"machinist","art":"de","pl":"machinisten","en":"train driver","pos":"n"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"De trein uit Utrecht komt tien minuten te laat aan.","en":"The train from Utrecht is arriving ten minutes late.","hint":["trein","minuut","te laat","aankomen"],"note":"In a main clause aankomen splits: komt stands in second place and aan goes to the end of the sentence."},{"nl":"Gisteren viel de laatste trein door een storing uit.","en":"Yesterday the last train was cancelled because of a fault. (simple past)","hint":["gisteren","laatst","trein","storing","uitvallen"],"note":"The simple past splits just like the present: viel stands second, uit goes to the end. Door een storing stays in front of the prefix.","alt":["Gisteren viel de laatste trein uit door een storing.","De laatste trein viel gisteren door een storing uit.","De laatste trein viel gisteren uit door een storing."]},{"nl":"Ben je in Amersfoort overgestapt?","en":"Did you change trains in Amersfoort? (perfect)","hint":["zijn","overstappen"],"note":"The participle is one word with ge in the middle: over-ge-stapt. Overstappen is a change of place, so the auxiliary is zijn.","alt":["Ben jij in Amersfoort overgestapt?","Ben je overgestapt in Amersfoort?","Ben jij overgestapt in Amersfoort?"]},{"nl":"Vergeet niet uit te checken als je uitstapt.","en":"Don't forget to check out when you get off.","hint":["vergeten","uitchecken","als","uitstappen"],"note":"With te you get three words: uit te checken. In the sub-clause with als the verb is one word again: uitstapt.","alt":["Vergeet niet om uit te checken als je uitstapt.","Vergeet niet uit te checken wanneer je uitstapt.","Vergeet niet om uit te checken wanneer je uitstapt."]},{"nl":"Je hoeft in Utrecht niet over te stappen.","en":"You don't have to change trains in Utrecht.","hint":["hoeven","niet","overstappen"],"note":"Hoeven takes te, and te goes between the prefix and the verb: over te stappen. Niet stands before this group.","alt":["Jij hoeft in Utrecht niet over te stappen.","In Utrecht hoef je niet over te stappen.","In Utrecht hoef jij niet over te stappen.","Je hoeft niet over te stappen in Utrecht.","Jij hoeft niet over te stappen in Utrecht."]},{"nl":"Ik kom later, omdat ik in de file vastzit.","en":"I'm coming later because I'm stuck in a traffic jam.","hint":["komen","later","omdat","file","vastzitten"],"note":"Omdat sends the verb to the end of the sub-clause, and there vastzitten is one word again: vastzit, not zit vast.","alt":["Ik kom later, omdat ik vastzit in de file."]},{"nl":"Weet je of de trein uit Zwolle al is aangekomen?","en":"Do you know whether the train from Zwolle has arrived yet?","hint":["weten","of","trein","al","aankomen"],"note":"In a sub-clause that ends in two verbs, both orders are right: is aangekomen or aangekomen is. The participle itself stays one word.","alt":["Weet jij of de trein uit Zwolle al is aangekomen?","Weet je of de trein uit Zwolle al aangekomen is?","Weet jij of de trein uit Zwolle al aangekomen is?","Weet je of de trein uit Zwolle al aan is gekomen?","Weet jij of de trein uit Zwolle al aan is gekomen?"]},{"nl":"De trein kan de vertraging niet meer inhalen, dus ik mis mijn aansluiting.","en":"The train can no longer make up the delay, so I am missing my connection.","hint":["trein","kunnen","vertraging","inhalen","dus","aansluiting"],"note":"After a modal verb the infinitive goes to the end as one word: kan … inhalen. After dus you may keep the normal order or invert (dus mis ik).","alt":["De trein kan de vertraging niet meer inhalen, dus mis ik mijn aansluiting."]},{"nl":"Zulke storingen komen in de winter vaak voor.","en":"Faults like that often occur in winter.","hint":["zulk","storing","winter","vaak","vóórkomen"],"note":"Vóórkomen (to occur) has the stress on voor, so it splits: komen … voor. Compare voorkómen (to prevent), which never splits.","alt":["In de winter komen zulke storingen vaak voor.","Zulke storingen komen vaak voor in de winter."]},{"nl":"Hoe kunnen we vertragingen op het spoor voorkomen?","en":"How can we prevent delays on the railways?","hint":["hoe","kunnen","vertraging","spoor","voorkómen"],"note":"Voorkómen (to prevent) has the stress on komen, so it is one word in every form: voorkomt, voorkwam, heeft voorkomen.","alt":["Hoe kunnen we vertraging op het spoor voorkomen?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Door een omleiding ___ de bus vandaag later aan.","options":["aankomt","komt","kom"],"correct":1,"en":"Because of a diversion the bus is arriving later today.","note":"In a main clause only komt stands in second place; aan is already at the end. Aankomt as one word belongs in a sub-clause."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Je hoeft hier niet ___.","options":["uitstappen","te uitstappen","uit te stappen"],"correct":2,"en":"You don't have to get off here.","note":"Hoeven needs te, and with a separable verb te goes between the prefix and the verb: uit te stappen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Zo'n storing ___ hier bijna nooit voor.","options":["voorkomt","komt","komen"],"correct":1,"en":"A fault like that hardly ever occurs here.","note":"Vóórkomen (to occur) splits, and voor already stands at the end, so only komt is left. Zo'n storing is singular."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De machinist heeft een ongeluk ___.","options":["voorgekomen","voorkomen","gevoorkomen"],"correct":1,"en":"The train driver prevented an accident.","note":"Voorkómen (to prevent) has the stress on komen, so it is not separable and its participle has no ge: heeft voorkomen. Voorgekomen belongs to vóórkomen (to occur)."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wij zijn in Zwolle ___ (uitstappen).","answers":["uitgestapt"],"en":"We got off in Zwolle.","note":"The participle is one word with ge between the prefix and the verb: uit-ge-stapt. Not *geuitstapt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heb je met je abonnement al ___ (inchecken)?","answers":["ingecheckt"],"en":"Have you already checked in with your season ticket?","note":"Ge goes in the middle: in-ge-checkt. The stem check ends in k, which is in 't kofschip, so the participle ends in t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ben vergeten ___ (uitchecken).","answers":["uit te checken","om uit te checken"],"en":"I forgot to check out.","note":"With te a separable verb becomes three words: uit te checken. Never *te uitchecken."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Kijk in de dienstregeling hoe laat de bus ___ (aankomen).","answers":["aankomt"],"en":"Look in the timetable to see what time the bus arrives.","note":"Hoe laat starts a sub-clause, so the verb goes to the end, and there the prefix joins it again: aankomt."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["stap","ik","in Utrecht","over"],"answers":["Morgen stap ik in Utrecht over.","Morgen stap ik over in Utrecht."],"en":"Tomorrow I am changing trains in Utrecht.","note":"Stap stands second, after Morgen, and ik follows. Over normally goes to the end; a phrase with a preposition such as in Utrecht may also come after it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Tijdens het spitsuur is het lastig","words":["om","in","te","stappen"],"answers":["Tijdens het spitsuur is het lastig om in te stappen."],"en":"During the rush hour it is difficult to get on.","note":"Om comes first, then the separable verb with te in the middle: in te stappen."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik weet niet","words":["of","de trein","al","is","aangekomen"],"answers":["Ik weet niet of de trein al is aangekomen.","Ik weet niet of de trein al aangekomen is."],"en":"I don't know whether the train has arrived yet.","note":"Of starts a sub-clause, so both verbs go to the end, in either order. Aangekomen stays one word."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"De sneltrein valt uit.","answers":["De sneltrein is uitgevallen."],"en":"The express train has been cancelled.","note":"Uitvallen is a change of state, so it takes zijn. The participle is one word: uit-ge-vallen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Start with Ik denk dat.","from":"We stappen in Amersfoort over.","answers":["Ik denk dat we in Amersfoort overstappen.","Ik denk dat we overstappen in Amersfoort."],"en":"I think that we change trains in Amersfoort.","note":"Dat sends the verb to the end of the clause, and there over and stappen join again: overstappen."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it an instruction with moeten: start with Je moet.","from":"Je checkt bij het perron in.","answers":["Je moet bij het perron inchecken.","Je moet inchecken bij het perron."],"en":"You have to check in at the platform.","note":"Moet takes the second place, and the infinitive goes to the end as one word: inchecken."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik overstap altijd in Utrecht.","answers":["Ik stap altijd in Utrecht over.","Ik stap altijd over in Utrecht.","In Utrecht stap ik altijd over.","Ik stap in Utrecht altijd over."],"en":"I always change trains in Utrecht.","note":"In a main clause only the verb part stands second; the prefix over goes to the end (or before in Utrecht)."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik hoop dat de bus vandaag niet valt uit.","answers":["Ik hoop dat de bus vandaag niet uitvalt."],"en":"I hope that the bus is not cancelled today.","note":"In a sub-clause with dat the verb goes to the end as one word: uitvalt. The prefix only splits off when the verb stands second."}]},
  {"id":"b1-18","level":"B1","icon":"🥗","title":"Healthy living","grammar":"Linking with adverbs: daarom, toch, bovendien, anders","summary":"Daarom, daardoor, toch, bovendien, daarna, anders and similar words are adverbs, not conjunctions. In first place they are the first element, so the verb follows at once: Daarom ga ik. They can also stand after the verb: Ik ga daarom.","explain":"Many words that link two sentences are not conjunctions like `en`, `maar` and `want`, but **adverbs** (*bijwoorden*): `daarom`, `toch`, `bovendien`, `anders`. That makes a big difference for word order. A conjunction stands outside the sentence; an adverb is part of it and counts as an element.\n\n### In first place: the verb comes next\n\nWhen the adverb starts the sentence, it is element number one, so the verb comes second and the subject follows the verb (inversion, unit A2 · 3).\n\n> Ik wil afvallen. Daarom eet ik minder suiker. — I want to lose weight. That's why I eat less sugar.\n> Het regende. Toch ging ze hardlopen. — It was raining. Still, she went running.\n\nThe adverb can also stand in the middle, after the verb and the subject. Then the sentence starts in the normal way:\n\n> Ik eet daarom minder suiker. — That's why I eat less sugar.\n\nA comma instead of a full stop changes nothing: `Ik wil afvallen, daarom eet ik minder suiker`.\n\n### The words by meaning\n\n- **Reason and result**: `daarom` (that's why: usually a reason or a choice, but also used for a cause), `daardoor` (as a result: a cause, not a choice), `dus` (so), `dan ook` (so, as you would expect; in the middle, never first: `Ze rookt niet en is dan ook zelden ziek`).\n- **Contrast**: `toch` (still, yet), `echter` (however; written Dutch, normally after the verb or after the first element: `Dat is echter niet genoeg`), `daarentegen` (on the other hand: it contrasts two things), `niettemin` (nevertheless; formal).\n- **Adding**: `bovendien` (what's more), `ook` (also), `verder` (furthermore), `daarnaast` (in addition), `trouwens` (by the way, besides).\n- **Order in time**: `eerst`, `dan`, `daarna`, `vervolgens` (next), `ten slotte` (finally), `intussen` (meanwhile), `tegelijkertijd` (at the same time).\n- **Condition**: `anders` (otherwise).\n\n> Hij rookt niet meer en daardoor slaapt hij beter. — He no longer smokes and as a result he sleeps better.\n> Eerst warm je op, daarna loop je hard. — First you warm up, then you run.\n> Neem een jas mee, anders word je ziek. — Take a coat, otherwise you'll get ill.\n\n`En` and `maar` do not count, but an adverb after them does: `… en daardoor slaapt hij beter`, `… maar toch ging ze`.\n\n`dus` is special: it can be an adverb (`dus blijf ik thuis`) or work like a conjunction with the normal order (`dus ik blijf thuis`). Both are correct.\n\n### Three ways to give a reason\n\n| word | kind | order after it |\n| want | conjunction | normal: want ik ben moe |\n| omdat | starts a sub-clause | verb at the end: omdat ik moe ben |\n| daarom | adverb | verb first: daarom ga ik |\n\n`Want` and `omdat` introduce the **reason**; `daarom` introduces the **result**: `Ik ben moe, want ik heb slecht geslapen`, but `Ik heb slecht geslapen, daarom ben ik moe`.\n\n### hoewel, maar, toch\n\nOne contrast, three patterns:\n\n> Hoewel ik moe ben, ga ik sporten. — Although I am tired, I am going to exercise.\n> Ik ben moe, maar ik ga sporten. — I am tired, but I am going to exercise.\n> Ik ben moe. Toch ga ik sporten. — I am tired. Still, I am going to exercise.\n\n`Hoewel` starts a sub-clause, so its verb goes to the end; `maar` keeps the normal order; `toch` is an adverb and causes inversion. `Maar` and `toch` often go together: `maar ik ga toch`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No subject between the adverb and the verb: *Daarom ik ga → `Daarom ga ik`.\n- `omdat` sends the verb to the end: *omdat ik ben moe → `omdat ik moe ben`.\n- `want` keeps the normal order: *want ben ik moe → `want ik ben moe`.\n- `hoewel` is not `toch`: *Hoewel ga ik sporten → `Toch ga ik sporten`.\n- Inversion with jij: `anders word je ziek`, without t (unit A2 · 1).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nAfter `тому` (that's why) or `проте` (however) Ukrainian keeps whatever order it likes: `Тому я йду`. In Dutch these words take the first place and push the subject behind the verb: `Daarom ga ik`.\n\nThe formal ones (`echter`, `daarentegen`, `niettemin`) come back in unit B2 · 7.","words":[{"nl":"gezondheid","art":"de","pl":"","en":"health","pos":"n"},{"nl":"leefstijl","art":"de","pl":"leefstijlen","en":"lifestyle","pos":"n"},{"nl":"voeding","art":"de","pl":"","en":"food / nutrition","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gewicht","art":"het","pl":"gewichten","en":"weight","pos":"n"},{"nl":"conditie","art":"de","pl":"condities","en":"fitness (physical condition)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vet","art":"het","pl":"vetten","en":"fat","pos":"n"},{"nl":"eiwit","art":"het","pl":"eiwitten","en":"protein","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zorgverzekering","art":"de","pl":"zorgverzekeringen","en":"health insurance","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afvallen","past":"viel af","perf":"is afgevallen","en":"to lose weight","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"bewegen","past":"bewoog","perf":"heeft bewogen","en":"to move / to exercise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"roken","past":"rookte","perf":"heeft gerookt","en":"to smoke","pos":"v"},{"nl":"vermijden","past":"vermeed","perf":"heeft vermeden","en":"to avoid","pos":"v"},{"nl":"volhouden","past":"hield vol","perf":"heeft volgehouden","en":"to keep it up / to keep going","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"voedzaam","en":"nutritious","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"matig","en":"moderate / in moderation","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik wil afvallen, daarom vermijd ik suiker en vet.","en":"I want to lose weight, that's why I avoid sugar and fat.","hint":["willen","afvallen","daarom","vermijden","suiker","vet"],"note":"Daarom is the first element of the second sentence, so the verb vermijd comes straight after it and ik follows.","alt":["Ik wil afvallen, ik vermijd daarom suiker en vet."]},{"nl":"Hij is gestopt met roken en daardoor is zijn conditie veel beter.","en":"He has stopped smoking, and as a result his fitness is much better.","hint":["stoppen","roken","daardoor","conditie","beter"],"note":"En does not count, daardoor does: it is element one, so is comes next and then zijn conditie. Daardoor gives a result that nobody chose.","alt":["Hij is gestopt met roken en zijn conditie is daardoor veel beter.","Hij is met roken gestopt en daardoor is zijn conditie veel beter.","Hij is met roken gestopt en zijn conditie is daardoor veel beter."]},{"nl":"Bewegen is belangrijk; goede voeding is echter net zo belangrijk.","en":"Exercise is important; good nutrition, however, is just as important.","hint":["bewegen","belangrijk","goed","voeding","echter"],"note":"Echter usually stands after the verb, in the middle of the sentence. It belongs to written and careful Dutch; in speech you would say maar.","alt":["Bewegen is belangrijk; goede voeding echter is net zo belangrijk.","Bewegen is belangrijk; goede voeding is echter even belangrijk."]},{"nl":"Groente is voedzaam, patat daarentegen bevat veel vet.","en":"Vegetables are nutritious; chips, on the other hand, contain a lot of fat.","hint":["groente","voedzaam","patat","daarentegen","bevatten","vet"],"note":"Daarentegen often follows the thing it contrasts: patat daarentegen counts as one element, and bevat still comes second.","alt":["Groente is voedzaam, patat bevat daarentegen veel vet.","Groente is voedzaam, daarentegen bevat patat veel vet."]},{"nl":"Het regende hard, toch fietste ze naar de sportschool.","en":"It was raining hard, yet she still cycled to the gym.","hint":["regenen","hard","toch","fietsen","sportschool"],"note":"Toch is an adverb, so in first place it is followed by the verb: toch fietste ze, never *toch ze fietste.","alt":["Het regende hard, toch fietste zij naar de sportschool.","Het regende hard, maar ze fietste toch naar de sportschool.","Het regende hard, maar zij fietste toch naar de sportschool.","Het regende hard, maar toch fietste ze naar de sportschool.","Het regende hard, maar toch fietste zij naar de sportschool.","Het regende hard, ze fietste toch naar de sportschool.","Het regende hard, zij fietste toch naar de sportschool."]},{"nl":"Hoewel ik gezond eet, val ik niet af.","en":"Although I eat healthily, I am not losing weight.","hint":["hoewel","gezond","eten","afvallen"],"note":"Hoewel starts a sub-clause, so eet goes to its end. That whole sub-clause is element one, so val comes next.","alt":["Ik val niet af, hoewel ik gezond eet."]},{"nl":"Wandelen is goed voor je hart en bovendien kost het niets.","en":"Walking is good for your heart, and what's more, it costs nothing.","hint":["wandelen","goed","hart","bovendien","kosten"],"note":"After en, bovendien is the first element, so kost comes before het.","alt":["Wandelen is goed voor je hart en het kost bovendien niets."]},{"nl":"Eerst warm je tien minuten op en vervolgens loop je een half uur hard.","en":"First you warm up for ten minutes, and next you run for half an hour.","hint":["eerst","opwarmen","minuut","vervolgens","hardlopen","half uur"],"note":"Eerst and vervolgens both take the first place, so each time the verb comes before je. Je directly after the verb: warm, loop, without t.","alt":["Eerst warm je tien minuten op en daarna loop je een half uur hard.","Eerst warm je tien minuten op en dan loop je een half uur hard."]},{"nl":"Je moet het elke dag volhouden, anders heeft het geen zin.","en":"You have to keep it up every day, otherwise there is no point.","hint":["moeten","elke dag","volhouden","anders","zin"],"note":"Anders is an adverb: it takes the first place of its sentence, and the verb heeft comes before the subject het.","alt":["Je moet het elke dag volhouden, anders heeft het geen nut.","Jij moet het elke dag volhouden, anders heeft het geen zin."]},{"nl":"Ik heb mijn leefstijl veranderd, omdat mijn gewicht te hoog was.","en":"I changed my lifestyle because my weight was too high.","hint":["leefstijl","veranderen","omdat","gewicht","hoog"],"note":"Omdat is not an adverb but a conjunction that starts a sub-clause: the verb was goes to the end. With daarom the result would come second: Mijn gewicht was te hoog, daarom heb ik …","alt":["Omdat mijn gewicht te hoog was, heb ik mijn leefstijl veranderd.","Ik veranderde mijn leefstijl, omdat mijn gewicht te hoog was.","Omdat mijn gewicht te hoog was, veranderde ik mijn leefstijl."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik slaap slecht. Daarom ___ 's avonds geen koffie meer.","options":["ik drink","drink ik","drinkt ik"],"correct":1,"en":"I sleep badly. That's why I no longer drink coffee in the evening.","note":"Daarom is the first element, so the verb comes second and the subject follows it. The ik-form stays drink: never drinkt ik."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik eet veel groente, ___ ik wil gezonder leven.","options":["daarom","want","omdat"],"correct":1,"en":"I eat a lot of vegetables, because I want to live more healthily.","note":"Ik wil has the normal order, and that fits only want. After daarom the verb would come first (daarom wil ik), and omdat needs the verb at the end (omdat ik gezonder wil leven)."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ben moe. ___ ga ik vanavond naar de sportschool.","options":["Hoewel","Toch","Maar"],"correct":1,"en":"I am tired. Still, I am going to the gym tonight.","note":"Ga comes before ik, so the first word must be an adverb: toch. After maar the order would be normal (maar ik ga), and hoewel starts a sub-clause."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Gebruik zout matig, ___ krijg je hoge bloeddruk.","options":["want","daarom","anders","dus"],"correct":2,"en":"Use salt in moderation, otherwise you'll get high blood pressure.","note":"Anders gives what happens if you do not follow the advice. Want would need normal order, and daarom and dus give a result, which makes no sense here."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik wil fit blijven. Daarom ___ (bewegen) ik elke dag een uur.","answers":["beweeg"],"en":"I want to stay fit. That's why I exercise for an hour every day.","note":"After daarom the verb comes first and ik follows. Ik takes the stem: beweeg, with a double e because the syllable is open in bewegen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij denkt aan zijn gezondheid en ___ (vermijden) daarom suiker.","answers":["vermijdt"],"en":"He thinks about his health and that is why he avoids sugar.","note":"Here daarom stands in the middle, so the order is normal and hij (left out after en) is the subject: stem vermijd + t = vermijdt."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Eerst rek je je spieren, daarna ___ (gaan) je hardlopen.","answers":["ga"],"en":"First you stretch your muscles, then you go running.","note":"Daarna is the first element, so the verb comes before je, and with je directly after it the verb loses its t: ga je."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Je moet stoppen met roken, anders ___ (worden) je conditie steeds slechter.","answers":["wordt"],"en":"You must stop smoking, otherwise your fitness will keep getting worse.","note":"After anders the verb comes first. The subject is je conditie (your fitness), not je, so the t stays: wordt je conditie."},{"type":"order","lead":"Bovendien","words":["is","mijn zorgverzekering","duurder","geworden"],"answers":["Bovendien is mijn zorgverzekering duurder geworden."],"en":"What's more, my health insurance has become more expensive.","note":"Bovendien is the first element, so is comes second and mijn zorgverzekering follows. The participle geworden goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mijn vader rookt niet meer","words":["en","daardoor","slaapt","hij","beter"],"answers":["Mijn vader rookt niet meer en daardoor slaapt hij beter.","Mijn vader rookt niet meer en hij slaapt daardoor beter."],"en":"My father no longer smokes and as a result he sleeps better.","note":"En does not count. Daardoor can be the first element, with the verb before hij, or stand after the verb in the normal order."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hoewel","words":["ik","eet","gezond","val","ik","niet af"],"answers":["Hoewel ik gezond eet, val ik niet af."],"en":"Although I eat healthily, I am not losing weight.","note":"In the sub-clause with hoewel the verb eet goes to the end. The sub-clause is the first element, so val comes next, then ik."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two with daarom at the start of the second sentence.","from":"Ik sport veel. Ik eet veel eiwit.","answers":["Ik sport veel, daarom eet ik veel eiwit."],"en":"I do a lot of sport, that's why I eat a lot of protein.","note":"Daarom becomes the first element of the second sentence, so eet comes before ik."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Give the reason with omdat instead: start with Ik eet veel eiwit.","from":"Ik sport veel, daarom eet ik veel eiwit.","answers":["Ik eet veel eiwit, omdat ik veel sport."],"en":"I eat a lot of protein because I do a lot of sport.","note":"Omdat introduces the reason, not the result, so the two parts change places. In the omdat-clause the verb sport goes to the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it without hoewel: two sentences, the second starting with Toch.","from":"Hoewel ik weinig tijd heb, sport ik drie keer per week.","answers":["Ik heb weinig tijd. Toch sport ik drie keer per week."],"en":"I have little time. Still, I do sport three times a week.","note":"Without hoewel, the first sentence has the normal order. Toch is an adverb, so the verb sport follows it directly."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Snoepen is een slechte gewoonte, daarom ik koop geen snoep meer.","answers":["Snoepen is een slechte gewoonte, daarom koop ik geen snoep meer.","Snoepen is een slechte gewoonte, ik koop daarom geen snoep meer."],"en":"Eating sweets is a bad habit, that's why I no longer buy sweets.","note":"Daarom is an adverb and counts as the first element, so the verb must follow it directly: daarom koop ik."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik neem de trap, omdat ik wil meer bewegen.","answers":["Ik neem de trap, omdat ik meer wil bewegen.","Ik neem de trap, omdat ik meer bewegen wil."],"en":"I take the stairs because I want to move more.","note":"Omdat is a conjunction that sends the verbs to the end of the clause. Meer belongs before them: omdat ik meer wil bewegen."}]},
  {"id":"b1-19","level":"B1","icon":"🎓","title":"Choosing a school","grammar":"Paired words: zowel … als, niet alleen … maar ook, hoe … hoe","summary":"zowel … als (both … and), niet alleen … maar ook, of … of, noch … noch (no extra niet). Hoe meer je oefent, hoe beter het gaat: verb at the end of the first part. Zo … dat, te … om te, oud genoeg om te.","explain":"Some links come in pairs: one word in front of the first part and one in front of the second. Most of them join words or phrases, but several also change the word order. This unit goes through them one by one, each with the order it causes.\n\n### zowel … als: both … and\n\n`zowel` stands before the first part, `als` before the second. Never *zowel … en.\n\n> Op deze school heb je zowel theoretische als praktische vakken. — At this school you have both theoretical and practical subjects.\n\nWhen the subject is `zowel A als B` and both are singular, the verb can be singular or plural, and both are correct: `Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter zit hier op school` or `… zitten hier op school`.\n\n### niet alleen … maar ook: not only … but also\n\nIn the middle of the sentence it joins two words or phrases, usually with a comma before `maar`:\n\n> Deze opleiding is niet alleen leerzaam, maar ook erg leuk. — This course is not only instructive but also great fun.\n\nWhen `Niet alleen` **starts the sentence**, it is the first element, so the verb follows it at once, as in English *not only is …*. The second part is then a normal sentence with `ook`, with or without `maar`:\n\n> Niet alleen is de school dichtbij, ze is ook goed. — Not only is the school close by, it is also good.\n\n### of … of and noch … noch\n\n`of … of` means either … or: `Je kiest of wiskunde of biologie`. Between two sentences the order stays normal: `Of je gaat studeren, of je zoekt een baan`.\n\n`noch … noch` means neither … nor. It is already negative, so there is **no** `niet` or `geen` with it. It sounds rather formal; in speech people often say `geen … en ook geen`.\n\n> Hij heeft noch een diploma noch werkervaring. — He has neither a diploma nor work experience.\n\nWrite `noch` with ch; `nog` means *still* or *yet*.\n\n### aan de ene kant … aan de andere kant\n\nThese are adverbial phrases: when one fills the first place of its sentence, the verb comes straight after it. The formal version is `enerzijds … anderzijds`.\n\n> Aan de ene kant is de school ver weg, aan de andere kant is het niveau hoog. — On the one hand the school is far away, on the other hand the level is high.\n\n### hoe … hoe: the more …, the better\n\n`hoe` + comparative, twice. The **first part is a sub-clause**: its verb goes to the end. In the **second part** the subject and verb normally follow the comparative: `hoe beter het gaat`. You will also hear `hoe beter gaat het`, and in writing `des te beter gaat het`; all three are correct.\n\n| first part | second part |\n| Hoe meer je oefent, | hoe beter het gaat. |\n| Hoe ouder hij wordt, | hoe minder hij slaapt. |\n\n> Hoe eerder je je inschrijft, hoe groter de kans op een plek. — The earlier you enrol, the bigger the chance of a place.\n\n### zo … dat, te … om te, genoeg … om te\n\n- `zo` + adjective + `dat`: a result. `dat` starts a sub-clause, so its verb goes to the end: `Het examen was zo moeilijk dat veel scholieren zakten`.\n- `te` + adjective + `om … te` + infinitive: too … to. `Het collegegeld is te hoog om te betalen`. English *to* is `om … te` here, not *voor.\n- adjective + `genoeg` + `om … te`: enough to. **genoeg comes after the adjective**: `oud genoeg`, never *genoeg oud. With a noun it usually comes first: `genoeg tijd`.\n\n> Mijn dochter is oud genoeg om zelf een school te kiezen. — My daughter is old enough to choose a school herself.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *zowel Engels en Duits → `zowel Engels als Duits`.\n- *Niet alleen de school is dichtbij, ze is ook goed → `Niet alleen is de school dichtbij, …`.\n- *Ik spreek niet noch Frans noch Duits → `Ik spreek noch Frans noch Duits`.\n- *Hoe meer oefen je → `Hoe meer je oefent`.\n- *genoeg goed → `goed genoeg`; *zo moeilijk zodat → `zo moeilijk dat`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\n`чим більше …, тим краще` is `hoe meer …, hoe beter`. Ukrainian says `ні … ні` together with `не`: `Я не говорю ні французькою, ні німецькою`. Dutch `noch … noch` stands alone: `Ik spreek noch Frans noch Duits`.","words":[{"nl":"schooladvies","art":"het","pl":"schooladviezen","en":"school recommendation (for secondary school)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"niveau","art":"het","pl":"niveaus","en":"level","pos":"n"},{"nl":"cijfer","art":"het","pl":"cijfers","en":"mark / grade","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rapport","art":"het","pl":"rapporten","en":"school report","pos":"n"},{"nl":"toelatingseis","art":"de","pl":"toelatingseisen","en":"entry requirement","pos":"n"},{"nl":"collegegeld","art":"het","pl":"","en":"tuition fee (university, college)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"scholier","art":"de","pl":"scholieren","en":"pupil (secondary school)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"zakken","past":"zakte","perf":"is gezakt","en":"to fail (an exam)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"zich inschrijven","past":"schreef me in","perf":"heeft zich ingeschreven","en":"to enrol / to register","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"afronden","past":"rondde af","perf":"heeft afgerond","en":"to complete / to finish","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"doorstromen","past":"stroomde door","perf":"is doorgestroomd","en":"to move up (to a higher level of school)","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"leerzaam","en":"instructive / educational","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"theoretisch","en":"theoretical","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter is naar het vwo doorgestroomd.","en":"Both my son and my daughter have moved up to vwo (pre-university level).","hint":["zowel","zoon","als","dochter","vwo","doorstromen"],"note":"Zowel … als joins the two subjects. With two singular parts the verb can be singular (is) or plural (zijn); both are correct.","alt":["Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter zijn naar het vwo doorgestroomd.","Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter is doorgestroomd naar het vwo.","Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter zijn doorgestroomd naar het vwo."]},{"nl":"Op deze school heb je zowel theoretische als praktische vakken.","en":"At this school you have both theoretical and practical subjects.","hint":["hebben","zowel","theoretisch","als","praktisch","vak"],"note":"Zowel stands before the first adjective, als before the second. Never zowel … en.","alt":["Je hebt op deze school zowel theoretische als praktische vakken.","Op deze school krijg je zowel theoretische als praktische vakken.","Je krijgt op deze school zowel theoretische als praktische vakken."]},{"nl":"Deze opleiding is niet alleen leerzaam, maar ook erg leuk.","en":"This course is not only instructive but also great fun.","hint":["opleiding","niet alleen","leerzaam","maar ook","leuk"],"note":"In the middle of the sentence niet alleen … maar ook joins two adjectives and changes no word order. A comma usually goes before maar."},{"nl":"Niet alleen is het collegegeld hoog, je moet ook boeken kopen.","en":"Not only is the tuition fee high, you also have to buy books.","hint":["niet alleen","collegegeld","hoog","moeten","ook","boek"],"note":"Niet alleen is the first element, so the verb is follows it at once and het collegegeld comes after the verb. The second part is a normal sentence with ook.","alt":["Niet alleen is het collegegeld hoog, maar je moet ook boeken kopen."]},{"nl":"Je kiest of wiskunde of biologie als extra vak.","en":"You choose either maths or biology as an extra subject.","hint":["kiezen","of","wiskunde","biologie","extra","vak"],"note":"Of … of means either … or. Here it joins two nouns and changes nothing in the word order.","alt":["Je kiest als extra vak of wiskunde of biologie.","Jij kiest of wiskunde of biologie als extra vak.","Als extra vak kies je of wiskunde of biologie."]},{"nl":"Hij heeft noch een diploma noch werkervaring.","en":"He has neither a diploma nor work experience.","hint":["hebben","noch","diploma","werkervaring"],"note":"Noch … noch is already negative, so there is no geen: never *geen noch. Noch is written with ch."},{"nl":"Hoe meer je oefent, hoe beter je cijfers worden.","en":"The more you practise, the better your marks get.","hint":["hoe","veel","oefenen","goed","cijfer","worden"],"note":"The first part is a sub-clause, so oefent goes to the end. In the second part the subject je cijfers and the verb follow beter.","alt":["Hoe meer je oefent, hoe beter worden je cijfers.","Hoe meer je oefent, des te beter worden je cijfers.","Hoe meer jij oefent, hoe beter je cijfers worden.","Hoe meer jij oefent, hoe beter worden je cijfers."]},{"nl":"Aan de ene kant is de school ver weg, aan de andere kant is het niveau hoog.","en":"On the one hand the school is far away, on the other hand the level is high.","hint":["aan de ene kant","school","ver weg","aan de andere kant","niveau","hoog"],"note":"Each phrase takes the first place of its sentence, so the verb is follows it and the subject comes after the verb, twice.","alt":["Aan de ene kant is de school ver weg, maar aan de andere kant is het niveau hoog."]},{"nl":"De toelatingseisen zijn zo streng dat veel scholieren een andere studie kiezen.","en":"The entry requirements are so strict that many pupils choose a different degree course.","hint":["toelatingseis","zo","streng","scholier","studie","kiezen"],"note":"Zo + adjective + dat gives the result. Dat starts a sub-clause, so kiezen goes to the end."},{"nl":"Mijn dochter is oud genoeg om zelf een school te kiezen.","en":"My daughter is old enough to choose a school herself.","hint":["dochter","oud","genoeg","zelf","school","kiezen"],"note":"Genoeg comes after the adjective: oud genoeg. Then om … te with the infinitive at the end: om zelf een school te kiezen."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze spreekt zowel Engels ___ Duits.","options":["en","als","of","maar"],"correct":1,"en":"She speaks both English and German.","note":"Zowel always goes with als. English 'both … and' tempts you to write en, but Dutch says zowel … als."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Hij heeft niet alleen een goed rapport, ___ ook een prijs voor zijn werkstuk.","options":["en","als","maar"],"correct":2,"en":"He has not only a good school report, but also a prize for his project.","note":"Niet alleen is followed by maar ook, just as 'not only' is followed by 'but also'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ze heeft noch tijd ___ geld voor een tweede studie.","options":["nog","noch","of","en"],"correct":1,"en":"She has neither time nor money for a second degree course.","note":"Noch … noch, written with ch both times. Nog (still, yet) sounds the same but is a different word."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Het examen was ___ moeilijk dat de helft van de klas is gezakt.","options":["te","zo","zoals"],"correct":1,"en":"The exam was so difficult that half the class failed.","note":"A result with a dat-clause needs zo: zo moeilijk dat. Te moeilijk goes with om … te, not with dat."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoe eerder je je ___ (inschrijven), hoe groter de kans op een plek.","answers":["inschrijft"],"en":"The earlier you enrol, the bigger the chance of a place.","note":"The first hoe-part is a sub-clause, so the verb goes to the end, and a separable verb is one word there: inschrijft, with t because je comes before it."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hoe sneller je je studie ___ (afronden), hoe minder collegegeld je betaalt.","answers":["afrondt"],"en":"The faster you finish your degree, the less tuition fee you pay.","note":"Verb at the end of the first part, in one piece: afrondt. The stem is rond (from ronden), so it is d + t."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit boek is te moeilijk ___ zelf te lezen.","answers":["om"],"en":"This book is too difficult to read on your own.","note":"Te + adjective is followed by om … te + infinitive. English 'to' is om … te here, never voor."},{"type":"order","lead":"Niet alleen","words":["is","de school","dichtbij","maar","ze is ook goed"],"answers":["Niet alleen is de school dichtbij, maar ze is ook goed."],"en":"Not only is the school close by, but it is also good.","note":"Niet alleen is the first element, so is comes before de school. After maar the sentence has the normal order."},{"type":"order","lead":"Hoe meer","words":["je","oefent","hoe","beter","het","gaat"],"answers":["Hoe meer je oefent, hoe beter het gaat.","Hoe meer je oefent, hoe beter gaat het."],"en":"The more you practise, the better it goes.","note":"In the first part the verb goes to the end: je oefent. In the second part hoe beter het gaat is the usual order; hoe beter gaat het is also correct."},{"type":"order","lead":"Met dit schooladvies kun je","words":["of","naar de havo","of","naar het vwo"],"answers":["Met dit schooladvies kun je of naar de havo of naar het vwo.","Met dit schooladvies kun je of naar het vwo of naar de havo."],"en":"With this school recommendation you can go either to havo or to vwo.","note":"Each of stands directly before one of the two choices. Their order does not matter."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two with zowel … als.","from":"Mijn zoon is geslaagd. Mijn dochter is ook geslaagd.","answers":["Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter is geslaagd.","Zowel mijn zoon als mijn dochter zijn geslaagd."],"en":"Both my son and my daughter have passed.","note":"Zowel goes before the first subject and als before the second. With two singular subjects both is and zijn are correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with noch … noch.","from":"Hij spreekt geen Frans en ook geen Duits.","answers":["Hij spreekt noch Frans noch Duits."],"en":"He speaks neither French nor German.","note":"Noch … noch replaces both geen: it is negative itself, so no geen or niet remains."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say it with te … om te.","from":"Het collegegeld is zo hoog dat ik het niet kan betalen.","answers":["Het collegegeld is te hoog om te betalen.","Het collegegeld is voor mij te hoog om te betalen.","Het collegegeld is te hoog voor mij om te betalen."],"en":"The tuition fee is too high to pay.","note":"Te hoog replaces zo hoog, and om … te replaces the dat-clause. The infinitive betalen goes last; it gets no het, because het collegegeld is already the thing paid."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Dit vak is zowel verplicht en moeilijk.","answers":["Dit vak is zowel verplicht als moeilijk."],"en":"This subject is both compulsory and difficult.","note":"Zowel goes with als, not with en."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Hoe meer leer je, hoe hoger je cijfers worden.","answers":["Hoe meer je leert, hoe hoger je cijfers worden.","Hoe meer je leert, hoe hoger worden je cijfers."],"en":"The more you study, the higher your marks get.","note":"The first part is a sub-clause: subject first, verb at the end, hoe meer je leert. With je before the verb, leert keeps its t."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn dochter is genoeg oud om haar opleiding zelf te kiezen.","answers":["Mijn dochter is oud genoeg om haar opleiding zelf te kiezen."],"en":"My daughter is old enough to choose her course herself.","note":"With an adjective genoeg comes after it: oud genoeg. Only before a noun does genoeg come first (genoeg tijd)."}]},
  {"id":"b1-20","level":"B1","icon":"🔧","title":"Getting things fixed","grammar":"Laten: letting, having something done, let's","summary":"laten + infinitive, without te: have something done (Ik laat mijn fiets repareren), let or allow (Laat hem maar praten), let's (Laten we beginnen). Perfect: heb … laten repareren, not gelaten. Alone, laten means to leave.","explain":"`laten` (liet, heeft gelaten) is one of the most useful verbs in Dutch. With an infinitive it has three meanings: **having something done** by someone else, **letting** someone do something, and **let's**. On its own it means **to leave**.\n\n### 1. Having something done\n\nYou do not do the work yourself, you have it done. Dutch cannot use `maken` or `doen` for this: only `laten`. The infinitive comes **without te** and goes to the end, as after a modal verb.\n\n> Ik laat mijn fiets repareren. — I am having my bike repaired.\n> We laten de cv-ketel elk jaar nakijken. — We have the boiler serviced every year.\n\nYou can add who does the work in two ways:\n\n- with `door`: `Ik laat de auto door de monteur nakijken`.\n- as an object, directly after `laten`: `Ik laat de monteur de auto nakijken`. The doer comes first, then the thing.\n\nWith `komen` it means to send for someone: `Ik laat een loodgieter komen`.\n\n### 2. Letting, allowing\n\n> Ik laat de kinderen buiten spelen. — I let the children play outside.\n> Laat hem maar praten. — Just let him talk.\n\nSome fixed combinations are worth learning as words: `laten zien` (to show), `laten weten` (to let someone know), `laten vallen` (to drop).\n\n> Laat het me even weten. — Just let me know.\n\n### 3. Let's and let me\n\n`Laten we` + infinitive is the Dutch *let's*. It is plural, so always `laten`, never *laat we. `Laat me` (or `laat mij`) + infinitive is *let me*.\n\n> Laten we eerst een offerte vragen. — Let's ask for a quote first.\n> Laat me even kijken. — Let me have a look.\n\n### laten on its own: to leave\n\nWithout an infinitive `laten` means to leave something somewhere, or to leave it as it is. `Laat maar!` means never mind, leave it.\n\n> Laat je jas maar hier. — Just leave your coat here.\n\n### The perfect: two infinitives\n\nWith an infinitive, the perfect of `laten` is **laten**, not *gelaten*: `Ik heb mijn fiets laten repareren`, not *Ik heb mijn fiets gelaten repareren. The second verb stays an infinitive too: not *laten gerepareerd. (Unit B1 · 26 explains this rule for all such verbs.)\n\nWithout an infinitive, the normal participle is used: `Ik heb mijn sleutels hier gelaten`.\n\n| | with an infinitive | on its own |\n| present | ik laat de fiets repareren | ik laat de fiets hier |\n| perfect | ik heb de fiets laten repareren | ik heb de fiets hier gelaten |\n\n### Word order\n\n`laten` is the conjugated verb and stands second; the infinitive closes the sentence. With a modal or in the perfect, `laten` moves to the end too, **before** the other infinitive:\n\n> Ik moet de kraan laten vervangen. — I have to have the tap replaced.\n> Hij zegt dat hij de kraan heeft laten vervangen. — He says that he has had the tap replaced.\n\nWith a separable verb you will also see the prefix in front of laten: `Ik heb de auto na laten kijken` next to `laten nakijken`. Both are correct.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- Not maken: *Ik maak mijn fiets repareren → `Ik laat mijn fiets repareren`. For *make someone do* use `laten` or `zorgen dat`.\n- No te: *Ik laat een loodgieter te komen → `Ik laat een loodgieter komen`.\n- Two infinitives in the perfect: *heb … gelaten repareren → `heb … laten repareren`.\n- `Laten we`, not *Laat we.\n\nYou may also meet `zich laten` in fixed phrases: `Dat laat zich raden` (that's easy to guess).\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian can say `Я підстригся` after a visit to the hairdresser's. Dutch must show that someone else did it: `Ik heb mijn haar laten knippen`.","words":[{"nl":"monteur","art":"de","pl":"monteurs","en":"mechanic / service engineer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"loodgieter","art":"de","pl":"loodgieters","en":"plumber","pos":"n"},{"nl":"elektricien","art":"de","pl":"elektriciens","en":"electrician","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kraan","art":"de","pl":"kranen","en":"tap","pos":"n"},{"nl":"offerte","art":"de","pl":"offertes","en":"quote / estimate (for a job)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"reparatie","art":"de","pl":"reparaties","en":"repair","pos":"n"},{"nl":"garantie","art":"de","pl":"garanties","en":"guarantee / warranty","pos":"n"},{"nl":"onderhoud","art":"het","pl":"","en":"maintenance / servicing","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vervangen","past":"verving","perf":"heeft vervangen","en":"to replace","pos":"v"},{"nl":"nakijken","past":"keek na","perf":"heeft nagekeken","en":"to check / to service","pos":"v","tag":"separable"},{"nl":"lekken","past":"lekte","perf":"heeft gelekt","en":"to leak","pos":"v"},{"nl":"haperen","past":"haperde","perf":"heeft gehaperd","en":"to play up / to not work properly","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verstopt","en":"blocked (a drain, a toilet)","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"defect","en":"broken / faulty","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik laat mijn auto elk jaar door de monteur nakijken.","en":"I have my car serviced by the mechanic every year.","hint":["laten","auto","elk jaar","monteur","nakijken"],"note":"Laat stands second and the infinitive nakijken goes to the end, without te. Door de monteur says who does the work.","alt":["Ik laat mijn auto elk jaar nakijken door de monteur.","Elk jaar laat ik mijn auto door de monteur nakijken.","Elk jaar laat ik mijn auto nakijken door de monteur.","Ik laat elk jaar mijn auto door de monteur nakijken.","Ik laat elk jaar mijn auto nakijken door de monteur."]},{"nl":"We hebben de cv-ketel laten vervangen.","en":"We have had the boiler replaced.","hint":["hebben","cv-ketel","laten","vervangen"],"note":"With an infinitive the perfect of laten is laten, not gelaten: two infinitives at the end, laten vervangen."},{"nl":"Ik laat de loodgieter de kraan vervangen.","en":"I am having the plumber replace the tap.","hint":["laten","loodgieter","kraan","vervangen"],"note":"The doer can be an object directly after laten: first the loodgieter (who does it), then de kraan (what is done).","alt":["Ik laat de kraan door de loodgieter vervangen."]},{"nl":"Vraag eerst een offerte voordat je de reparatie laat uitvoeren.","en":"Ask for a quote first before you have the repair carried out.","hint":["vragen","offerte","voordat","reparatie","laten","uitvoeren"],"note":"In the sub-clause with voordat, laat goes to the end, but still in front of its infinitive: laat uitvoeren.","alt":["Vraag eerst een offerte aan voordat je de reparatie laat uitvoeren.","Vraag eerst om een offerte voordat je de reparatie laat uitvoeren.","Vraag eerst een offerte voordat je de reparatie uit laat voeren.","Vraag eerst een offerte aan voordat je de reparatie uit laat voeren.","Vraag eerst om een offerte voordat je de reparatie uit laat voeren."]},{"nl":"De elektricien liet me zien hoe de schakelaar werkt.","en":"The electrician showed me how the switch works.","hint":["elektricien","laten zien","schakelaar","werken"],"note":"Laten zien (literally 'let see') means to show. The simple past of laten is liet, and zien goes to the end.","alt":["De elektricien liet mij zien hoe de schakelaar werkt.","De elektricien heeft me laten zien hoe de schakelaar werkt.","De elektricien heeft mij laten zien hoe de schakelaar werkt."]},{"nl":"Laten we eerst de garantie controleren.","en":"Let's check the warranty first.","hint":["laten","eerst","garantie","controleren"],"note":"Laten we + infinitive is 'let's'. We is plural, so the verb is laten, not laat.","alt":["Laten we de garantie eerst controleren."]},{"nl":"Laat me even kijken wat er defect is.","en":"Let me just have a look at what is broken.","hint":["laten","even","kijken","defect"],"note":"Laat me + infinitive is 'let me'. It is an imperative, so just the stem: laat.","alt":["Laat mij even kijken wat er defect is."]},{"nl":"Laat je fiets maar hier, dan kijk ik hem morgen na.","en":"Just leave your bike here, then I will check it tomorrow.","hint":["laten","fiets","hier","nakijken","morgen"],"note":"Without an infinitive, laten means to leave. In the second sentence nakijken splits: kijk … na.","alt":["Laat je fiets hier maar, dan kijk ik hem morgen na."]},{"nl":"Ik laat de kinderen niet in de keuken spelen zolang de kraan lekt.","en":"I don't let the children play in the kitchen as long as the tap is leaking.","hint":["laten","keuken","spelen","zolang","kraan","lekken"],"note":"Here laten means to allow. Niet stands before the place and the infinitive spelen closes the main clause.","alt":["Zolang de kraan lekt, laat ik de kinderen niet in de keuken spelen."]},{"nl":"De monteur heeft zijn gereedschap bij ons laten liggen.","en":"The mechanic left his tools at our place.","hint":["monteur","gereedschap","laten","liggen"],"note":"Laten liggen (leave lying) is a common way to say 'leave behind'. Because there is an infinitive, the perfect is laten liggen, not gelaten.","alt":["De monteur liet zijn gereedschap bij ons liggen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ mijn fiets repareren bij de fietsenmaker.","options":["maak","laat","doe"],"correct":1,"en":"I am having my bike repaired at the bike shop.","note":"Having something done by someone else is always laten + infinitive in Dutch. Maken and doen cannot be used this way."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb mijn sleutels hier ___.","options":["laten","gelaten","gelaat"],"correct":1,"en":"I have left my keys here.","note":"There is no infinitive here: laten means to leave, and then the normal participle gelaten is used. Only laten + infinitive gives two infinitives."},{"type":"choose","nl":"___ we de monteur bellen, de verwarming hapert weer.","options":["Laat","Laten","Liet"],"correct":1,"en":"Let's call the mechanic, the heating is playing up again.","note":"Let's is laten we: we is plural, so laten. Laat we is a common mistake."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De wc is verstopt; ik laat morgen een loodgieter ___ komen.","options":["te","–","om te"],"correct":1,"en":"The toilet is blocked; I am having a plumber come tomorrow.","note":"Laten takes the bare infinitive, without te, just like the modal verbs: laat … komen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vorig jaar ___ (laten) we het dak repareren.","answers":["lieten"],"en":"Last year we had the roof repaired.","note":"Laten is a strong verb: simple past liet, plural lieten. Vorig jaar comes first, so the verb comes before we."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hebben jullie de kraan al ___ (laten) vervangen?","answers":["laten"],"en":"Have you already had the tap replaced?","note":"With an infinitive (vervangen) the perfect of laten is the infinitive laten, not gelaten."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Heb je de lekkage al laten ___ (nakijken)?","answers":["nakijken"],"en":"Have you had the leak checked yet?","note":"After laten the second verb stays an infinitive, also in the perfect: laten nakijken, never laten nagekeken."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["laat","de monteur","de auto","nakijken"],"answers":["Ik laat de monteur de auto nakijken."],"en":"I am having the mechanic check the car.","note":"When the doer is an object, it comes right after laten: first de monteur, then de auto. The infinitive goes last."},{"type":"order","lead":"Morgen","words":["laat","ik","de lekkage","door een loodgieter","repareren"],"answers":["Morgen laat ik de lekkage door een loodgieter repareren.","Morgen laat ik de lekkage repareren door een loodgieter."],"en":"Tomorrow I am having the leak repaired by a plumber.","note":"Morgen is first, so laat comes second and ik follows. Repareren goes to the end; door een loodgieter may also come after it."},{"type":"order","lead":"Omdat de verwarming hapert","words":["laat","ik","een monteur","komen"],"answers":["Omdat de verwarming hapert, laat ik een monteur komen."],"en":"Because the heating is playing up, I am sending for a mechanic.","note":"The omdat-clause is the first element, so laat comes next and then ik. Laten komen means to have someone come."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Ik laat de kraan vervangen.","answers":["Ik heb de kraan laten vervangen."],"en":"I have had the tap replaced.","note":"Heb stands second, and laten and vervangen both go to the end as infinitives, laten first."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a suggestion with Laten we.","from":"We vragen eerst een offerte.","answers":["Laten we eerst een offerte vragen."],"en":"Let's ask for a quote first.","note":"Laten we takes the first place, and vragen moves to the end as an infinitive."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say that you have it done instead of doing it yourself: start with Ik laat, and leave out zelf.","from":"Ik repareer de lekkage zelf.","answers":["Ik laat de lekkage repareren."],"en":"I am having the leak repaired.","note":"Laat takes the place of repareer, and repareren goes to the end as an infinitive without te."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik maak mijn wasmachine repareren.","answers":["Ik laat mijn wasmachine repareren."],"en":"I am having my washing machine repaired.","note":"Dutch uses laten for having something done. Maken + infinitive does not exist in this meaning."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"We hebben de oude wasmachine gelaten repareren.","answers":["We hebben de oude wasmachine laten repareren."],"en":"We have had the old washing machine repaired.","note":"When laten has an infinitive with it, its own perfect form is laten as well: two infinitives at the end."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Laat we het onderhoud van de ketel plannen.","answers":["Laten we het onderhoud van de ketel plannen."],"en":"Let's schedule the maintenance of the boiler.","note":"In laten we the verb agrees with we, which is plural: laten."}]},
  {"id":"b1-21","level":"B1","icon":"🧹","title":"Tidying up","grammar":"Staan, liggen, zitten, hangen and zetten, leggen, stoppen","summary":"Where English says 'is', Dutch says how: staan (upright), liggen (flat), zitten (inside or stuck on), hangen. To put: zetten, leggen, stoppen or doen, hangen. lag, gelegen (strong) against legde, gelegd (regular).","explain":"English says *the bottle is on the table* and *put it on the table*. Dutch is more precise: it chooses the verb by **how** the thing is positioned. A bottle stands, a book lies, keys sit in a bag, a coat hangs. These are the *verbs of position* (de positiewerkwoorden). `zijn` is not wrong, but it sounds vague; Dutch speakers use the position verb almost every time.\n\n### Where something is: four verbs\n\n| verb | when | example |\n| staan | upright, or on its base | De fles staat op tafel. |\n| liggen | flat, lying down | Het boek ligt op tafel. |\n| zitten | inside, or stuck on | De pen zit in mijn tas. |\n| hangen | hanging from a point | De jas hangt aan de kapstok. |\n\n- **staan** is for everything that stands on its base, also when it is low and flat: plates, pans and bowls stand on the table (`Het eten staat op tafel`). A parked car or bike stands too. And text stands on paper or a screen.\n- **liggen** is for things lying flat, people and animals lying down, and for places on the map.\n- **zitten** is for people sitting, for things **inside** something that holds them all round (a bag, a pocket, a box, a jar), for things mixed into something (`Er zit suiker in de thee`) and for things **stuck on** or attached to something: a stain, a hole, a button.\n- In a cupboard, on a shelf or in the fridge, Dutch usually looks at the position again: `De melk staat in de koelkast`, `De handdoeken liggen in de kast`. For small things in a drawer or a box both work: `De messen liggen in de la` or `zitten in de la`.\n\n> De auto staat voor de deur. — The car is (parked) in front of the house.\n> Het staat in de krant. — It is in the newspaper.\n> Utrecht ligt in het midden van het land. — Utrecht is in the middle of the country.\n> Er zit een vlek op je shirt. — There is a stain on your shirt.\n\n### Putting something somewhere\n\nEach position has its own verb for *to put*: you put a thing into the position.\n\n| put it … | verb | example |\n| upright | zetten | Ik zet de fles op tafel. |\n| flat | leggen | Ik leg het boek op tafel. |\n| inside | stoppen / doen | Ik stop de pen in mijn tas. |\n| hanging | hangen | Ik hang de jas aan de kapstok. |\n\n`doen` is the everyday word for putting something in or into: `Doe de melk in de koelkast`. `opbergen` means *to put away* when it does not matter how. To sit down or lie down yourself is `gaan zitten` and `gaan liggen`, not zetten or leggen.\n\n> Zet de vaas op de vensterbank. — Put the vase on the windowsill.\n> Ik hang de handdoek aan een haak. — I hang the towel on a hook.\n\n### The forms: strong against regular\n\n| verb | past | perfect |\n| staan | stond | heeft gestaan |\n| liggen | lag | heeft gelegen |\n| zitten | zat | heeft gezeten |\n| hangen | hing | heeft gehangen |\n| zetten | zette | heeft gezet |\n| leggen | legde | heeft gelegd |\n| stoppen | stopte | heeft gestopt |\n\nThe verbs for *being* somewhere are strong (the vowel changes; plural `lagen`, `zaten`, `stonden`). The verbs for *putting* are regular. `hangen` is one verb for both meanings, and it is strong in both. All of them take `hebben` in the perfect.\n\n> Ik heb de hele dag in bed gelegen. — I lay in bed all day.\n> Ik heb het boek op tafel gelegd. — I put the book on the table.\n\n### With er\n\nWhen the subject is new (`een`, a number, no article), the sentence starts with `er` (or with the place: `Op tafel staat een fles`), and the verb agrees with what follows: `Er staat een fles op tafel`, `Er liggen overal papieren`. In a question `zijn` is normal (`Waar is mijn telefoon?`), but the answer uses the position: `Hij ligt op de bank`.\n\n### Fixed expressions\n\n- `in de file staan`: to be stuck in a traffic jam.\n- `op het punt staan (om) te`: to be about to: `Ik stond op het punt om te gaan`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Ik heb het boek op tafel gelegen → `Ik heb het boek op tafel gelegd`. `gelegen` is *lain*, `gelegd` is *put*.\n- *Ik heb de fles op tafel gestaan → `Ik heb de fles op tafel gezet`.\n- *Ik zit de vaas op de vensterbank → `Ik zet de vaas op de vensterbank`. zitten and zetten sound alike.\n- *Er ligt een vlek op mijn broek → `Er zit een vlek op mijn broek`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian works the same way: `стояти`, `лежати`, `висіти` and `ставити`, `класти`, `вішати`. Two differences: Ukrainian can leave the verb out (`Ключі в сумці`), Dutch needs one (`De sleutels zitten in mijn tas`); and Dutch uses `zitten` for things inside or stuck on, where `сидіти` is mainly for people and animals.","words":[{"nl":"la","art":"de","pl":"la's","en":"drawer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"plank","art":"de","pl":"planken","en":"shelf","pos":"n"},{"nl":"aanrecht","art":"het/de","pl":"aanrechten","en":"kitchen counter","pos":"n"},{"nl":"kapstok","art":"de","pl":"kapstokken","en":"coat rack","pos":"n"},{"nl":"haak","art":"de","pl":"haken","en":"hook","pos":"n"},{"nl":"emmer","art":"de","pl":"emmers","en":"bucket","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vensterbank","art":"de","pl":"vensterbanken","en":"windowsill","pos":"n"},{"nl":"wasmand","art":"de","pl":"wasmanden","en":"laundry basket","pos":"n"},{"nl":"theedoek","art":"de","pl":"theedoeken","en":"tea towel","pos":"n"},{"nl":"schoonmaakmiddel","art":"het","pl":"schoonmaakmiddelen","en":"cleaning product","pos":"n"},{"nl":"stof","art":"het","pl":"","en":"dust","pos":"n","tag":"no plural"},{"nl":"opbergen","past":"borg op","perf":"heeft opgeborgen","en":"to put away / to store","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ophangen","past":"hing op","perf":"heeft opgehangen","en":"to hang up","pos":"v"},{"nl":"rommelig","en":"messy / untidy","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Er staat een emmer water onder het aanrecht.","en":"There is a bucket of water under the kitchen counter.","hint":["staan","emmer","water","aanrecht"],"note":"A bucket stands on its base, so staan. Een emmer is new information, so the sentence starts with er.","alt":["Onder het aanrecht staat een emmer water.","Er staat een emmer met water onder het aanrecht.","Onder het aanrecht staat een emmer met water."]},{"nl":"Je jas hangt aan de kapstok in de gang.","en":"Your coat is hanging on the coat rack in the hall.","hint":["jas","hangen","kapstok","gang"],"note":"Hangen goes with aan: the coat hangs from a point. English says 'on', Dutch says aan.","alt":["Jouw jas hangt aan de kapstok in de gang."]},{"nl":"De boeken staan op de plank en de tijdschriften liggen op de vensterbank.","en":"The books are standing on the shelf and the magazines are lying on the windowsill.","hint":["boek","staan","plank","tijdschrift","liggen","vensterbank"],"note":"Books in a row are upright, so staan; magazines lie flat, so liggen. Both subjects are plural: staan, liggen."},{"nl":"Mijn sleutels zitten in mijn jaszak.","en":"My keys are in my coat pocket.","hint":["sleutel","zitten","jaszak"],"note":"Things inside a closed space such as a pocket or a bag take zitten. Ukrainian would leave the verb out here; Dutch cannot.","alt":["Mijn sleutels zitten in de zak van mijn jas."]},{"nl":"Mijn bureau is rommelig, want er liggen overal papieren.","en":"My desk is messy, because there are papers lying everywhere.","hint":["bureau","rommelig","want","liggen","overal","papier"],"note":"Er + liggen for loose papers lying around. The verb agrees with papieren, which comes after it: er liggen, plural.","alt":["Mijn bureau is rommelig, want overal liggen papieren."]},{"nl":"Kun je de messen in de bovenste la leggen?","en":"Can you put the knives in the top drawer?","hint":["kunnen","mes","bovenste","la","leggen"],"note":"Knives lie flat in a drawer, so the usual put verb is leggen; doen and stoppen, the verbs for putting something in, are also fine here.","alt":["Kun jij de messen in de bovenste la leggen?","Kan je de messen in de bovenste la leggen?","Kan jij de messen in de bovenste la leggen?","Kun je de messen in de bovenste la doen?","Kun jij de messen in de bovenste la doen?","Kan je de messen in de bovenste la doen?","Kan jij de messen in de bovenste la doen?","Kun je de messen in de bovenste la stoppen?","Kun jij de messen in de bovenste la stoppen?"]},{"nl":"Ik heb de melk in de koelkast gezet.","en":"I put the milk in the fridge.","hint":["melk","koelkast","zetten"],"note":"A carton of milk stands upright in the fridge, so zetten. Zetten is regular: heeft gezet.","alt":["Ik heb de melk in de koelkast gedaan.","Ik zette de melk in de koelkast.","Ik deed de melk in de koelkast."]},{"nl":"Toen ik thuiskwam, lag de kat op de schone was.","en":"When I came home, the cat was lying on the clean laundry.","hint":["toen","thuiskomen","liggen","kat","schoon","was"],"note":"Liggen is strong: the past is lag, not *ligde. The sub-clause comes first, so lag stands right after the comma.","alt":["De kat lag op de schone was toen ik thuiskwam."]},{"nl":"Wie heeft de natte theedoek over de stoel gehangen?","en":"Who hung the wet tea towel over the chair?","hint":["wie","nat","theedoek","stoel","hangen"],"note":"Hangen is used both for 'be hanging' and for 'hang something', and both are strong: heeft gehangen, never *gehangd.","alt":["Wie hing de natte theedoek over de stoel?"]},{"nl":"Mijn broer staat al een uur in de file.","en":"My brother has been stuck in a traffic jam for an hour.","hint":["broer","staan","al","uur","file"],"note":"In de file staan is a fixed expression. Al + the present tense says how long it has been going on, as in English 'has been'."}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"De wasmand ___ naast de wasmachine.","options":["ligt","staat","zit"],"correct":1,"en":"The laundry basket is next to the washing machine.","note":"A basket stands on its bottom, so staan. Liggen would mean it had fallen over."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Er ___ geen suiker meer in de pot.","options":["ligt","staat","zit"],"correct":2,"en":"There is no sugar left in the jar.","note":"What is inside a jar, a box or a bag zit erin. The sugar has no position of its own; it is simply inside."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ de vaas met bloemen op de vensterbank.","options":["leg","zit","zet"],"correct":2,"en":"I put the vase of flowers on the windowsill.","note":"You make the vase stand, so zetten: ik zet. Zit comes from zitten (to sit), which never means 'to put'."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb de hele middag op de bank ___.","options":["gelegd","gelegen","gelegt"],"correct":1,"en":"I lay on the sofa all afternoon.","note":"Lying somewhere is liggen, a strong verb: heeft gelegen. Gelegd belongs to leggen, to put something down."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Gisteren ___ (zetten) ik de lege flessen in de schuur.","answers":["zette"],"en":"Yesterday I put the empty bottles in the shed.","note":"Zetten is regular. Its stem zet ends in t, so the past gets -te: zette (one t from the stem, one from the ending)."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vorige week ___ (liggen) er nog een dikke laag stof op de kast.","answers":["lag"],"en":"Last week there was still a thick layer of dust on the cupboard.","note":"Dust lies on a surface, so liggen, and liggen is strong: lag. Een laag is singular, so lag, not lagen."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik ___ de brief in de envelop en breng hem naar de brievenbus.","answers":["stop","doe","steek"],"en":"I put the letter in the envelope and take it to the postbox.","note":"Putting something into a closed space is stoppen, or in everyday speech doen (steken is also possible). Not leggen or zetten: the letter goes inside."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hier ___ (hangen) vroeger een haak voor de theedoeken.","answers":["hing"],"en":"There used to be a hook for the tea towels here.","note":"Hangen is strong: the past is hing. The place hier stands first, so the verb comes second and the subject een haak follows."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Wat ___ (staan) er in de brief van de huisbaas?","answers":["staat"],"en":"What does the landlord's letter say?","note":"Text on paper or a screen staat. Where English asks what the letter says, Dutch usually asks what stands in it: Wat staat er in de brief?"},{"type":"order","lead":"Onder het aanrecht","words":["staan","de emmer","en","de schoonmaakmiddelen"],"answers":["Onder het aanrecht staan de emmer en de schoonmaakmiddelen.","Onder het aanrecht staan de schoonmaakmiddelen en de emmer."],"en":"The bucket and the cleaning products are under the kitchen counter.","note":"The place stands first, so the verb staan comes second and the subject follows. Two things together make a plural subject: staan."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","de winterjassen","op zolder","opgeborgen"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik de winterjassen op zolder opgeborgen.","Gisteren heb ik op zolder de winterjassen opgeborgen."],"en":"Yesterday I put the winter coats away in the attic.","note":"Opbergen is the general word for putting away, whatever the position. The participle opgeborgen stays in one piece at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say where the towels are as a result, with the matching verb of position.","from":"Ik heb de handdoeken in de kast gelegd.","answers":["De handdoeken liggen in de kast.","In de kast liggen de handdoeken."],"en":"The towels are (lying) in the cupboard.","note":"What you leggen, ligt afterwards. The pair is leggen (put flat) and liggen (lie flat)."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"We zaten de hele avond in de tuin.","answers":["We hebben de hele avond in de tuin gezeten.","Wij hebben de hele avond in de tuin gezeten."],"en":"We sat in the garden all evening.","note":"Zitten is strong: gezeten, with hebben. Do not mix it up with zetten, which gives gezet."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect tense.","from":"Mijn vader hangt de nieuwe lamp op.","answers":["Mijn vader heeft de nieuwe lamp opgehangen."],"en":"My father has hung up the new lamp.","note":"Ophangen is separable and strong: op + ge + hangen = opgehangen, with hebben."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb de fles op tafel gestaan.","answers":["Ik heb de fles op tafel gezet.","Ik heb de fles op tafel neergezet."],"en":"I put the bottle on the table.","note":"Staan cannot take an object: a bottle is not 'stood' by you. To make something stand is zetten: heb gezet."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Er ligt een vlek op mijn broek.","answers":["Er zit een vlek op mijn broek."],"en":"There is a stain on my trousers.","note":"A stain is stuck on the fabric, so zitten. The same goes for a hole or a button: er zit een gat in, er zit een knoop aan."}]},
  {"id":"b1-22","level":"B1","icon":"📖","title":"Telling a story","grammar":"Choosing the past tense; Ukrainian aspect in Dutch","summary":"Perfect: the fact or result, to open or close a story. Simple past: the events in a row, the background, habits, and after toen. Past perfect: a step back to before. Translate what Ukrainian aspect means, not the aspect itself.","explain":"When you tell what happened, Dutch has three past tenses, and in a story each one has its own job. You know the forms from earlier units: the perfect (de voltooide tijd: `ik heb gewerkt`), the simple past (de onvoltooid verleden tijd: `ik werkte`) and the past perfect (de voltooid verleden tijd: `ik had gewerkt`). This unit is about choosing between them, and about why Ukrainian aspect does not tell you which one to use.\n\n### The perfect: the fact and the result\n\nThe perfect says **that** something happened and that it matters now. Use it for a single event you report, for news, for questions about experience, and to open or close a story.\n\n> Er is gisteren iets geks gebeurd. — Something strange happened yesterday.\n> Heb je ooit zoiets meegemaakt? — Have you ever experienced anything like it?\n> Uiteindelijk is alles goed afgelopen. — In the end everything turned out fine.\n\nIn conversation the perfect is the normal past for one event: `Ik heb gisteren mijn moeder gebeld`.\n\n### The simple past: the story itself\n\nOnce the story is running, the simple past carries it:\n\n- **events one after another**: and then, and then …\n- **the background**: what was going on, how things looked, the weather.\n- **habits**: what you used to do, with `vroeger`, `altijd`, `vaak`, `elke zomer`.\n- a clause with **toen** (when): `Toen ik thuiskwam, …`\n\n> Ik stond op het perron en het regende. — I was standing on the platform and it was raining.\n> Opeens zag ik mijn oude buurman. — Suddenly I saw my old neighbour.\n> Vroeger fietsten we elke dag naar school. — We used to cycle to school every day.\n\n`zijn`, `hebben` and the modal verbs are often in the simple past even in a single remark: `Ik was gisteren ziek`, `Ik moest lang wachten`. After `toen` meaning *then* (`En toen …`) a story also goes on in the simple past, although in speech you hear the perfect too: `En toen heb ik de politie gebeld`.\n\n### The past perfect: a step back\n\nThe story is in the past; the past perfect goes back to something that had happened **before** that moment. It is `had` or `was` + participle, with the same auxiliary as in the perfect.\n\n> Toen we aankwamen, was de film al begonnen. — When we arrived, the film had already started.\n> Ik had hem al tien jaar niet gezien. — I had not seen him for ten years.\n\n### Signal words\n\n- `toen` (when / then), `daarna` (after that), `opeens` (suddenly): events in a row, simple past.\n- `ondertussen` (meanwhile): something happening at the same time.\n- `net` (just): `Ik heb net gegeten` (a moment ago) against `Ik had net gegeten` (just before that moment).\n- `al` (already) and `nog nooit` (never yet) often go with the perfect or the past perfect: `Ik had hem nog nooit gezien`.\n\n### Ukrainian aspect is not a Dutch tense\n\nMost Ukrainian verbs come in pairs: perfective (`прочитав`) and imperfective (`читав`). Dutch verbs have no such pairs; Dutch chooses a tense by its job in the story. So translate the meaning, not the aspect:\n\n| Ukrainian | meaning | Dutch |\n| Я прочитав книжку. | done, a result | Ik heb het boek gelezen. |\n| Ти читав цю книжку? | ever? a fact | Heb je dit boek gelezen? |\n| Я читав, коли він подзвонив. | in the middle of it | Ik was aan het lezen toen hij belde. |\n| Раніше я часто читав. | used to | Vroeger las ik vaak. |\n\nSo an imperfective verb can be `las`, `was aan het lezen` or `heb gelezen`, and a perfective verb can be `heb gelezen` or, inside a story, `las`. For *in the middle of it* use `was aan het …` or `zat / stond / lag te …` (unit B1 · 8).\n\n### Watch out\n\n- After toen (when), the simple past: *Toen ik in Utrecht heb gewoond → `Toen ik in Utrecht woonde`.\n- `Ik heb op het perron gestaan` is correct Dutch, but it only states a fact. As the background of a story, say `Ik stond op het perron`.\n- The past perfect needs an earlier point in the story: *Gisteren had ik een verrassing gekregen → `Gisteren heb ik een verrassing gekregen`.\n- When the main clause is in the past, use the past perfect for what happened before it: `Ik merkte dat iemand mijn tas had opengemaakt`. That keeps the order of events clear; `heeft opengemaakt` mixes in the present.","words":[{"nl":"verrassing","art":"de","pl":"verrassingen","en":"surprise","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gebeuren","past":"gebeurde","perf":"is gebeurd","en":"to happen","pos":"v"},{"nl":"meemaken","past":"maakte mee","perf":"heeft meegemaakt","en":"to experience / to go through","pos":"v"},{"nl":"schrikken","past":"schrok","perf":"is geschrokken","en":"to get a fright / to be startled","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontdekken","past":"ontdekte","perf":"heeft ontdekt","en":"to discover / to find out","pos":"v"},{"nl":"merken","past":"merkte","perf":"heeft gemerkt","en":"to notice","pos":"v"},{"nl":"tegenkomen","past":"kwam tegen","perf":"is tegengekomen","en":"to run into (someone)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"opeens","en":"suddenly","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"ondertussen","en":"meanwhile / in the meantime","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"toevallig","en":"by chance","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"achteraf","en":"afterwards / looking back","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"uiteindelijk","en":"in the end / eventually","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"ongelooflijk","en":"incredible","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"verbaasd","en":"surprised / amazed","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Er is gisteren iets geks gebeurd op mijn werk.","en":"Something strange happened at work yesterday.","hint":["gisteren","gek","gebeuren","werk"],"note":"The perfect opens the story: it states that something happened. Gebeuren takes zijn: is gebeurd.","alt":["Gisteren is er iets geks gebeurd op mijn werk.","Er is gisteren op mijn werk iets geks gebeurd.","Gisteren is er op mijn werk iets geks gebeurd.","Er is gisteren iets geks gebeurd op het werk.","Gisteren is er iets geks gebeurd op het werk.","Er is gisteren op het werk iets geks gebeurd.","Gisteren is er op het werk iets geks gebeurd.","Er gebeurde gisteren iets geks op mijn werk.","Gisteren gebeurde er iets geks op mijn werk."]},{"nl":"Ik stond op het perron en het regende hard.","en":"I was standing on the platform and it was raining hard.","hint":["staan","perron","regenen","hard"],"note":"This is the background of the story, so both verbs are in the simple past: stond, regende. English uses 'was …ing' here; Dutch needs no extra form."},{"nl":"Opeens kwam ik mijn oude buurman tegen.","en":"Suddenly I ran into my old neighbour.","hint":["opeens","tegenkomen","oud","buurman"],"note":"A new event in the story: simple past. Tegenkomen is separable, so kwam stands second and tegen goes to the end.","alt":["Ik kwam opeens mijn oude buurman tegen.","Opeens ben ik mijn oude buurman tegengekomen."]},{"nl":"Ik had hem al tien jaar niet gezien.","en":"I had not seen him for ten years.","hint":["al","jaar","zien"],"note":"A step back to the time before the meeting: the past perfect, had + gezien."},{"nl":"Toen hij mijn naam riep, schrok ik erg.","en":"When he called my name, I got a real fright.","hint":["toen","naam","roepen","schrikken","erg"],"note":"In a clause with toen (when) the verb is in the simple past: riep. The next event follows in the simple past too: schrok, from the strong verb schrikken.","alt":["Ik schrok erg toen hij mijn naam riep.","Toen hij mijn naam riep, ben ik erg geschrokken."]},{"nl":"Vroeger logeerden we elke zomer bij mijn oma op het platteland.","en":"Every summer we used to stay with my grandmother in the countryside.","hint":["vroeger","logeren","zomer","oma","platteland"],"note":"A habit in the past: the simple past with vroeger and elke zomer. Ukrainian would use an imperfective verb here; Dutch uses no extra word for 'used to'.","alt":["We logeerden vroeger elke zomer bij mijn oma op het platteland.","Vroeger logeerden we elke zomer op het platteland bij mijn oma."]},{"nl":"Ik was aan het koken toen de bel ging.","en":"I was cooking when the doorbell rang.","hint":["koken","toen","bel","gaan"],"note":"For 'in the middle of it' Dutch uses was aan het + infinitive. The event that interrupts it is a plain simple past: ging.","alt":["Ik stond te koken toen de bel ging.","Toen de bel ging, was ik aan het koken.","Toen de bel ging, stond ik te koken."]},{"nl":"Heb je ooit zoiets geks meegemaakt?","en":"Have you ever experienced anything so strange?","hint":["ooit","zoiets","gek","meemaken"],"note":"A question about experience takes the perfect, even where Ukrainian would use an imperfective verb (Ти колись …?).","alt":["Heb jij ooit zoiets geks meegemaakt?"]},{"nl":"Achteraf hebben we er veel om gelachen.","en":"Afterwards we laughed about it a lot.","hint":["achteraf","veel","lachen"],"note":"The perfect closes the story: looking back, this is the result. Erom belongs to lachen om (to laugh about).","alt":["We hebben er achteraf veel om gelachen.","Achteraf lachten we er veel om."]},{"nl":"Ondertussen had iemand mijn fiets meegenomen.","en":"Meanwhile someone had taken my bike.","hint":["ondertussen","iemand","fiets","meenemen"],"note":"The theft happened before you found out, so the past perfect: had meegenomen.","alt":["Iemand had ondertussen mijn fiets meegenomen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Toen we bij de bioscoop aankwamen, ___ de film al begonnen.","options":["is","was","werd"],"correct":1,"en":"When we arrived at the cinema, the film had already started.","note":"The film started before we arrived, so the past perfect. Beginnen takes zijn, so was begonnen. Is begonnen would not fit the past story."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ de afwas aan het doen toen je belde.","options":["had","ben","was"],"correct":2,"en":"I was doing the washing-up when you called.","note":"In the middle of something in the past: was aan het + infinitive. This form is built with zijn, never with hebben."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Weet je wat? Ik ___ vandaag toevallig een oude vriendin tegengekomen.","options":["heb","ben","was"],"correct":1,"en":"You know what? Today I ran into an old friend by chance.","note":"You are reporting news, so the perfect. Tegenkomen takes zijn: ben tegengekomen. Was would make it a step back to an earlier point in a story, and there is none here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik merkte pas thuis dat iemand mijn tas ___ opengemaakt.","options":["is","was","had"],"correct":2,"en":"I only noticed at home that someone had opened my bag.","note":"Merkte is in the past, and the opening happened before that: past perfect. Openmaken takes hebben, so had opengemaakt, not is or was."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Een van mijn mooiste herinneringen: ik ___ vijf en mijn vader leerde me fietsen.","options":["ben","was","had"],"correct":1,"en":"One of my best memories: I was five and my father taught me to ride a bike.","note":"How old you were is background, so the simple past: was. Dutch says ik was vijf with zijn, never with hebben."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Opeens ___ (zien) ik dat de deur openstond.","answers":["zag"],"en":"Suddenly I saw that the door was open.","note":"Opeens moves the story on with a new event: simple past. Zien is strong: zag."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik wist niet dat ze al ___ (vertrekken) was.","answers":["vertrokken"],"en":"I did not know that she had already left.","note":"Her leaving came before the moment you did not know it: the past perfect. Vertrekken takes zijn and its participle is vertrokken."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik stond in de file en ondertussen ___ (beginnen) de vergadering zonder mij.","answers":["begon"],"en":"I was stuck in a traffic jam and meanwhile the meeting started without me.","note":"Two things at the same time in the story, both in the simple past. Beginnen is strong: begon."},{"type":"order","lead":"Uiteindelijk","words":["heeft","een buurjongen","mijn tas","gevonden"],"answers":["Uiteindelijk heeft een buurjongen mijn tas gevonden."],"en":"In the end a boy from next door found my bag.","note":"The result that closes the story: the perfect. Heeft stays second after uiteindelijk, and the participle goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["was","aan het","fietsen","toen","het","begon","te regenen"],"answers":["Ik was aan het fietsen toen het begon te regenen.","Ik was aan het fietsen toen het te regenen begon."],"en":"I was cycling when it started to rain.","note":"Was aan het fietsen is the action going on; toen brings in the event, in the simple past. In the toen-clause both begon te regenen and te regenen begon are correct."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Keep the first sentence. Turn the second into story-telling: background and event in the simple past.","from":"Ik ben gisteren mijn oude lerares tegengekomen. Ik heb in de supermarkt gestaan en zij is naar me toe gelopen.","answers":["Ik ben gisteren mijn oude lerares tegengekomen. Ik stond in de supermarkt en zij liep naar me toe.","Ik ben gisteren mijn oude lerares tegengekomen. Ik stond in de supermarkt en ze liep naar me toe."],"en":"I ran into my old teacher yesterday. I was standing in the supermarket and she walked up to me.","note":"The perfect states the news; then the story starts. Where you stood is background and her walking up is the next event, so both go into the simple past: stond, liep."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join the two sentences with toen. Start with the toen-clause.","from":"Ik kwam thuis. Ik ontdekte dat het raam openstond.","answers":["Toen ik thuiskwam, ontdekte ik dat het raam openstond."],"en":"When I came home, I discovered that the window was open.","note":"In the toen-clause the verb goes to the end: thuiskwam, written as one word. The clause counts as the first element, so ontdekte comes next and then ik."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Join with omdat. The reason happened earlier, so use the past perfect for it.","from":"Ik was verbaasd. Niemand vertelde het me.","answers":["Ik was verbaasd, omdat niemand het me had verteld.","Ik was verbaasd, omdat niemand het me verteld had."],"en":"I was surprised, because nobody had told me.","note":"Not being told came before the surprise: had verteld. In the omdat-clause the verbs go to the end, in either order."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it a habit in the past. Start with Vroeger.","from":"Mijn vader leest elke ochtend de krant.","answers":["Vroeger las mijn vader elke ochtend de krant."],"en":"My father used to read the newspaper every morning.","note":"A habit in the past is the simple past: las, from the strong verb lezen. Dutch has no separate form for 'used to'."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Toen ik in Utrecht heb gewoond, fietste ik elke dag.","answers":["Toen ik in Utrecht woonde, fietste ik elke dag."],"en":"When I lived in Utrecht, I cycled every day.","note":"A clause with toen (when) takes the simple past: woonde. The perfect after toen is heard in Belgium, but in the Netherlands it sounds wrong."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Gisteren had ik een ongelooflijke verrassing gekregen.","answers":["Gisteren heb ik een ongelooflijke verrassing gekregen.","Gisteren kreeg ik een ongelooflijke verrassing."],"en":"Yesterday I got an incredible surprise.","note":"The past perfect needs an earlier point in a story to step back from, and there is none. For news about yesterday use the perfect; the simple past kreeg is also correct."}]},
  {"id":"b1-23","level":"B1","icon":"💬","title":"Small talk","grammar":"Small words that set the tone: maar, even, eens, toch, wel, hoor","summary":"Unstressed little words in the middle of the sentence set the tone: even and eens make a request light, maar gives permission, toch asks for agreement, wel contradicts or reassures, hoor at the end is friendly.","explain":"Dutch speakers colour almost every sentence with small unstressed words: *modal particles* (de modale partikels). They do not change the facts, they change the tone: friendly, impatient, reassuring, surprised. Without them Dutch sounds abrupt. `Wacht!` is an order; `Wacht even` is a friendly 'just a moment'. They have no direct English translation, so learn them as tones.\n\n### Making a request friendly: even, eens, maar\n\n| word | what it adds | example |\n| even | only a moment, no big deal | Kun je even helpen? |\n| eens | a friendly push or invitation | Kijk eens! |\n| maar | go ahead, you may | Ga maar zitten. |\n\n> Mag ik je even iets vragen? — Can I ask you something?\n> Kom eens langs. — Come round some time.\n> Zeg het maar. — Go ahead, tell me. / What can I get you?\n\n- `maar` also means **only**: `Ik heb maar vijf euro`. With `niet` it means *better not*: `Doe dat maar niet`.\n- `eens` also means *some time*: `Ik wil eens naar Japan`. And `niet eens` is *not even*.\n- Stressed `even` is a different word: `even groot` (just as big).\n\n### toch\n\n- **Asking for agreement**: `Je komt toch?` (You are coming, aren't you?). It can also stand at the end: `Je komt, toch?`\n- **Surprise or a reproach**: `Dat weet je toch?` (Surely you know that?)\n- **Insisting**: `Kom toch binnen!` (Do come in!)\n- **Anyway, after all**, stressed: `Ik ga toch` (I am going anyway).\n\n### wel\n\n- **Contradicting a negative**: `Hij komt niet. — Hij komt wel!` To say yes to a negative question, use `jawel`, not just `ja`.\n- **Reassuring**: `Het komt wel goed` (It will be all right).\n- **Probably**, with `zullen`: `Hij zal wel ziek zijn`.\n- **Granted …, but**: `Dat klopt wel, maar …`\n\n### hoor\n\n`hoor` goes at the **end** of a statement, or straight after `ja` and `nee`. It is friendly and takes the edge off: `Ja hoor`, `Nee hoor`, `Dat is niet erg, hoor`. It **never** goes in a question, and it does not belong in formal writing.\n\n### nou, gewoon, zeker, dan\n\n- `nou` (the spoken form of `nu`) starts a reaction, `Nou, ik weet het niet`, or adds impatience: `Kom nou!`\n- `gewoon`: just, simply, without fuss: `Zeg het gewoon`.\n- `zeker`, unstressed, makes a guess you want confirmed: `Je bent zeker moe?` (I suppose you are tired?)\n- `dan` in a question reacts to what the other person just said: `Wat is er dan?` `Waarom dan?`\n\n> Je bent zeker de nieuwe collega? — I suppose you are the new colleague?\n> Wat is er dan? — What is the matter, then?\n\n### Where they go\n\nParticles stand in the middle of the sentence: after the verb and the subject, **after** short pronouns such as `het`, `me`, `je`, `hem`, and **before** `niet` and the verbs at the end.\n\n> Kun je me even helpen? — Can you help me for a moment?\n> Doe het toch maar niet. — Better not do it after all.\n\nA noun object with `de` or `het` can stand on either side: `Geef me even het zout` and `Geef me het zout even` are both fine. When particles pile up, the order is fixed: `dan` / `nou`, `toch`, `maar`, `eens`, `even`. `Kom dan toch maar eens even langs` is a famous example, but one or two at a time is normal.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- No hoor in a question: *Kunt u me helpen, hoor? → `Kunt u me even helpen?`\n- A pronoun comes before the particle: *Zeg maar het → `Zeg het maar`; *Kun je even me helpen? → `Kun je me even helpen?`\n- `maar` in the middle is not *but*: `Ga maar` means *go ahead*.\n- Contradict a negative with `wel`, not with `ja`: `Ben je niet moe? — Jawel.`\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian has similar little words, such as `ж` (`Ти ж прийдеш?` — `Je komt toch?`) and `-но` (`Подивись-но!` — `Kijk eens!`), but they do not match the Dutch ones one to one.","words":[{"nl":"opmerking","art":"de","pl":"opmerkingen","en":"remark / comment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"compliment","art":"het","pl":"complimenten","en":"compliment","pos":"n"},{"nl":"smoes","art":"de","pl":"smoezen","en":"excuse (made up)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"een praatje maken","en":"to have a (quick) chat","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"kletsen","past":"kletste","perf":"heeft gekletst","en":"to chat / to chatter","pos":"v"},{"nl":"onderbreken","past":"onderbrak","perf":"heeft onderbroken","en":"to interrupt","pos":"v"},{"nl":"storen","past":"stoorde","perf":"heeft gestoord","en":"to disturb","pos":"v"},{"nl":"kloppen","past":"klopte","perf":"heeft geklopt","en":"to be right / to be correct","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overdrijven","past":"overdreef","perf":"heeft overdreven","en":"to exaggerate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"beleefd","en":"polite","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"brutaal","en":"cheeky / rude","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"trouwens","en":"by the way","pos":"adv"},{"nl":"eerlijk gezegd","en":"to be honest","pos":"expr"},{"nl":"het maakt niet uit","en":"it doesn't matter","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Sorry, stoor ik even? Ik heb een korte vraag.","en":"Sorry, am I disturbing you for a moment? I have a quick question.","hint":["sorry","storen","even","kort","vraag"],"note":"Even makes the interruption small: only a moment. Without it, Stoor ik? sounds heavier."},{"nl":"Ga maar zitten, ik zet even koffie.","en":"Go ahead and sit down, I'll just make some coffee.","hint":["gaan","maar","zitten","zetten","even","koffie"],"note":"Maar in an order gives permission or encouragement: go ahead. Even makes the coffee-making a small, quick thing.","alt":["Ga maar zitten, dan zet ik even koffie."]},{"nl":"Kom trouwens eens langs, dan kunnen we gezellig kletsen.","en":"By the way, come round some time, then we can have a nice chat.","hint":["langskomen","trouwens","eens","gezellig","kletsen"],"note":"Eens turns the order into a friendly invitation. It stands in the middle, before the separable part langs at the end.","alt":["Trouwens, kom eens langs, dan kunnen we gezellig kletsen."]},{"nl":"Je komt toch ook naar het feest?","en":"You are coming to the party too, aren't you?","hint":["komen","toch","ook","feest"],"note":"Toch asks the other person to agree, like an English tag question. It stands right after the verb and the subject.","alt":["Jij komt toch ook naar het feest?","Je komt ook naar het feest, toch?","Jij komt ook naar het feest, toch?"]},{"nl":"Dat klopt wel, maar je overdrijft een beetje.","en":"That is true enough, but you are exaggerating a bit.","hint":["kloppen","wel","maar","overdrijven","beetje"],"note":"Wel … maar grants one point and then adds an objection. Here maar is the ordinary 'but', joining two sentences.","alt":["Dat klopt wel, maar jij overdrijft een beetje."]},{"nl":"Hij is er nog niet. Hij zal wel in de file staan.","en":"He is not here yet. He is probably stuck in a traffic jam.","hint":["er","nog niet","zullen","wel","file","staan"],"note":"Zal … wel is a guess: probably. The speaker does not know, but thinks it likely."},{"nl":"Dat maakt helemaal niet uit, hoor.","en":"That doesn't matter at all, honestly.","hint":["helemaal","niet","uitmaken","hoor"],"note":"Hoor at the end makes the reassurance warm and friendly. It is only used at the end of a statement, never in a question."},{"nl":"Ik kan maar even blijven, want ik moet nog koken.","en":"I can only stay for a little while, because I still have to cook.","hint":["kunnen","maar","even","blijven","moeten","koken"],"note":"Here maar means 'only', and even means 'a short time'. Together, maar even: only briefly."},{"nl":"Wat bedoel je dan met die opmerking?","en":"What do you mean by that remark, then?","hint":["wat","bedoelen","dan","opmerking"],"note":"Dan in a question reacts to what the other person has just said. It stands after the verb and the subject.","alt":["Wat bedoel jij dan met die opmerking?"]},{"nl":"Je bent zeker de nieuwe collega?","en":"I suppose you are the new colleague?","hint":["zijn","zeker","nieuw","collega"],"note":"Unstressed zeker in a statement with a question mark is a guess you want confirmed. It does not mean 'certainly' here.","alt":["Jij bent zeker de nieuwe collega?"]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Mag ik je ___ onderbreken? Ik heb een vraag.","options":["even","hoor","zeker"],"correct":0,"en":"May I interrupt you for a moment? I have a question.","note":"Even makes the request small and polite. Hoor never goes in a question, and zeker would make it a guess."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Mag ik hier zitten? — Ja hoor, ga ___ zitten.","options":["eens","maar","wel"],"correct":1,"en":"May I sit here? — Of course, go ahead and sit down.","note":"Maar gives permission: go ahead. Eens would be your own suggestion, not an answer to a request, and wel contradicts a negative."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Eerlijk gezegd vind ik die film niet zo goed. — Nou, ik vind hem ___ goed!","options":["maar","wel","eens"],"correct":1,"en":"To be honest I don't think that film is very good. — Well, I do think it's good!","note":"Wel contradicts the negative of the other person: niet goed against wel goed. English uses a stressed 'do' for this."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Je hebt het ___ niet vergeten? We hadden om acht uur afgesproken.","options":["eens","maar","toch"],"correct":2,"en":"You haven't forgotten, have you? We had agreed on eight o'clock.","note":"Toch niet asks for reassurance: surely not? The speaker hopes the answer is no."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik maak ___ een praatje met de buurvrouw, ik ben zo terug.","answers":["even","nog even","eventjes","snel even"],"en":"I'm just having a quick chat with the neighbour, I'll be right back.","note":"Even says it will only take a moment. It stands after the verb and the subject, before the object."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Hij zegt dat hij ziek is, maar dat zal ___ een smoes zijn.","answers":["wel","vast","vast wel","waarschijnlijk"],"en":"He says he is ill, but that is probably an excuse.","note":"Zal … wel is a guess: probably. Vast (surely), vast wel and waarschijnlijk give almost the same meaning and are also right."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Vind jij dat niet beleefd? Ik vind het ___ beleefd.","answers":["wel"],"en":"Don't you think that's polite? I do think it's polite.","note":"Wel is the opposite of niet: it contradicts the negative. English stresses 'do' instead."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Nee ___, je stoort helemaal niet.","answers":["hoor","joh"],"en":"No, don't worry, you're not disturbing me at all.","note":"Nee hoor is a friendly no: no, don't worry. Hoor can stand straight after ja or nee. Nee joh, more familiar, is also right."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Ik kreeg vandaag een compliment van mijn baas. — Waarvoor ___?","answers":["dan"],"en":"I got a compliment from my boss today. — What for, then?","note":"Dan in a question reacts to what you have just heard and asks for more."},{"type":"order","lead":"Mag","words":["ik","je","even","iets","vragen"],"answers":["Mag ik je even iets vragen?"],"en":"Can I ask you something?","note":"The short pronoun je comes before the particle even. The rest follows, and the infinitive vragen goes to the end."},{"type":"order","lead":"Doe","words":["het","toch","maar","niet"],"answers":["Doe het toch maar niet."],"en":"Better not do it after all.","note":"The pronoun het comes first, then the particles in their fixed order, toch before maar, and niet after them."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the request friendlier with even.","from":"Geef me het zout.","answers":["Geef me even het zout.","Geef me het zout even."],"en":"Could you pass me the salt?","note":"Even after the pronoun me. A noun with het or de may stand before or after even, so both orders are right."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Turn it into a question that expects 'yes', with toch.","from":"Je vond het een leuk onderwerp.","answers":["Je vond het toch een leuk onderwerp?","Je vond het een leuk onderwerp, toch?"],"en":"You liked the topic, didn't you?","note":"Toch asks for agreement. In the middle it comes after the pronoun het; it can also stand at the end after a comma."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Contradict the sentence with wel.","from":"Hij overdrijft niet.","answers":["Hij overdrijft wel."],"en":"He does exaggerate.","note":"Wel takes the place of niet and says the opposite. It is the Dutch answer to a negative."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Is dat niet een beetje brutaal, hoor?","answers":["Is dat niet een beetje brutaal?"],"en":"Isn't that a bit cheeky?","note":"Hoor never goes in a question. It belongs at the end of a statement: Dat is een beetje brutaal, hoor."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Zeg maar het.","answers":["Zeg het maar."],"en":"Go ahead, tell me.","note":"A short pronoun such as het comes before the particle: Zeg het maar. A waiter uses the same words: What can I get you?"}]},
  {"id":"b1-24","level":"B1","icon":"🎁","title":"Giving and receiving","grammar":"Word order in the middle of the sentence","summary":"Between the verb and the end: pronouns first; person before thing (or thing + aan + person), but het before a person pronoun; an indefinite object after the time; time, manner, place; niet after a definite object.","explain":"Unit A2 · 3 put the conjugated verb in second place, and the other verbs go to the end. Between them lies the *middle of the sentence* (het middenveld): objects, pronouns, time, manner, place and `niet`. Dutch has a few firm habits there, and they are why *Ik heb gisteren hem gezien sounds foreign.\n\n### 1. Pronouns come early\n\nShort pronouns without stress (`me`, `je`, `hem`, `haar`, `het`, `ons`, `ze`) stand at the very start of the middle: right after the verb, or right after the subject when the subject follows the verb. Time words and everything else come after them.\n\n> Ik heb hem gisteren met zijn verjaardag gefeliciteerd. — I congratulated him on his birthday yesterday.\n> Gisteren heb ik hem gefeliciteerd. — Yesterday I congratulated him.\n\n*Ik heb gisteren hem gefeliciteerd is only possible with strong stress on hem (him, not her).\n\n### 2. Two objects\n\nVerbs like `geven`, `sturen`, `schenken`, `vertellen` and `overhandigen` can have two objects: the thing, and the person who gets it.\n\n| what you have | order |\n| two nouns | person, thing: `mijn moeder een boek` |\n| two nouns with aan | thing, aan + person: `een boek aan mijn moeder` |\n| a pronoun and a noun | the pronoun first: `haar een boek`, `het aan mijn moeder` |\n| two pronouns | het or ze (the thing) first: `het haar`, `het aan haar` |\n\n> Ik geef mijn moeder een boeket. — I am giving my mother a bouquet.\n> Ik geef een boeket aan mijn moeder. — I am giving a bouquet to my mother.\n> Ik heb het haar gisteren gegeven. — I gave it to her yesterday.\n\nWithout `aan`, the person must stand **before** the thing: *Ik geef een boeket mijn moeder is wrong. With two pronouns it is the other way round, the thing first: never *Ik geef haar het. When the thing is a pronoun and the person a noun, the usual way is with `aan`: `Ik geef het aan mijn moeder`. `Ik geef het mijn moeder` is correct but rarer.\n\n### 3. Known before new\n\nA definite object (`het cadeau`, `die bloemen`, `haar verlanglijstje`) is something already known. It may stand before or after a time word: before it is the neutral choice, after it puts a little more weight on the object. An indefinite object (`een cadeau`, `bloemen`, `veel felicitaties`) is new information and goes **after** the time word, close to the verbs at the end.\n\n> Ik heb gisteren een cadeau gekocht. — I bought a present yesterday.\n> Ik heb het cadeau gisteren gekocht. — I bought the present yesterday.\n\n`Ik heb gisteren het cadeau gekocht` is right too, but *Ik heb een cadeau gisteren gekocht is not. The same goes for `al`, `altijd` and `vaak`: `Ik heb al een cadeau`, not *Ik heb een cadeau al.\n\n### 4. Time, manner, place\n\nWhen several of these stand in the middle, the neutral order is **time** (when?), **manner** (how? with whom?), **place** (where? where to?). A place or direction belongs closely to the verb at the end.\n\n> Ik ben gisteren met de fiets naar het feest gegaan. — Yesterday I went to the party by bike.\n\nEnglish puts the time last (*to the party yesterday*); Dutch does not: *Ik ben met de fiets naar het feest gisteren gegaan. Any of the three can also take the first place: `Gisteren ben ik met de fiets naar het feest gegaan`. A phrase with a preposition may follow the last verb, a direction less often: `… naar het feest gegaan met de fiets`.\n\n### 5. Niet\n\nAs in unit A2 · 7, `niet` comes after pronouns, after a definite object and after a time word, and before a place, a phrase with a preposition and the verbs at the end.\n\n> Ik heb het cadeau nog niet ingepakt. — I have not wrapped the present yet.\n> We gaan morgen niet naar de bruiloft. — We are not going to the wedding tomorrow.\n\n`Niet` before a definite object or a time word is only used for a contrast: `niet het cadeau, maar de kaart`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Ik heb gisteren hem gezien → `Ik heb hem gisteren gezien`.\n- *Ik geef haar het → `Ik geef het haar` or `Ik geef het aan haar`.\n- *Ik geef een boek mijn moeder → `Ik geef mijn moeder een boek` or `Ik geef een boek aan mijn moeder`.\n- *Ik heb een cadeau gisteren gekocht → `Ik heb gisteren een cadeau gekocht`.\n- *Ik heb niet het cadeau ingepakt → `Ik heb het cadeau niet ingepakt`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian marks the person who receives with the dative ending (`мамі`, `їй`), so the order is free: `Я дав їй книжку` and `Я дав книжку їй` are both fine. Dutch nouns have no case endings, so in `Ik geef mijn moeder een boek` only the position shows who gets what. That is why, without `aan`, the person must come first.","words":[{"nl":"uitpakken","past":"pakte uit","perf":"heeft uitgepakt","en":"to unwrap / to unpack","pos":"v"},{"nl":"versieren","past":"versierde","perf":"heeft versierd","en":"to decorate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"schenken","past":"schonk","perf":"heeft geschonken","en":"to give (as a present) / to donate","pos":"v"},{"nl":"overhandigen","past":"overhandigde","perf":"heeft overhandigd","en":"to hand over / to present","pos":"v"},{"nl":"attentie","art":"de","pl":"attenties","en":"small present / token","pos":"n"},{"nl":"slinger","art":"de","pl":"slingers","en":"paper garland / streamer","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gastvrouw","art":"de","pl":"gastvrouwen","en":"hostess","pos":"n"},{"nl":"cadeaubon","art":"de","pl":"cadeaubonnen","en":"gift voucher","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bruiloft","art":"de","pl":"bruiloften","en":"wedding","pos":"n"},{"nl":"boeket","art":"het/de","pl":"boeketten","en":"bouquet","pos":"n"},{"nl":"verlanglijstje","art":"het","pl":"verlanglijstjes","en":"wish list (for presents)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"felicitatie","art":"de","pl":"felicitaties","en":"congratulation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gelukwens","art":"de","pl":"gelukwensen","en":"congratulation / good wish","pos":"n"},{"nl":"attent","en":"thoughtful / considerate","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"dankbaar","en":"grateful","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"iemand een plezier doen","en":"to do someone a favour","pos":"expr"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik heb hem gisteren met zijn verjaardag gefeliciteerd.","en":"I congratulated him on his birthday yesterday.","hint":["feliciteren","gisteren","met","verjaardag"],"note":"The pronoun hem has no stress, so it comes first in the middle, before the time word gisteren. *Ik heb gisteren hem … would put stress on hem.","alt":["Gisteren heb ik hem met zijn verjaardag gefeliciteerd.","Ik heb hem gisteren gefeliciteerd met zijn verjaardag.","Gisteren heb ik hem gefeliciteerd met zijn verjaardag."]},{"nl":"Voor onze bruiloft hebben mijn ouders ons een cadeaubon gegeven.","en":"For our wedding my parents gave us a gift voucher.","hint":["voor","bruiloft","ouders","cadeaubon","geven"],"note":"Two objects: the pronoun ons (the person) comes before the noun een cadeaubon (the thing). The subject mijn ouders stands right after the verb hebben.","alt":["Mijn ouders hebben ons voor onze bruiloft een cadeaubon gegeven.","Mijn ouders hebben ons een cadeaubon voor onze bruiloft gegeven.","Mijn ouders hebben ons een cadeaubon gegeven voor onze bruiloft."]},{"nl":"Bij zijn afscheid hebben de collega's hun baas een nieuwe fiets geschonken.","en":"When he left, the colleagues gave their boss a new bike as a present.","hint":["afscheid","collega","baas","nieuw","fiets","schenken"],"note":"Two nouns without aan: the person hun baas comes before the thing een nieuwe fiets. *een nieuwe fiets hun baas is wrong.","alt":["De collega's hebben hun baas bij zijn afscheid een nieuwe fiets geschonken.","De collega's hebben bij zijn afscheid hun baas een nieuwe fiets geschonken."]},{"nl":"Ik laat het je morgen weten.","en":"I will let you know tomorrow.","hint":["laten","morgen","weten"],"note":"Two pronouns: het (the thing) comes before je (the person), and both come before morgen. *Ik laat je het morgen weten is wrong.","alt":["Ik laat het jou morgen weten.","Morgen laat ik het je weten.","Morgen laat ik het jou weten."]},{"nl":"We gaan zaterdag met de trein naar de bruiloft.","en":"We are going to the wedding by train on Saturday.","hint":["gaan","zaterdag","trein","bruiloft"],"note":"Time, manner, place: zaterdag (when), met de trein (how), naar de bruiloft (where to). English puts the time last; Dutch puts it first in the middle.","alt":["Zaterdag gaan we met de trein naar de bruiloft."]},{"nl":"Ik heb gisteren een kleine attentie voor haar gekocht.","en":"I bought a little present for her yesterday.","hint":["gisteren","klein","attentie","voor","kopen"],"note":"Een kleine attentie is indefinite, new information, so it comes after the time word gisteren. *Ik heb een kleine attentie gisteren gekocht is wrong.","alt":["Gisteren heb ik een kleine attentie voor haar gekocht.","Ik heb gisteren voor haar een kleine attentie gekocht.","Gisteren heb ik voor haar een kleine attentie gekocht.","Ik heb gisteren een kleine attentie gekocht voor haar.","Gisteren heb ik een kleine attentie gekocht voor haar."]},{"nl":"Ik heb het cadeau nog niet ingepakt.","en":"I have not wrapped the present yet.","hint":["cadeau","nog niet","inpakken"],"note":"Het cadeau is a definite object, so niet comes after it. Niet before het cadeau would only be used for a contrast.","alt":["Het cadeau heb ik nog niet ingepakt."]},{"nl":"Wil je me een plezier doen en de taart ophalen?","en":"Will you do me a favour and pick up the cake?","hint":["willen","plezier","doen","taart","ophalen"],"note":"The pronoun me (the person) comes before the noun een plezier. Pronoun before noun, wherever two objects meet.","alt":["Wil jij me een plezier doen en de taart ophalen?","Wil je mij een plezier doen en de taart ophalen?","Wil jij mij een plezier doen en de taart ophalen?"]},{"nl":"Ik ben je heel dankbaar voor je hulp.","en":"I am very grateful to you for your help.","hint":["heel","dankbaar","voor","hulp"],"note":"Je is the person you are grateful to. As a pronoun without stress it stands right after the verb, before heel dankbaar.","alt":["Ik ben jou heel dankbaar voor je hulp.","Ik ben je heel dankbaar voor jouw hulp.","Ik ben jou heel dankbaar voor jouw hulp."]},{"nl":"Ze heeft ons gisteren haar verlanglijstje gestuurd.","en":"She sent us her wish list yesterday.","hint":["gisteren","verlanglijstje","sturen"],"note":"The pronoun ons comes first. Haar verlanglijstje is definite, so it may also stand before gisteren: Ze heeft ons haar verlanglijstje gisteren gestuurd.","alt":["Ze heeft ons haar verlanglijstje gisteren gestuurd.","Gisteren heeft ze ons haar verlanglijstje gestuurd.","Gisteren heeft zij ons haar verlanglijstje gestuurd."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb ___ gisteren gegeven.","options":["hem het","het hem","aan hem het"],"correct":1,"en":"I gave it to him yesterday.","note":"With two pronouns, het (the thing) comes first and the person second: het hem. With aan it would be het aan hem, never aan hem het."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb ___ voor de gastvrouw gekocht.","options":["een taart gisteren","gisteren een taart","een taart al"],"correct":1,"en":"I bought a cake for the hostess yesterday.","note":"Een taart is indefinite, new information, so it goes after the time word: gisteren een taart. The same holds for al: al een taart, not een taart al."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb ___ uitgepakt, want ik wacht tot mijn verjaardag.","options":["niet het cadeau","het cadeau niet","het niet cadeau"],"correct":1,"en":"I have not unwrapped the present, because I am waiting until my birthday.","note":"Het cadeau is definite, so niet comes after it. Niet het cadeau is only used for a contrast (not the present, but the card)."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn buurman is erg attent: hij geeft ___ (mijn dochter) altijd iets op haar verjaardag.","answers":["haar"],"en":"My neighbour is very thoughtful: he always gives her something on her birthday.","note":"Mijn dochter becomes the pronoun haar. As a pronoun it stands right after the verb, before altijd and before the thing iets."},{"type":"gap","nl":"We hebben het boeket ___ de bruid overhandigd.","answers":["aan"],"en":"We handed the bouquet to the bride.","note":"When the thing comes first, the person needs aan: het boeket aan de bruid. Without aan the person would have to come first: de bruid het boeket."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Waar is de cadeaubon? Ik heb ___ (de cadeaubon) al aan mijn broer gegeven.","answers":["hem","die"],"en":"Where is the gift voucher? I have already given it to my brother.","note":"Cadeaubon is a de-word, so the pronoun for it is hem, not het. It comes early, before al. In speech die is also used here."},{"type":"order","lead":"Gisteren","words":["heb","ik","haar","een boeket","gestuurd"],"answers":["Gisteren heb ik haar een boeket gestuurd."],"en":"Yesterday I sent her a bouquet.","note":"After Gisteren come the verb heb and the subject ik. Then the pronoun haar (the person) before the noun een boeket (the thing), and the participle last."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","de zaal","gisteren","met slingers","versierd"],"answers":["Ik heb de zaal gisteren met slingers versierd.","Ik heb gisteren de zaal met slingers versierd.","Ik heb de zaal gisteren versierd met slingers.","Ik heb gisteren de zaal versierd met slingers."],"en":"I decorated the hall with garlands yesterday.","note":"De zaal is a definite object, so it can stand before or after gisteren. Met slingers says how (manner), so it comes after the time, or as an afterthought after versierd. With een zaal only gisteren een zaal would be right."},{"type":"order","lead":"We","words":["geven","onze buren","een mooie lamp","voor hun bruiloft"],"answers":["We geven onze buren een mooie lamp voor hun bruiloft.","We geven onze buren voor hun bruiloft een mooie lamp."],"en":"We are giving our neighbours a nice lamp for their wedding.","note":"Two nouns without aan: the person onze buren comes before the thing een mooie lamp. Voor hun bruiloft may stand at the end or between the two."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["ben","gisteren","lopend","naar het feest","gegaan"],"answers":["Ik ben gisteren lopend naar het feest gegaan."],"en":"Yesterday I walked to the party.","note":"Time, manner, place: gisteren (when), lopend (how), naar het feest (where to). The direction stays close to the participle gegaan."},{"type":"order","lead":"Heb","words":["je","het","hem","al","gegeven"],"answers":["Heb je het hem al gegeven?"],"en":"Have you given it to him yet?","note":"In the question the subject je comes right after the verb. Then the two pronouns, het (the thing) before hem (the person), then al, and the participle last."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace both nouns with pronouns.","from":"Ik geef mijn vader het boek.","answers":["Ik geef het hem.","Ik geef het aan hem."],"en":"I am giving it to him.","note":"Two nouns: the person first. Two pronouns: the thing first, het hem. With aan: het aan hem. Never *Ik geef hem het."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make the object definite: het boeket.","from":"Ik heb gisteren een boeket gekocht.","answers":["Ik heb het boeket gisteren gekocht.","Ik heb gisteren het boeket gekocht."],"en":"I bought the bouquet yesterday.","note":"An indefinite object must follow gisteren. A definite one may stand before it (the neutral choice) or after it."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make it negative with niet.","from":"Ik heb de cadeaubon aan mijn broer gegeven.","answers":["Ik heb de cadeaubon niet aan mijn broer gegeven."],"en":"I did not give the gift voucher to my brother.","note":"Niet comes after the definite object de cadeaubon and before the phrase with a preposition, aan mijn broer."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb gisteren hem mijn gelukwensen gestuurd.","answers":["Ik heb hem gisteren mijn gelukwensen gestuurd.","Gisteren heb ik hem mijn gelukwensen gestuurd."],"en":"I sent him my congratulations yesterday.","note":"A pronoun without stress comes first in the middle, before the time word: hem gisteren. Gisteren hem only works with strong stress on hem."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Wij hebben veel felicitaties gisteren gekregen.","answers":["Wij hebben gisteren veel felicitaties gekregen.","Gisteren hebben wij veel felicitaties gekregen."],"en":"We received a lot of congratulations yesterday.","note":"Veel felicitaties is indefinite, new information, so it comes after the time word gisteren, close to the participle."}]},
  {"id":"b1-25","level":"B1","icon":"🌳","title":"Nature and the environment","grammar":"Word building: compounds, -heid, -ing, -baar, on-","summary":"Compounds are one word, and the last part gives the meaning and the article: het natuurgebied. -ing and -heid make de-words, het + infinitive a het-word. -baar: can be …-ed. on-: not. No ge- after ver-, be-, ont-.","explain":"Dutch makes new words from old ones and writes the result as **one word**: `natuur` + `gebied` is `natuurgebied`, never *natuur gebied. If you know the parts, you can work out what most long words mean, and often their article too.\n\n### Compounds (samenstellingen)\n\nThe **last part** is the main word. It says what the thing is, and it decides the article and the plural. The parts in front only say which kind.\n\n| the parts | the compound |\n| de tuin + het huis | het tuinhuis (a summer house) |\n| het huis + de deur | de huisdeur (the front door) |\n| de natuur + het gebied | het natuurgebied, natuurgebieden |\n\n> Het natuurgebied in onze omgeving wordt goed beschermd. — The nature reserve in our area is well protected.\n> De bosbrand was niet ver van onze camping. — The forest fire was not far from our campsite.\n\nSometimes a linking sound stands between the parts: `-s-` (`het stadscentrum`), `-e-` or `-en-` (`de zonnebloem`, `de pannenkoek`). There is no simple rule for when; learn them with the word (how to spell them is unit B2 · 19).\n\n### Nouns from verbs and adjectives\n\n| ending | example | article |\n| -ing (from a verb) | vervuilen → vervuiling | always de |\n| -heid (from an adjective) | schoon → schoonheid | always de |\n| -te (from an adjective) | warm → warmte | de |\n| ge- + stem of a verb | voelen → gevoel | mostly het |\n| the infinitive | recyclen → recyclen | always het |\n\n- `-ing` names an action or its result: `de vervuiling`, `de bescherming`, `de verspilling`.\n- `-heid` names a quality: `de duurzaamheid`. The plural is `-heden`: `de mogelijkheid`, `de mogelijkheden`.\n- `-te` is mostly for size and temperature: `de hoogte`, `de lengte`, `de warmte`.\n- `ge-` + stem: `het geluid`, `het gebouw`, `het gevoel`. A common exception: `de gedachte`.\n- `het` + infinitive names the activity: `het recyclen`, `het scheiden van afval`.\n- People: `-er` (`de onderzoeker`), `-aar` usually after a stem ending in an unstressed -el, -er or -en (`de wandelaar`, `de luisteraar`; but also `de leraar`), and `-ster` often for a woman (`de verkoopster`).\n\n> Het recyclen van glas is heel normaal. — Recycling glass is quite normal.\n\n### Adjectives\n\n| ending | meaning | example |\n| -baar | can be …-ed | drinkbaar, herbruikbaar, betaalbaar |\n| -loos | without | eindeloos, werkloos |\n| -lijk, -ig | having, like | schadelijk, zonnig |\n| -vol, -zaam | full of, with | waardevol, duurzaam |\n\n`-baar` goes on the stem of a verb: `drinken` → `drinkbaar` (can be drunk), `betalen` → `betaalbaar` (affordable). `herbruikbaar` is `her-` (again) + `bruikbaar` (usable): reusable.\n\n`on-` in front makes the opposite: `ongezond`, `onbekend`, `onmogelijk`, `onbetaalbaar`. It does not go on every adjective: *onmooi does not exist (say `lelijk` or `niet mooi`). Only use an on- word you have seen.\n\n> Dit water is niet drinkbaar. — This water is not drinkable.\n> Zonnepanelen zijn voor veel mensen onbetaalbaar. — Solar panels are unaffordable for many people.\n\n### Verbs with ver-, be-, ont-\n\n- `ver-` often turns an adjective into \"make it so\": `beter` → `verbeteren`, `vuil` → `vervuilen`, `warm` → `verwarmen`.\n- `be-` lets a verb take a direct object: `wonen in een huis`, but `een huis bewonen`; `beschermen` (to protect).\n- `ont-` often means \"away\" or a beginning: `ontbossen` (to clear the forest), `ontstaan` (to come into being).\n\nThese prefixes never split off, and the participle gets **no ge-**: `verbeterd`, `beschermd`, `ontstaan`.\n\n> De fabriek heeft de rivier vervuild. — The factory polluted the river.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- The article comes from the last part: *de natuurgebied → `het natuurgebied`; *het milieuvervuiling → `de milieuvervuiling`.\n- One word: *het natuur gebied → `het natuurgebied`.\n- -ing and -heid words are always de, `het` + infinitive always het.\n- No ge- after these prefixes: *gevervuild → `vervuild`, *gebeschermd → `beschermd`.\n- Not every adjective takes on-, and not every verb takes -baar: check that the word exists.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nWhere Ukrainian uses an adjective with a noun, Dutch often makes one compound: `лісова пожежа` is `de bosbrand`. Ukrainian `не-` works like `on-`: `нездоровий` is `ongezond`.","words":[{"nl":"milieu","art":"het","pl":"milieus","en":"environment (nature)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vervuiling","art":"de","pl":"","en":"pollution","pos":"n"},{"nl":"afval","art":"het/de","pl":"","en":"waste / rubbish","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitstoot","art":"de","pl":"","en":"emissions","pos":"n"},{"nl":"natuurgebied","art":"het","pl":"natuurgebieden","en":"nature reserve","pos":"n"},{"nl":"landschap","art":"het","pl":"landschappen","en":"landscape","pos":"n"},{"nl":"omgeving","art":"de","pl":"omgevingen","en":"surroundings / area","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vervuilen","past":"vervuilde","perf":"heeft vervuild","en":"to pollute","pos":"v"},{"nl":"beschermen","past":"beschermde","perf":"heeft beschermd","en":"to protect","pos":"v"},{"nl":"verspillen","past":"verspilde","perf":"heeft verspild","en":"to waste","pos":"v"},{"nl":"ontstaan","past":"ontstond","perf":"is ontstaan","en":"to come into being / to arise","pos":"v"},{"nl":"uitsterven","past":"stierf uit","perf":"is uitgestorven","en":"to die out","pos":"v"},{"nl":"duurzaam","en":"sustainable / durable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"herbruikbaar","en":"reusable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"afbreekbaar","en":"(bio)degradable","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"schadelijk","en":"harmful","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Het natuurgebied in onze omgeving wordt goed beschermd.","en":"The nature reserve in our area is well protected.","hint":["natuurgebied","omgeving","worden","goed","beschermen"],"note":"Natuurgebied ends in het gebied, so it is a het-word. Beschermen has the prefix be-, so its participle has no ge-: beschermd.","alt":["In onze omgeving wordt het natuurgebied goed beschermd."]},{"nl":"De vervuiling van de rivier is de laatste jaren sterk afgenomen.","en":"The pollution of the river has decreased sharply in recent years.","hint":["vervuiling","rivier","laatst","jaar","sterk","afnemen"],"note":"Vervuiling is made from the verb vervuilen with -ing, and every -ing noun is a de-word.","alt":["De laatste jaren is de vervuiling van de rivier sterk afgenomen."]},{"nl":"Deze flessen zijn herbruikbaar, dus gooi ze niet weg.","en":"These bottles are reusable, so do not throw them away.","hint":["fles","herbruikbaar","dus","weggooien"],"note":"Herbruikbaar is her- (again) + bruik (use) + -baar (can be …-ed): it can be used again.","alt":["Deze flessen zijn herbruikbaar, dus gooi die niet weg."]},{"nl":"Plastic is slecht afbreekbaar, dus het blijft lang in de natuur.","en":"Plastic does not break down easily, so it stays in nature for a long time.","hint":["plastic","slecht","afbreekbaar","blijven","natuur"],"note":"Afbreekbaar is the stem of afbreken (to break down) + -baar: something that can be broken down. Slecht afbreekbaar: hard to break down.","alt":["Plastic is slecht afbreekbaar, dus blijft het lang in de natuur."]},{"nl":"Veel diersoorten dreigen uit te sterven.","en":"Many animal species are in danger of dying out.","hint":["veel","diersoort","dreigen","uitsterven"],"note":"Diersoort is het dier + de soort. The last part decides: de diersoort, plural diersoorten."},{"nl":"Het scheiden van afval is in Nederland heel gewoon.","en":"Separating rubbish is very normal in the Netherlands.","hint":["scheiden","afval","Nederland","gewoon"],"note":"An infinitive used as a noun names the activity and is always a het-word: het scheiden. What you separate follows with van.","alt":["In Nederland is het scheiden van afval heel gewoon.","Het scheiden van afval is heel gewoon in Nederland."]},{"nl":"Voedselverspilling is slecht voor het milieu.","en":"Food waste is bad for the environment.","hint":["voedselverspilling","slecht","milieu"],"note":"Verspillen + -ing gives de verspilling; with voedsel in front it is still a de-word, because the last part decides."},{"nl":"De uitstoot van auto's is schadelijk voor de gezondheid.","en":"The emissions from cars are harmful to health.","hint":["uitstoot","auto","schadelijk","gezondheid"],"note":"Two built words: schade + -lijk gives schadelijk (harmful), and gezond + -heid gives de gezondheid. Uitstoot is singular in Dutch (English says emissions), so the verb is singular: is."},{"nl":"Dit landschap is in de laatste ijstijd ontstaan.","en":"This landscape came into being in the last ice age.","hint":["landschap","laatst","ijstijd","ontstaan"],"note":"Ontstaan has the prefix ont-, so the participle has no ge- and looks like the infinitive: is ontstaan. De ijstijd is het ijs + de tijd.","alt":["Dit landschap is ontstaan in de laatste ijstijd.","In de laatste ijstijd is dit landschap ontstaan."]},{"nl":"Zonnepanelen zijn duurzaam, maar voor veel mensen onbetaalbaar.","en":"Solar panels are sustainable, but unaffordable for many people.","hint":["zonnepaneel","duurzaam","maar","veel","mens","onbetaalbaar"],"note":"Onbetaalbaar is on- (not) + betaal (pay) + -baar: it cannot be paid. Het zonnepaneel has the linking -e- between zon and paneel.","alt":["Zonnepanelen zijn duurzaam, maar onbetaalbaar voor veel mensen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"___ natuurgebied is alleen open voor wandelaars.","options":["Deze","Dit","Die"],"correct":1,"en":"This nature reserve is only open to walkers.","note":"The last part, het gebied, decides the article: het natuurgebied, so dit. Deze and die go with de-words."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Kijk, ___ zonnebloem is al twee meter hoog!","options":["dit","het","deze"],"correct":2,"en":"Look, this sunflower is already two metres tall!","note":"Zonnebloem is zon + linking -e- + de bloem. The last part is a de-word, so deze zonnebloem."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De lucht in de stad is vies. De ___ is een groot probleem.","options":["vervuiling","vervuilheid","vervuilen"],"correct":0,"en":"The air in the city is dirty. The pollution is a big problem.","note":"A noun from a verb takes -ing: vervuilen → de vervuiling. -heid goes on adjectives, not on verbs."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De gemeente heeft het park flink ___.","options":["geverbeterd","verbeterd","verbetert"],"correct":1,"en":"The municipality has greatly improved the park.","note":"Verbeteren starts with ver-, so the participle gets no ge-: verbeterd. The stem ends in r, not in a 't kofschip' letter, so -d."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Door de ___ (vervuilen) van de zee sterven veel vissen.","answers":["vervuiling"],"en":"Many fish die because of the pollution of the sea.","note":"After de a noun is needed: the stem vervuil + -ing. Words ending in -ing are always de-words."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De ___ (schoon) van dit landschap is bijzonder.","answers":["schoonheid"],"en":"The beauty of this landscape is special.","note":"A noun for a quality is the adjective + -heid: schoon → de schoonheid."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Er zijn veel ___ (mogelijk) om energie te besparen.","answers":["mogelijkheden"],"en":"There are many ways to save energy.","note":"Mogelijk + -heid gives de mogelijkheid. After veel a plural is needed, and -heid becomes -heden."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Dit water is niet ___ (drinken): je wordt er ziek van.","answers":["drinkbaar"],"en":"This water is not drinkable: it will make you ill.","note":"The stem drink + -baar means 'can be drunk'. In this meaning -baar goes on the stem of a verb."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De ___ (hoog) van deze boom is dertig meter.","answers":["hoogte"],"en":"The height of this tree is thirty metres.","note":"For a size Dutch adds -te to the adjective: hoog → de hoogte, like lang → de lengte."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with one adjective ending in -baar.","from":"Je kunt deze tas opnieuw gebruiken.","answers":["Deze tas is herbruikbaar."],"en":"This bag is reusable.","note":"Her- means 'again' and bruikbaar means 'usable': herbruikbaar is 'can be used again'."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Say the same with one adjective ending in -loos; keep lijkt.","from":"Het strand lijkt geen einde te hebben.","answers":["Het strand lijkt eindeloos.","Het strand lijkt eindeloos te zijn."],"en":"The beach seems endless.","note":"-loos means 'without': eind + -loos is eindeloos, without an end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace niet bekend with one word starting with on-.","from":"Deze vogel is in Nederland niet bekend.","answers":["Deze vogel is in Nederland onbekend."],"en":"This bird is unknown in the Netherlands.","note":"On- in front of an adjective makes the opposite: bekend → onbekend, written as one word."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Make one sentence starting with Het and an infinitive: Het … van afval is belangrijk.","from":"We scheiden ons afval. Dat is belangrijk.","answers":["Het scheiden van afval is belangrijk.","Het scheiden van ons afval is belangrijk."],"en":"Separating rubbish is important.","note":"The infinitive scheiden becomes a noun: het scheiden, always with het. The object afval follows with van."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Het milieuvervuiling is een groot probleem.","answers":["De milieuvervuiling is een groot probleem.","Milieuvervuiling is een groot probleem."],"en":"Environmental pollution is a big problem.","note":"Het milieu is only the first part. The last part, de vervuiling, decides the article: de milieuvervuiling."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"De fabriek heeft de rivier gevervuild.","answers":["De fabriek heeft de rivier vervuild."],"en":"The factory polluted the river.","note":"Vervuilen starts with ver-, and verbs with ver-, be- or ont- never get ge- in the participle: vervuild."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"We wandelen graag in het natuur gebied.","answers":["We wandelen graag in het natuurgebied."],"en":"We like walking in the nature reserve.","note":"A compound is written as one word in Dutch: natuurgebied, not natuur gebied."}]},
  {"id":"b1-26","level":"B1","icon":"🧠","title":"Learning something new","grammar":"Two infinitives in the perfect: heb moeten werken, is gaan zwemmen","summary":"A verb with an infinitive after it takes its own infinitive in the perfect, not its participle: heb moeten werken, is gaan zwemmen, heb zitten studeren. Zijn with gaan, komen, blijven; hebben with the rest.","explain":"Some verbs take another verb in the infinitive: the modal verbs (`moeten`, `kunnen`, `mogen`, `willen`), and also `gaan`, `komen`, `blijven`, `laten`, `zien`, `horen`, `leren`, `helpen`, `hoeven` and `zitten`, `staan`, `liggen`, `lopen`. When you put such a verb in the perfect, it does not use its participle but its **infinitive**. So the sentence ends in two infinitives. Dutch grammars call this *de vervangende infinitief* (the infinitive that replaces the participle).\n\n### The form\n\n| present | perfect |\n| Ik moet werken. | Ik heb moeten werken. |\n| Hij gaat zwemmen. | Hij is gaan zwemmen. |\n| We blijven oefenen. | We zijn blijven oefenen. |\n| Ik zie haar dansen. | Ik heb haar zien dansen. |\n\nNever *Ik heb gemoeten werken or *Hij is gegaan zwemmen.\n\n> Ik heb gisteren twee uur moeten oefenen. — I had to practise for two hours yesterday.\n> Mijn vader heeft me leren fietsen. — My father taught me to ride a bike.\n> De docent heeft ons de tekst laten lezen. — The teacher had us read the text.\n\nNote `leren`: it means both \"to learn\" and \"to teach\". `Ik heb leren zwemmen` is \"I learned to swim\", `Hij heeft me leren zwemmen` \"he taught me to swim\". With `leren` the participle is also correct, after the other verb: `Hij heeft me zwemmen geleerd`.\n\n### Which auxiliary?\n\nThe **first verb** decides: the one that was conjugated in the present.\n\n- `zijn` with `gaan`, `komen` and `blijven`: `Ik ben gaan zwemmen`, `Ze is komen kijken`, `We zijn blijven zitten`.\n- `hebben` with all the others: the modal verbs, `laten`, `zien`, `horen`, `leren`, `helpen`, `hoeven`, `zitten`, `staan`, `liggen`, `lopen`.\n\nThe verb at the end does not matter. `Werken` alone takes hebben, but `Ik ben gaan werken`. `Gaan` alone takes zijn, but `Ik heb naar huis moeten gaan`, because `moeten` decides.\n\n> Mijn moeder is naar mijn eerste les komen kijken. — My mother came to watch my first lesson.\n\n### No te\n\n`Zitten`, `staan`, `liggen`, `lopen` and `hoeven` take `te` in the present: `Ze zit te studeren`, `Je hoeft niet te komen`. In the perfect `te` normally disappears:\n\n> Ze heeft de hele avond zitten studeren. — She sat studying all evening.\n> Ik heb niet lang hoeven wachten. — I did not have to wait long.\n\nYou will also hear and read the form with `te` (`heeft zitten te studeren`, `heb niet hoeven te wachten`); it is accepted, but the form without `te` is the usual one.\n\n### Word order\n\nIn a main clause the auxiliary stands second and both infinitives go to the end: first the verb that would have been the participle, then the main verb: `heb … moeten werken`, never *heb … werken moeten.\n\nIn a sub-clause all the verbs go to the end, and the auxiliary comes **first**, before the two infinitives:\n\n> Ik ben blij dat ik heb leren zwemmen. — I am glad that I have learned to swim.\n> Het spijt me dat ik niet heb kunnen komen. — I am sorry that I could not come.\n\nThe order *dat ik leren zwemmen heb, with the auxiliary last, is regional (mainly in the west of Belgium), not standard Dutch. (More about three verbs at the end in unit B2 · 3.)\n\n### On its own: the normal participle\n\nWithout a second verb these verbs simply take their participle:\n\n> Ik heb het niet gekund. — I was not able to do it.\n> Hij is gisteren naar de les gegaan. — He went to the lesson yesterday.\n\nSo `Ik heb het gewild`, but `Ik heb het willen leren`.\n\n### Watch out\n\n- *Ik heb gemoeten werken → `Ik heb moeten werken`.\n- *Hij heeft gaan zwemmen → `Hij is gaan zwemmen` (gaan decides).\n- `Ik heb naar huis moeten gaan`, with hebben: moeten decides, not gaan. `Ik ben naar huis moeten gaan` is standard in Belgium; in the Netherlands it is less common, so use hebben.\n- In a sub-clause the auxiliary comes first: `dat ik heb moeten werken`, not *dat ik moeten werken heb (regional).\n- *Ik heb het niet kunnen → `Ik heb het niet gekund`: with no second verb, the participle.\n- `proberen` normally keeps its participle and `te`: `Ik heb geprobeerd te bellen`.\n\n### Compared with Ukrainian\n\nUkrainian says this with one past form and an infinitive: `я мусив працювати`. Dutch can do the same with the simple past (`Ik moest werken`), and both are common. But as soon as you choose the perfect, you need the two infinitives.","words":[{"nl":"vaardigheid","art":"de","pl":"vaardigheden","en":"skill","pos":"n"},{"nl":"gevorderde","art":"de","pl":"gevorderden","en":"advanced learner","pos":"n"},{"nl":"cursist","art":"de","pl":"cursisten","en":"course participant / student","pos":"n"},{"nl":"proefles","art":"de","pl":"proeflessen","en":"trial lesson","pos":"n"},{"nl":"bijles","art":"de","pl":"bijlessen","en":"extra lessons / tutoring","pos":"n"},{"nl":"uitspraak","art":"de","pl":"uitspraken","en":"pronunciation","pos":"n"},{"nl":"vooruitgang","art":"de","pl":"","en":"progress","pos":"n"},{"nl":"certificaat","art":"het","pl":"certificaten","en":"certificate","pos":"n"},{"nl":"rijexamen","art":"het","pl":"rijexamens","en":"driving test","pos":"n"},{"nl":"herkansing","art":"de","pl":"herkansingen","en":"resit (of an exam)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"beheersen","past":"beheerste","perf":"heeft beheerst","en":"to master (a skill)","pos":"v"},{"nl":"herhalen","past":"herhaalde","perf":"heeft herhaald","en":"to repeat / to go over again","pos":"v"},{"nl":"lesstof","art":"de","pl":"","en":"course material (what is taught)","pos":"n"},{"nl":"lesgeld","art":"het","pl":"","en":"course fee / tuition fee","pos":"n"},{"nl":"geduldig","en":"patient","pos":"adj"},{"nl":"vloeiend","en":"fluent / fluently","pos":"adj"}],"sentences":[{"nl":"Ik heb gisteren twee uur moeten oefenen voor mijn rijexamen.","en":"Yesterday I had to practise for two hours for my driving test. (perfect)","hint":["gisteren","uur","moeten","oefenen","rijexamen"],"note":"Moeten has an infinitive after it, so in the perfect it does not become gemoeten but stays moeten: heb … moeten oefenen.","alt":["Gisteren heb ik twee uur moeten oefenen voor mijn rijexamen.","Ik heb gisteren twee uur voor mijn rijexamen moeten oefenen.","Gisteren heb ik twee uur voor mijn rijexamen moeten oefenen.","Ik heb gisteren voor mijn rijexamen twee uur moeten oefenen.","Gisteren heb ik voor mijn rijexamen twee uur moeten oefenen."]},{"nl":"Ze is vorig jaar een cursus voor gevorderden gaan volgen.","en":"Last year she started a course for advanced learners. (perfect)","hint":["vorig jaar","cursus","gevorderde","gaan","volgen"],"note":"Gaan + infinitive in the perfect: is … gaan volgen. The auxiliary is zijn, because gaan decides, not volgen.","alt":["Vorig jaar is ze een cursus voor gevorderden gaan volgen.","Vorig jaar is zij een cursus voor gevorderden gaan volgen."]},{"nl":"We zijn na de les nog een uur blijven oefenen.","en":"After the lesson we stayed on to practise for another hour. (perfect)","hint":["na","les","nog","uur","blijven","oefenen"],"note":"Blijven takes zijn, and with an infinitive after it, it stays blijven instead of gebleven: zijn … blijven oefenen.","alt":["Na de les zijn we nog een uur blijven oefenen."]},{"nl":"Ik heb hem nog nooit zo vloeiend Nederlands horen spreken.","en":"I have never heard him speak Dutch so fluently.","hint":["nog nooit","vloeiend","Nederlands","horen","spreken"],"note":"Horen + infinitive: in the perfect horen replaces gehoord. The person you hear, hem, comes early in the middle.","alt":["Ik heb hem nooit zo vloeiend Nederlands horen spreken."]},{"nl":"Mijn vader heeft me leren fietsen.","en":"My father taught me to ride a bike. (perfect)","hint":["vader","leren","fietsen"],"note":"Leren here means 'to teach'. With an infinitive after it, the perfect is heeft … leren fietsen, with hebben.","alt":["Mijn vader heeft mij leren fietsen.","Mijn vader heeft me fietsen geleerd.","Mijn vader heeft mij fietsen geleerd."]},{"nl":"Ze heeft de hele avond woordjes zitten herhalen.","en":"She sat going over vocabulary all evening. (perfect)","hint":["hele","avond","woordje","zitten","herhalen"],"note":"In the present it is ze zit … te herhalen. In the perfect zitten stays an infinitive, takes hebben, and te normally disappears.","alt":["Ze heeft de hele avond woordjes zitten te herhalen."]},{"nl":"Ik heb dit jaar geen lesgeld hoeven betalen.","en":"I did not have to pay any course fee this year. (perfect)","hint":["dit jaar","lesgeld","hoeven","betalen"],"note":"Hoeven + infinitive: in the perfect heb … hoeven betalen, usually without te.","alt":["Ik heb dit jaar geen lesgeld hoeven te betalen.","Dit jaar heb ik geen lesgeld hoeven betalen.","Dit jaar heb ik geen lesgeld hoeven te betalen."]},{"nl":"Omdat ik ziek was, heb ik de les niet kunnen volgen.","en":"Because I was ill, I was not able to attend the lesson. (perfect)","hint":["omdat","ziek","les","kunnen","volgen"],"note":"Kunnen with an infinitive: heb … kunnen volgen, not gekund. The sub-clause comes first, so heb comes right after the comma, before ik.","alt":["Ik heb de les niet kunnen volgen, omdat ik ziek was."]},{"nl":"Ik vind het jammer dat ik de proefles heb moeten missen.","en":"I am sorry that I had to miss the trial lesson. (perfect)","hint":["vinden","jammer","dat","proefles","moeten","missen"],"note":"In a sub-clause all verbs stand at the end, with the auxiliary first: heb moeten missen. Moeten missen heb is regional, not standard Dutch.","alt":["Het spijt me dat ik de proefles heb moeten missen."]},{"nl":"De docent is erg geduldig: hij heeft ons de uitspraak tien keer laten oefenen.","en":"The teacher is very patient: he had us practise the pronunciation ten times. (perfect)","hint":["docent","geduldig","uitspraak","keer","laten","oefenen"],"note":"Laten + infinitive: in the perfect laten replaces gelaten, with hebben: heeft … laten oefenen.","alt":["De docent is erg geduldig: hij heeft ons tien keer de uitspraak laten oefenen."]}],"exercises":[{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik heb die nieuwe vaardigheid snel ___ beheersen.","options":["geleerd","leren","geleerde"],"correct":1,"en":"I learned to master that new skill quickly.","note":"Leren has another infinitive after it, beheersen, so in the perfect it stays an infinitive: heb … leren beheersen."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Na de bijles ___ mijn zoon nog gaan voetballen.","options":["heeft","is","zijn"],"correct":1,"en":"After the tutoring my son went on to play football.","note":"The first verb, gaan, decides the auxiliary, and gaan takes zijn. Voetballen alone would take hebben, but that does not count here."},{"type":"choose","nl":"De eerste keer heb ik het niet ___, maar bij de herkansing wel.","options":["kunnen","gekund","kan"],"correct":1,"en":"The first time I was not able to do it, but at the resit I was.","note":"There is no second verb here, so kunnen takes its normal participle: gekund. The infinitive only replaces it when another infinitive follows."},{"type":"choose","nl":"Ik ___ hem naar huis laten gaan, want hij voelde zich niet goed.","options":["ben","heb","is"],"correct":1,"en":"I let him go home, because he was not feeling well.","note":"Laten is the first verb, and it takes hebben. Gaan at the end does not decide: heb … laten gaan."},{"type":"gap","nl":"De cursisten hebben in korte tijd veel vooruitgang ___ (kunnen) boeken.","answers":["kunnen"],"en":"The course participants have been able to make a lot of progress in a short time.","note":"An infinitive follows, boeken, so kunnen does not become gekund: hebben … kunnen boeken."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Mijn moeder ___ (zijn / hebben) bij mijn eerste les komen kijken.","answers":["is"],"en":"My mother came to watch at my first lesson.","note":"Komen is the first verb and takes zijn, so is … komen kijken. Komen stays an infinitive because kijken follows."},{"type":"gap","nl":"Gelukkig heb ik niet lang ___ (hoeven) wachten op mijn certificaat.","answers":["hoeven","hoeven te"],"en":"Luckily I did not have to wait long for my certificate.","note":"Hoeven with an infinitive stays hoeven in the perfect. Te usually disappears, but hoeven te wachten is also accepted."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","gisteren","de lesstof","moeten","herhalen"],"answers":["Ik heb gisteren de lesstof moeten herhalen.","Ik heb de lesstof gisteren moeten herhalen."],"en":"Yesterday I had to go over the course material again.","note":"The two infinitives close the sentence: first moeten, then herhalen. De lesstof is definite, so it may stand before or after gisteren."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik ben blij dat","words":["ik","eindelijk","heb","leren","zwemmen"],"answers":["Ik ben blij dat ik eindelijk heb leren zwemmen."],"en":"I am glad that I have finally learned to swim.","note":"In a sub-clause the auxiliary heb comes before the two infinitives: heb leren zwemmen. Leren zwemmen heb is regional, not standard Dutch."},{"type":"order","lead":"Ik","words":["heb","me","niet meer","kunnen","inschrijven"],"answers":["Ik heb me niet meer kunnen inschrijven."],"en":"I was no longer able to sign up.","note":"The pronoun me comes early, then niet meer, and the two infinitives go last: kunnen inschrijven. The auxiliary is hebben, because kunnen decides."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Na de les blijven we nog even praten.","answers":["Na de les zijn we nog even blijven praten."],"en":"After the lesson we stayed on for a chat.","note":"Blijven takes zijn and stays an infinitive because praten follows: zijn … blijven praten, not *gebleven praten."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Ze zit de hele middag te studeren.","answers":["Ze heeft de hele middag zitten studeren.","Ze heeft de hele middag zitten te studeren."],"en":"She sat studying all afternoon.","note":"Zitten takes hebben and stays an infinitive. Te normally disappears in the perfect: heeft … zitten studeren."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Put it in the perfect.","from":"Ik zie mijn dochter elke dag piano oefenen.","answers":["Ik heb mijn dochter elke dag piano zien oefenen."],"en":"I have seen my daughter practise the piano every day.","note":"Zien with an infinitive after it: zien replaces gezien, with hebben. Zien comes before oefenen at the end."},{"type":"rewrite","task":"Replace want with omdat.","from":"Ze is voor haar examen gezakt, want ze heeft niet genoeg kunnen oefenen.","answers":["Ze is voor haar examen gezakt, omdat ze niet genoeg heeft kunnen oefenen.","Ze is voor haar examen gezakt, omdat zij niet genoeg heeft kunnen oefenen."],"en":"She failed her exam because she was not able to practise enough.","note":"After omdat all verbs go to the end, and the auxiliary heeft comes first: heeft kunnen oefenen."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Ik heb gisteren niet gekund komen.","answers":["Ik heb gisteren niet kunnen komen."],"en":"I was not able to come yesterday.","note":"Komen follows, so kunnen does not take its participle: heb … kunnen komen. Gekund is only used when there is no second verb."},{"type":"fix","wrong":"Mijn broer heeft na zijn werk nog gaan sporten.","answers":["Mijn broer is na zijn werk nog gaan sporten."],"en":"After work my brother went on to do some sport.","note":"Gaan is the first verb and always takes zijn, also with an infinitive after it: is … gaan sporten."}]}
];
